      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <head>Me DOUG- A. 14 L'S PATE= SE LF-R ISING FLOUR BEST PASTRY</head>
        <p>Me DOUG- A. 14 L'S PATE= SE LF-R ISING FLOUR BEST PASTRY</p>
        <p>FASHIONS OF TO-DAY.</p>
        <p>Long basques are very fashionable on all kinds of coats this season and are occasionally cut so as to appear part of the skirt and not of the bodice, the junction being concealed under a shaped band of embroidery or passe- menterie. I recently admired, and shall certainly council all my friends to adopt, an exceedingly clever device by which an outdoor gown can be converted into an indoor toilette.</p>
        <p>When worn out of doors, the skirt is cut all round to clear the ground, in the sensible fashion which is at present considered cor- rect. The bodice or coat had a basque of the same material attached to a shaped band which was detachable and was enly worn when out of doors.</p>
        <p>A shoulder tippet or pelerine, also of the same material as the costume, with long ends</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>FASHIONABLE COS=</head>
        <p>A FASHIONABLE COS=</p>
        <p>in front edged with cord and passementerie matching the trimming on the rest of the dress, formed the finishing adjunct of the toilette for street wear to be removed when indoors.</p>
        <p>For the house, the necessary length of the skirt was obtained by the addition of a silk underskirt edged with a shaped flounce of the dress material worn underneath the skirt proper. A broad satin ribbon band fitted the waist neatly and formed a butterfly bow at the back, while two long ends of the ribbons fell down the skirt at the back. A dainty front with a neckband and jabot of lace com- pleted a smart indoor toilette. The fashionable costume depicted is expressed in a warm brown cloth, the bodice having turned-back fronts faced in with rich brocade with a shaded yellow pattern on a cream ground. A broad satin belt finishes the waist above the fashionable basque, while the skirt is made in the most modish style. This treble skirt has the top skirt shaped well to the hips all round without any fulness behind at the top. The effect, however, is not at all tight, as all the tiers are cut like volants and, therefore, fall in graceful lines and folds. This style of skirt is very decidedly popular this winter and is undoubtedly eminently becoming to tall figures, nor is it heavy to wear, for each flounce is mounted upon a light silk foundation, the edges only just overlapping the lower flounce. The triple skirt stands out well from the feet when standing or walking, a particularly desirable qualification. Indeed, many of the new model skirts—more particularly those for house wear—convey the impression that they will be much trimmed this season at the back, braidings, cords, tassels and sashes being pressed into service.</p>
        <p>Another fashionable skirt has fan-shaped kiltings from the knees downwards. Others again are set into a series of minute fiat pleats at the top, which widen towards the bottom of the skirt, while others again are box-pleated and stitched down at the top and then left to fall fully. The pretty lace slip sketched is suitable for evening wear, and is made of a fine make of</p>
        <p>cream coloured lace such as Valenciennes. The yoke is left transparent and a full frill of lace _ . .</p>
        <p>-edging is drawn to the lower edge. The silk lining below is nearly tight fitting and is caught into a waistband of rose-coloured silk. Little strappings of the same coloured silk, wlalch finish in tiny rosettes, cross the arm at the join of the full frill, which comes almost to the wrists, and a large chonx of chiffon of the same shade of pink is arranged in front of the bodice.</p>
        <p>The effect is exceedingly charming, and is not difficult to achieve by the home worker. The lace slip can be cut from a blouse pattern as it is only the yoke which requires fitting, the lace frill hanging loose. An old evening blouse can do duty for the silk or satin under- bodice, while even an old afternoon blouse 2ould be cut to answer the purpose. The lace slip could also form a dainty addi- tion to a tea-gown, worn over the upper part, and, indeed, in every way, it will be found a useful dressy adjunct of the winter outfit of many a girl. Now is the time when the charms of a cos% dressing jacket such as forms the subject or my illustration can be appreciated to the utmost The material employed is pale blue flannel lined with pale pink flannelette, while</p>
        <p>;OSY DIIESSIN6 JACKET.</p>
        <p>the deep collar and turned back cuffs are of quilted pink satin. A full bow and long ends of satin ribbon fasten the fronts together. The shape is very simple as the jacket is a sac and the sleeve re of the ,wide kimono shape. Any girl could, in her spare time, soon manufacture this charming little garment, which would be found invaluable.</p>
        <p>Its decorative effect could be further en- hanced by the embroidery of sprays, or wreath of flowers, in silks on the collar and cuff:. The (Jost of the jacket would not be found great. The quilted silk can be bought already quilted, or, if preferred, can be done at home. in which case the quality of the silk is sure to be better.</p>
        <p>There are many girls, now-a-days, who fall into the habit of buying their things all ready- made, but although this is possible when one is the possessor of a liberal dress allowance, it is quite out of the question for those who have seriously to consider ways and means, and to whom the purchase of even a trifling article of dress is a matter for careful consideration.</p>
