      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>The King, when Prince of Wales was an indefatigable theatre goer, and his Majesty clearly intends to keep up the custom of Queen Victoria in baying private dramatic entertainments at the Royal palaces. The Waterloo chamber, which is used for this purpose at Windsor Castle, was converted into a theatre some ten or twelve ,1 years ago, and the first piece to be played on the stage was " Tele gondoliers." The room is named after a number of portraits it contains of generals and states- men associated with the battle of Waterloo ard is admirably adopted for theatricals. It is a magnificent ariartmsnt being 160 feet long, and its floor is covered with a very valuable Indian carpet. Dramatic entertainments at Sandrineham are usually of an informal character,but those at Windsor Castle, aro conducte.l in full State. The officers of the household are in full uniform, and everything is arranged with due regard to official ceremony. The Court and invited guests take their places beforehand. The announce- ment of their Majesties precedes the entry of the Royal party, all the company remaining standing during the playing of the National Anthem, and until the King and Queen are seated in their chairs. During the perfor- mance there most be no undue laughter or applauee,snd in fact until tbe King laughs or cheers there must be no demonstration of appreciation from the audience. Lord Lamle., who for the second time has bad the honour of entertaining the German Emperor, is the patron of forty livings, as well as the owner of 68,000 acres, and some £70,000 a year. The only other laymen who has nearly so many livings in his gift is the Duke of Devonshire, wbo has tbirty-nine presentations, and gives them away himself. Lord Lonedate saves himself this trouble by handing over his extensive patronage to the Bishop, the livings being mostly in Cumberland, Westmorland, and Yorkshire. But though the Earl and the Dubs head the list, there are several other peers who are patrons of mere than twenty livings. The Duke of Bedford has twenty-five in his gift, the Duke of Beau- fort, and the Duke of Northumberland have each twenty-tour, and the Duke of Rutland has twenty-one. Some of the livings in the gift of these peers are very valuable osscs,bnt this Is not the ease with these belong- ing te the Earl of Lonsdale, es not one of his liviegs is thought to be worth more than £3OO a year. The death of Min Rose McDowell recalls one of the incidents in the life of Daniel O'Connell. The Irish Liberator died in 1847, and rot very long before,he bad made an cffer of marriage to this lady, who refused him apparently because be was many years older than she was. O'Connell is said to have taken his rejection so much to heart that it accelerated his death. Miss McDowell, after surviving her friend for 55 years, has died at the age of 82. Tinto was a similar incident in tho life of the great Duke of Wellington, but his Grass was not of the same romantic temperament as O'Connell, and he does not appear to have worried himself very much about the matter. . .</p>
        <p>lbere is scriptural authority for saying that no man by taking thought can add to his stature, but it is nevertheless true that the women site-day are taller as a rule than those of forger generation. A reason for this may be that the people have much mere and better food than in former times, and thie view is adopted by the Japanese, who hope by directing attention to questions of diet, to gradually increase the stature of their people, But there is another aspect to this question furnished in the belief that the tall woman generally lacks the bright and cheerful dispesititn of her smaller sister. We usually associate the long and lean with melancholy, and regard the jolly person as almost necessarily fat, theugb the late M. Corney Grain was one of the very few professional humorists answering to this description. One can under- stand that temperament may make us thin or sleek as the case may be, but if the same rule applies to beet, the tall woman ought to be the merriest. In sp:te, however, of these contradictions most wemen prefer to be tall and slender, and regard it as a terrible mis- fortune to be short and stout.</p>
        <p>The cry of "murderous millinery "is ones more raised in a fresh demand for the extension of the close season for our seabirds, such as the tern, the kittiwake, aid others. The Government of India has decided to stop the export of bird-skins and plumage,and,fte one country after another is more vigorously takiNg up the protee- thin of its birds, so it narrows the sources of supply, and at the same time makes the pursuit of the birds all the more kienwherever they can be taken advantage of. It is therefore said that more stregent laws, of a more vigttrons application of existing statutes are heeded, if many of our most beautiful wild birds are to be preserved. Osteasionally however, fashion may tend towards beneficent purposes, and the very large demand for moleskin fur this winter ought to Jo the farmer a great deal of good if he is right in regarding this ingenious little animal as his enemy. But the mole, like many of the birds, is probably not BO black as he is painted, and it is not unlikely that he does the farmer more good by his destruction of mere worms, and insects, than he does harm by his burrowings. This however matters little to the professional molecateber, who has recently found his occupation a most profitable one, and real moleskin promises soon to rank with the valuable furs. With the washable white kid glove many of us are familiar, but the washable suede variety that is now being offered cis is a novelty. The:white suede glove:is very delightful wear,and most beeoming, but alas it so quickly soils, that so far it bas been an impossibility to all save those possessed of an ample dress allowance. But we have changed all that, and the washable suede glove seems likely to enjoy a wide vogue. Those women who object to a muff—and there are many—are patronis- ing fur-lined, fur-backed gloves for all save dressy occa. shone. The majority are in tbe favourite shade of grey. Even sealskin is being dyed this colour, and the idea has little to recommend it, save novelty. For the soft silks, satins, and brocades of British manufacture, there is great demand at the present time se say the heads of several large drapery houses. And when we consider the beautiful designs and exquisite ecloringe shown in these fabrics, and again, their won- derful wearing qualities we can only marvel, putting aside the question of patriotism altogether, that we have so long been blind to our own interests. Irish poplins also, in the most beautiful colorings, and very fine and soft in texture,are in great favor,and deservedly a', as those of us who have gowns end wraps of this particu- lar fabric know well. A great success too are the blouses in fine wool poplin wbieb can now be had in almost any color and trimmed with Irieh lace. Sense are smart enough to be donned with a dressy iseetume, others plain enough fcr morning wear in the house. and are very moderate in price. JUDGE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>HOW I LOST MY MEMORY.</head>
        <p>HOW I LOST MY MEMORY.</p>
        <p>Schoolmaster's Weird Case.</p>
        <p>The "Lancet " hos eeldom contained a medical mar- vel more remarkable than the case of a sahoelmaster ?sunny reported in a country paper. The brain of Mr B Hoskin, a schoolmaster, of 176 Brighton-itrest, Beacom's', underwent a scrt of paralysis. arising from grief at the death of his loved mother in 1899, the elect of which was to deprive him of his memory. "It often ailed me." he said, 'and I found myself saying things</p>
