      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>SOME EXHIBITS AT THE STANLEY SHOW.</head>
        <p>SOME EXHIBITS AT THE STANLEY SHOW.</p>
        <p>attempts to reduce vibration are obvious, notably in the Celeripede machine. With the motor-cycle the " trailer ' has come along in force, and many specimens are in evidence to hitch on in front or behind. Amongst the novelties is a neat attach- Araent which can be fixed to the handle-bars, to act •as a sun-screen or to be used as a sail, called the Anderson canopy. An interesting antiquarian curiosity is shown in the shape of a very early bicycle, built by Shergold, who was one of the many claimants to the distinction of having in- vented the safety bicycle. On the whole, the Stanley Show this year is an excellent one, if not particularly noteworthy in the bicycle section itself.</p>
        <p>THE LATE FREDERICK VON KRUPP.</p>
        <p>The great German ironmaster, Frederick von Krupp, died on Saturday afternoon at his residence in Essen, of apoplexy. He had suffered from an affection of the heart for several years, and the nervous excitement produced by the libellous attacks on him by a Socialistic paper. in Berlin is said to have been a Contributory cause of his death. To Essen the news of Herr Krupp's death came like a thunderbolt, as it was quite unex- pected. Special editions of the newspapers spread the sad tidings. Groups collected in the streets and dis- cussed the heavy blow the town had suffered. In the morning 3000 employees at his works had as- sembled to pro- test against ale base calumnina- tons of their master, and sent a deputation to the Villa Eluegal, but it was not ad- mitted, as Herr Krupp_ was al-</p>
        <p>`TEE LATE ICEITPP.</p>
        <p>ready dying. Ile Was born in 1854 and was the only son of Alfred Krupp, who founded the great firm as it is known to-day. It consists of the great steel works at Essen, other steel works in Westphalia, the Cru9on works near Magdeburg, four great blast furnace plants, four coal mines in Germany, part interest in other German coal mines, many iron mines in Germany and Spain, several ocean steamers, stone quarries, clay and sand pits, etc. They employ first and last 150,000 men, wo'men, and children. At Essen alone the workmen number 50,000. The works there cover 860 acres. There are no fewer than thirty-six miles of railway lines within their boundaries.</p>
        <p>GOVERNOR-GENERALSHIP OF AUSTRALIA. Since the resignation of the Marquis of Linlithgow, better known to the public as the Earl of • Hopetoun, there have been endless rumours as to his successor in the Governor - generalship of the Commonweath of Australlia and almost every week fresh names have been mentioned in connection with this important post. At one time Colonel McCalmont was reported a certainty; and more recently the Duke of Somerset and Lord Windsor were mentioned as having been selected. Now comes the one appointment never whispered by public gossip...the confirmation of Lord Tennyson in</p>
        <p>the position which he took up during the Interregnum between Lord Linlithgow's resig- nation and the arrival of the Governor-general However, by Lord Tennyson's own wish the appointment is only to be for one year, instead of five; but no reason is offered for this concession. Lord Tennyson, is the second sonof the great poet, whom he succeeded In the peerage at his</p>
        <p>LOUD TENNYSON, THE NEW GOVERNOR- GENERAL OF AUSTRALIA. death ten years ago. Just over fifty years of age, he was born on August 11th, 1852, and was named Hallam in memory of the dearly loved friend, whose early death bed inspired his father to write "In Memoriam." Educated at Marlborough and Trinity, Cambridge, he acted as his father's private secretary up to the time of his death, and, following i this up, n 1897, published a "Memoir " of him which was read 'with great interest. In 1899 he was Governor and Commander-in-Chief of South Australia which post he continued to hold after the Federation. He has made . himself very popular amongst the colonials, and his promotion to the higher appointment has been well received and has given general satisfaction. LATE EX-COMISSIONER KERR. Ex.-Coinmisidoner Kerr died from heart disease on *Yridity last, at his residence, Northwood, Middlesex. For forty-two years he administered ;tidies with law at the City of London Court, fearlessly, and, above all, with common-sense. He was " down " on unscrupulous money-lenders, and, always desiring to restrict eases as much as possible, was often severe upon solicitors prac- tising before him, especially when they objected to having their bills of costs taxed. As a sriminal judge he was [never by any. means looked upon favourably by the hardened classes. He was too shrewd for them. But in the case of first offenders he al- ways endeav- oared to temper j astiee with mercy, and with the attempts at ref orminL not to o hardened criminals he warmly syin- pathised, taking a deep interest in THE LATE EX-COMMISSIONER furthering the work of the St. mom Giles's Chris- tian Mission. He was born in Glasgow in 1821, and educated at Glasgow University, which created him a Doctor of Law in 1857. He became a member of the Faculty of Advocates in Edinburgh, but came to London as a London pA a member of Lincoln's Inn, and in 1848 was called to the English Bar, twelve years afterwards becoming a member .of the Middle Temple. He was appointed Judge of the City of London Court in 1859. AN UNEXPECTED SENTENCE. Though the public indignation awakened by the prosecution in the Penruddooke cruelty case was considerably modified by the strong defence, such lenient judgment was very unexpected. Mrs. Penruddocke the wife of a wealthy Wilt- shire landowner and magistrate had some amaz- ing charges of cruelty to her little seven-year-old daughter, Conny, brought against her at the instance of the N.S.P.C.C. The allegations were that the girl had been made to eat mustard sandwiches and pepper; to run up and down the drive for two or three hours in the rain; !thrashed with nettles, a stick, whip and keys; mace to stand on the branch of a tree on one leg; given raisins livith mustard in them; poorly fed and poorly clothed and had her hair cut off because someone admired it. This ill-treatment was asserted to have taken place since 1901, and chiefly at Compton Park, the seat of the Penrud dockes, near Salisbury. Connie was the youngest but one of a family of six children, and suffered from a physical dis- ability; in con- sequence of which the mother appeared to have "taken a spite against her," said counsel, A series of witnesses to the conduct of Mrs. Penruddocke, most of whom had been in her service, emphati- cally bore out the charges, and it appeared that as a result of information being given to the ,N.S.P.C.C. by one of the servants, the whole of the employees were discharged. The defence was a denial of all the charges, though Mrs. Penrud- docke admitted chastising the child for her physical weakness, believing that to be proper treatment. Mr. Justice Bigham imposed, a fine of £5O which he thought would "meet the justice of the case, and. sufficlfently mark the character of the offence." As a contrast one may notice the sen- tence of two months' hard labour passed at West- minster Police Court on Saturday= a poor woman named Mary Keen for having neglected her two children, aged eleven and Was.</p>
        <p>MRS. PENRUDDOCKE.:</p>
        <p>Tan colliers' claim— Eight hours work, and eight hours play, Eight hours sleep, eight bob a day— is, like " three acres and a cow," not attained yet, in spite of strikes. "In the mining district" (writes the local " Sheffield Weekly Indepekdent " commissioner on the late bard times) " I found employment scarce and earnings at a low standard amongst the colliers. The reasons for this were ioselligently discussed by Mr , William Bradley, collier, of Cunliffe-street, Coal Aston, at whose house I called to enquire about facts which had caused some stir." The commissioner first saw Mrs. Bradley, who, in response to enquiries, said : " It is not my husband but my eldest daughter there who has been the subject of all this commotion. Sho has been a great suffarer all her life. We had the doctor to her several times, and he said that her comp' slat was summia, and she was inclined to be consumptive, But he said her case was so serious that nothing could do her any real good. Nevertheless, in the end Dr. Williams pink pills for pale people cured her and made her well and hearty." " In what way was she affected ?" " She was never able to digest her food, and always felt a heavy oppressiveness, especially in the morning. She never felt ready to e,ret up. When the doctor gave her medicine she was seldom able to keep it on her stomach. We had tried well nigh everything, when a friend said, ' Why no got abe of Dr. Williams' pink pills ; they can't do your daughter harm, as they con- tain nothing that could injure anyone.' We had read of their curse, of course, in almost every paper, but I must bell you truthfully, none of Us really expected they would be of any use to my daughter, who had been a life-long sufferer. But the event proved otherwise. " It will be about eighteen months ago since we had tho first bov, and it was wonderful to see what an im- provement even one box made. We were so pleased that a second box was obtained, and after that was finished my daughter began to pull up quicker than we thought possible.</p>
        <p>Miss Brad ley, a bright-looking young woman, con- irmed this and bore out her mother's statements in praise of the medicine. Anminia is not a disease of girls only ; men often suffer from it ; pale lips, loss of appetite, ehortness of breath, and lost energy, mark its progress towards consumption. It is due to poverty of blood, and once a cold is caught, spittiug of bleed, pro- fuse perspiration, loss of weight, and as increasing gaunt sallownese, tell the tale but too plainly as in the case of Miss Bradley. De Macau:is' pink piila cure ' anemia and consumption by enriching sand increasing the blood. whieh heals the wounded lunge, restores appetite and digestion, and gives back vital energy which may be oozing away. Further evidence ef their Dower to cure the most intractable diseases will be found in the history of a schoolmaster who lost his memory frem paralysis in the brain, as described on another page of this paper. Mr Bradley said the pills had saved him many pounds in doctors' bills, besides saving his daughter's life after the doctor despaired of her. He is a typical miner, but was only working about three days a week, a result of restricted output.</p>
        <p>A 4. FRBDERTCE VON</p>
