      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <p>-</p>
        <p>This week, Mr. Chamberlain embarked on board H.M.S. Good Hope on his great journey to South Africa, sailing a few days later than was originally intended. On Friday he bade his Majesty farewell at Windsor, dining and sleeping and meeting the King of Portugal there. On Tuesday he e,mbarked upon the Good Hope at Portsmouth. The route to be taken is the east coast one via the Suez Canal to Durban, which, to the disgust of Cape Town, will be the port of landing. The visit will include Natal, the Transvaal, Orange River Colony and Cape Colony, where he will interview all the leading repr es entative men of every party and shade of opinion; and he will again hold consultation with Messrs. Botha and De- larey, who are now most friendly in their feelings towards t h e Colonial Secretary, and will foregather with him in the Western Transvaal. His object he put into a nutshell in the !House of Commons, when he said, "I am going out with an open mind and a firm determination to learn for myself." H.M.S. Good Hope is under the command of Admiral Fawkes' who has given up his quarters to the Colonial Secretary. Mr. and Mrs. Chamberlain's apartments have been simply but comfortably furnished for the voyage, and the admiral's " look- out " has been turned into a study, where the Secretary for the Colonies will do his work. THE STANLEY SHOW. The Stanley Show at the Agricultural Hall, opened last week by Mr. Thomas Lough, M. P., has attracted as much attention this year as ever; and the number of exhibitors is well maintained. The general quality, also, is well preserved throughout, and the English-made bicycle still holds its position unrivalled. It is noteworthy, however, how very few novelties are put forward. The founders of this show are forced by their lease to limit the number of motors to twenty-five; but the motor- rycle is out in force and has obviously cone to ltay. This form of locomotion, which is still in its infancy, shows a marked advance and many changes, while no two makers show an exactly similar type, he motors and the frames being made and placed just as individual taste or judgment suggests. Many</p>
        <p>-FAWKES.</p>
