      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>NORTHERN UNION. THE NORTHERN RUGBY LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>THE NORTHERN UNION. THE NORTHERN RUGBY LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>First Division.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON v. HULL KINI3TON EMIRS.</p>
        <p>Warrington made another journey to Hull on Saturday when I hey met Kingston Rovers, being still without Dicken- son at ()entre. Brooks of the "A. team filled the vaeancy. the team being otherwise at full strength. Mr B Ashton of Oldham was the referee and the teams were as follows :</p>
        <p>Hull Kingston Rovers—Book, -11 Binalair : threequarter backs, G H West, .1 Gordon, 0 Levels and G Bell ; half banks J Barry and W S Cards; forwards A Starks (Captain) ,J Gath G Fletcher, A Windle, W Feetham, T aorman, It Golding and A Kemp. Warrington—Back, Knight; threaquarter baeks, Fish Isherwood (captain), Brooks and Harris ; half backs, Burgess and Hockenhall ; forwards. Morrison. Boardman, F Swift, J Swift, A Naylor, Taylor, Forster, and Edmonsen. Starks started, and his side had the better of the opening tactics. The home team soored first. It was from a scrum- mage on the Warrington line that Barry threw the ball to West. who, however, finding himself in a tight plias, passed it back to Lewis, and that player beating a coople of his opponents. got over the line. Starks failed at goal. War- rington played with pluok and determination, and gave the Rovers a hot time, but their record at half-time was only a penalty goal kicked by Fish, so that the home team stood at 3 points to 2. After the interval some ezoltement was provided by Bell getting over the line at the corner, and by the point being raft:Bed. Soon afterwards Starks pick d up the ball. ran down touch, and Macaw it to the forwards. Feather&amp; took it and added a capital try, which however, was not converted. Warrington were forced to defend almost continuously, they were so badly beaten forward. A dropped goal by Sinclair gave the Rovers two more points and the game ended— Kingston Rovers, 1 goal 2 tries (a points) ; Warrington, I. goal (2 points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>SWINTONy. WIDNBS.</head>
        <p>SWINTONy. WIDNBS.</p>
        <p>"I hear they want more</p>
        <p>Wretched weather prevailed at Swinton for this Inateh,and there was but a moderate attendance The game was faet,and though Swinton held the ascendant their passing was faulty. and the visitors had no difficulty in repelling the attack. Then Slughes ran grandly for Widnes, being pulled up on the line, and Swinton followed with a fierce attack. Wallwork , dropped the ball when he had no one to beat, but made ; amends shortly after by dropping a goal. Roused by this reverse, Widnes attacked,and Hughes scored a splendid try. Tilley failed at goal, and the interval arrived with the score--Widaes 1 try (3 points), Swinton 1 goal (2 points). On resuming operations were contested in neutral terri- tory, but Widnes were forced back, and Hampson scared at the corner, Presto-, failed at goal. Morgan threw a grand chance away by a wild pass. Cooper obtained another try for the home side, Preston failing at goal. In the closing stages Widnes had the best of matters, and an exciting finish ensued. Tilley placed two penalty goals, and crossed the Swinton line after a short dribble, but the point was dis- allowed, and Swinton won by 1 goal and 2 tries (S points), to Widnes 2 goals and 1 try (7 points). Teams : Swinton—A Chorley, back: J X Cooper, R Valentine, W Wallwork andY Xampson, three-qnsrter hacks; F J Morgan and T Samuels, half backs; J Evans, W Yates, B Yates. H Barnett, R Berry, R Tickle, T Pomfret and J Preston, forwards. Widnes-4 II Tilley, back; T Peacock, G Bowe, A Taylor and J. Hughes three-quarter backs; J Taylor and. B Douglas half backs; G Aspey, J Power. H Curtis, W Rigby, J Leyshon, M Webster, T Lanch and Turner. forwards. Referee, Ur B Newton, Altritcham. Touch judges, Messrs B H mirk, Wigan and T Barnes, Warrington,</p>
        <p>ST HELENS v OLDHAM On Saturday before 2,000 people at St Helens. There was tie return to the kick-off, and Banks breaking away, gave a grand pass to Hall, but Civil tackled him. St Helens found their way to the centre, where several tight scrimmages were contested. A grand kick by Hall sent play into the Oldham half, but Thomas made a spirited return, the ball going dead. The drop-out saw play at the half-way the again, bat all attempts of the Oldham basks to get away were futile, meths St Helens tackling was very fine, Thomas shortly after scored for Oldham. Half-time score .—Oldham 1 try, St Helene nil. On resuming, a free kick gave Oldham a good foothold in the home half, and Civil getting possession made a fine run, but Foulkes brought him down. Oldham kept up the pres- sure for some time but a grand kick by Foulkes changed the aspect of the game, and Oldham being penalised. Hall kie,ked, but Oldham teuebed down. t Helene made bold eff a rts, and reached the home line, and only determined tackling prevented It Helens from scoring. Immediately after this the Oldham basks rushed down, and Sam Less had hard lines, the ball being kicked dead. Wilkinson was sent off the field for rough play. 'Result :—Oldharn 1 try (3 points), It Helene nil. BROUGHTON RANGERS T. BRIGHOUSE RANGERS. Played at Brighouse, before 2,000 spectators. Broughton started, and kicking exchanges saw the game at centre. Smart play by Kam James caused Saville to be forced Into tench. Wilson nnesed to Harnpson, who hod a walk In, but failed to take the ball. Kilburn commenced the second half. and Brighouse attacked, but Barrett sent the home team back. Here a lot of kicking exchanges followed with ad• vantage to the Lancashire men. Eventually Hozq gave Hampeon another chances, but he Gould not gerthrough. Broughton attacked strongly near the finish of the game, and looked liked scoring on several occasions, and Oram missed a penalty goal. BriShOUSB then assumed the aggres- sive, but were repulsed. Broughton. however, could not penetrate the defence and the game ended in a pointless draw. BATLEY V LEIGH.</p>
        <p>In this snatch, which was played at Monist Pleasant, neither side was at full strength, and it did not mend matters, from the Batley standpoint, when early on in the game Robinson had to retire for good, owing to a broken rib. The Isitors had to act on the defensive for the first ten minutes, and in the end testy scored a capital try, after the ball had been in the bands of Hodgson, Oakland, Fitz- gerald, and Goodall. The plane-kick, however, proved too difficult for Davies to succeesfully negotiate. Interesting play followed, and Batley being penalised, Johnson seat the ball over the bar. Nothing further was scored up to half- time. Batley re-commenced In eager fashion. At length I Rogers made a mark, and Davies kicked a gaol. Later on Bennett dropped a goal for Leigh. and in the last minute Johnson would have placed two more points to the visitors' score. but an easy plece•kick was missed, The gains ended In a win fer Batley by 1 goal. I try (5 points), to Leigh 2 goals (4 points). BRAD73RD V WIGAN. Played at Park Avenue' before 4,000 spectators. After the kick-off the home team had the advantage for a while, ind Mosby had a futile shot at zeal. Then Wigan. by means of good kicking, reached the home *. 25." but not for 'long. as Broadley charged a kick, mad ran over for a try. Then from penalties Mosby and Hager' kicked goals for Bradford. Half -timo score :—Bradford, 2 goals and I try a points). Wigan I goal (2 points). On resuming, the Wigaa men pressed very hard for a long time, and Rothwell was near dropping another goal. Anderson made a very smart effort. but he was held, and nothing result sd. Result :—Bradford goals and 1 try (7 points) ; Wizan,l goal (2 points). DEFEAT OF TIME LEADEBB BY SALFORD. Vile weather was experienced at The Willows, Weaste, en Saturday, on the occasion of the visit of Halifax, who came with an unbeaten record. There would be about 8.010 people present. Teams Salford—D Smith, back; U Rodgers, R J Messer, J Lomas and T G Bell, threeguartsr basks ; I Harter and Ben Griffiths, half backs J Rhapps. J Williams. G Heath, P Tunney, SI Bulkier, W Brown, II Short, and R Shaw, forwards. Halitax—W 3 Little. back ;3 B Jones, 7.8 Riley. W Williams, and H Hadwen, thresquarter basks ' • A J Drown and J. A Rigg, half backs ; G Kitson. J. Riley,Santmerskill, W Blunter, 3 Switsbank, F Hammond, W Merton, and II Wilson forwards. Referee, Slr J Oakland, Outwool Church. Touch judges, Messrs 3 Halliwell, Wigan, and J Bdwards; St Helens. Little started, and Brown returned to mid-lebt. The Halifax full bask, from a dribble on the part of the home formsras,*punted to the centre. • bout of passing between Griffiths, Lomas, and Bell caused the visitors' defence some troable. Further passing was indulged in by Rigg, Jones, Riley, and Little, en behalf of Halifax, but although a footing was galued in the home half there seemed no likelihood of a score. It was a stiff forward game, and in the scrummage Salford had rather the best of it. Harter got possession several times, and Lomas, Bell. and Messer were given opportunities. 'terse on the interval several scrummages I were formed a few yards from the Halifax line. Griffiths, , almost between the p"sts, lost possession, and missed a fine</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <p>1,:</p>
        <p>BEE</p>
        <p>opportunity. Immediately Lomas, from the free kick ,landed a goal. Half-time some—Salford 1 goal, Halifax ail. On resuming, the earnestness of the Salford men was strongly made manifest. They attacked }lonely, and Messer and Rodgers carried play almost to the verge al the visitors' line. Tbs ball was eventually kicked dead, and than the game was stooped for a short time owing to the home half back. Griffiths. receiving an Injury to his bead, He resumed and Halifax made one of the best bids to Beare during the whole game, They had nit previonsly been dangerous, hut now there was a concerted forward dribble, headed by Jack Riley. to the verge of the Raiford line, where Dan Smith and his men were rather lucky to effect a clearance. Subse- quently pla v again. favenrest Salford, and from a pass by Lomas, Bell crossed the line at the corner, the plaee kick failing. Lomas next dropped a goal, and the minute from time Shore scored a try close to the posts, the goal points being added by Lomas. Result :—Salford, 2 goals, 2 tries (12 points) ; Halifax, nil. HUNSLET • HUDDERSFIELD. A LIST MOMENT VICTORY. There was a splendid match, with a most exciting (Males, between the Hunelet and Hudiersfield clubs at Park Side. Hunslet gaining the verdict in the last minute of time The match took place before 4.000 spectators. The scores at the interval were—Huddersfield 1 goal and 2 tries (8 points), Hunslet 2 goals (4 points). The second half saw the Hunslet forwards in far better trim than they had previously been, the scrummages being wheeled, or the bail heeled oat, very frequently. The result was that the home backs were repeatedly in possession. Ilunslet 4 goals and one try (11 points). Huddersfield 1 goal and 2 tries (8 palate).</p>
