      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>NESLEYAN FOREIGN MISSIONS Anniversary Meetings.</head>
        <p>NESLEYAN FOREIGN MISSIONS Anniversary Meetings.</p>
        <p>Wesleyan missionary anniversary services and mne'- ings have been held in several parts of the Warrington Circuit during the past week. At Peakoth, on Sunday, the Rev Wilkins Res, of Bewsey-road Chureh,presched morning and evening and also conducted a young people's service in the aftorneon Ov Monday evening. a public meeting was held. Mr C E Parker presided and the speakers were the Rev G H Carnson (superintendent of the circuit), and the Rev R Wilkins Rees. The ohnirman, in the course of his ad- dress, referred to the introduction of Christianity into these islands as the result of missionary I effort by the ROMMIS *ad emphasised the necessity of going back to the primary source of inspiration in connection with foreign missionary work.—The Rev R Wilkis liNis referred to the diffi- euities which missiontriee experienced, and to the noble self-sacrifices which they made.—The Rev G H Carnson referred generally to the various missions carried on in different parts of the world.—The collections on Sunday amounted to over LIS, whieh is an increase upon the previous year. Penketh also contributed largely to the ladies auxiliary, for which a sale of work was held last week. _ _ _</p>
        <p>Missionary services were held at Stockton Heath on Sunday and a missionary meeting was held on Tuesday evening, Councillor Upson presided and exprassed his pleaeure that as far as could be at prescnt ascertained there was a general advance in the gifts to foreign missions throughout the circuit. He proceeded to speak of kis interest in foreign mission- ary work and aornmended it for their support.—The Rev R Wilkins Rees dealt with some of the criticisms which were occasionally passed os foreign missions and proceeded to dilate on the benefits which followed iu the train of missionary effort. He instanced the educational advantages, the saving of life through the introdaction of our own system of medicine and through the enlight- enment of the people in regard to the value of human life, the improved status of women under Christianity, and the improved administration of law. Re also spoke of the spiritual aspect. At Sankey Bridges, neiseionary anniversary services were held on Sunday. The Rev J Gunnell preached in the morning, and a young people's service was con- ducted in the afternoon by Mr A Perkins. At night,the pulpit was occupied by Mr Councillor Upson. The missionary meeting was held on Wednesday evesing,tha chair being taken by Mr J Bsiley.—The Rev R Wilkins Rees reviewed the work in the mission field and the Rev W J J Neville also spoke on missionary work. Col- lections were taken on behalf of Wesleyan Foreign Miesione at all the services and meetings named with satisfactory results. Sankey Bridges is the only place where the collections have fallen short of these of a year age.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>Halton Church.</head>
        <p>Halton Church.</p>
        <p>At the Chester Consistory Court en Wednesday the Rev 0 D Wray, M.A., yicar of St Mary's,Ralten, was granted permission to place stained glass re- presenting the" Annunciation of the Blessed 'Virgin Mary" in a window in the south aisle ef the church, to commemorate the fhtiatla anniversary of the church.</p>
