      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>TITF. FREE CHURCHES. ,twitt</head>
        <p>TITF. FREE CHURCHES. ,twitt</p>
        <p>REV. HUGH PRICE HUGHES.</p>
        <p>The passing away of the Rev. Hugh Pries Hughes, M.A., came as a sudden and terrible shock to his friends as well as to the great Wesleyan body and to the Free Churches is general. True, he had been laid aside for a lengthened period in consequence of a break- down in health, but once again he was at his old work, with nearly, if not quite all, his usual vigour. The Rev. Thomas Law, the General Secretary of the National Free Church Council, who had been a close friend of Mr. Hughes for many years, had perhaps the last sustained conversation with the deceased gentleman, being with him at noon on the day of his death. "I thought he was looking very much better," says Mr. Law in an interview in the Free Church Chronicle; "in- deed, for the first time since his illness, I came to the conclusion that he was becoming his old self again. He was full of buoyancy, and chatted brightly about his future work. He said he fully meant to attend the next meeting of the General Committee at Birming- ham, and also promised to be at Brighton during the week of our annual Council, and to address a mass meeting in the Dome.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>MR. HUGHES AND PASSIVE RESISTANCE.</head>
        <p>MR. HUGHES AND PASSIVE RESISTANCE.</p>
        <p>At the same interview, Mr. Hughes referred to the Education Bill, emphasising the great importance of unity amongst Free Churchmen. "Indeed," says Mr. Law, "it may be said that his last message was a warn- ing against division. He himself always said he would refuse the rate to be levied under the Bill. The loss of Mr. Hughes is a severe blow to the Free Churches, eoming at such a critical time. Mr. Hughes took a pro- minent part in the formation of the National Free Church COUWAIS, traversing the country with Dr. Berry and Mr. Law, east, west, north, and south, advocating the formation of Free Church Councils. He was present at the first congress, and heilipedlo pilot the scheme through its initial difficulties, and up to the day of his death gave much time to the movement.</p>
        <p>DE. PARKER AND THE NATIONAL FREE CHURCI</p>
        <p>COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>We understand that as late as Thursday last, Dr. Parker expressed a strong hope that he would be able to take his place as President of the National Council at Brighton in March next. His presidential address was already practically prepared, and he intimated his desire that under any circumstances it should be read. Dr. Parker also expressed a strong wish that in the event of his prolonged illness or death, Dr. Maclaren should be induced to occupy his place in presiding over the Sessions.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>DR. MONRO GIBSON ON THE EDUCA2ION BILL.</head>
        <p>DR. MONRO GIBSON ON THE EDUCA2ION BILL.</p>
        <p>Speaking at the annual meeting of the Metropolitan Free Church Federation recently, the Rev. J. Monro Gibson, DD., the newly- appointed President, said that, notwithstanding the amendments made in the Education Bill in passing through Committee, it remained so radically unjust that they were obliged to maintain their attitude of determined resist- ance. He submitted to the meeting a mani- festo, stating that while acknowledging chat the adoption of the Kenyon-Slaney amend- ment was satisfactory so far as it goes, in the way of giving to lay representatives a share of responsibility for the religious teaching, it gives no guarantee that in schools to which children of all denominations will be compelled to go, there will not be teaching of such sort as to beget and foster in children whose parents hold the Evangelical faith a suspicion of, and even' contempt for, the religion of their parents. On the other hand, of such amendments as have been ostentatiously proposed as concessions to Nonconformists, some are wholly illusory, and ethers only aggravate the evils the Bill inflicts.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>SYMPATHY FROM THE COLONIES.</head>
        <p>SYMPATHY FROM THE COLONIES.</p>
        <p>The Colonies are showing givat interest in the educational battle now proceeding. The Jamaica Council has sent to the National Council a resolution of "heartiest sym- pathy with the determined opposition to the Government Education Bill, being as- sured that this Bill is subversive of the Rights of Conscience, and of the Educational Interests of the Nation." A resolution from the Queens- land Congregational Union, Brisbane, expresses "our abhorrence of the attempt to fetter re- ligious liberty and outrage conscience, and that we most heartily encourage those who are seek- ing to maintain the freedom of the faith once delivered to the Saints." A cablegram has also been received from the Queensland Council of Churches, and a resolution from the Presbytery of Gippsland, Australia, expressing sympathy.</p>
        <p>WHY DO NOT WORKING MEN ATTEND CHURCH ?</p>
        <p>This is the problem dealt with in an inter- view in the Free Church Chronicle with Mr. Richard Bell, M.P., Secretary of the Amalga- mated Society of Railway Servants. Mr. Bell holds that working men have no objection to religious services, but in the busy centres, where men have no time to themselves in the week, and no opportunities of getting recreation, they thoughtlessly take the few hours of Sunday for that purpose. The first step is to get at the child, says Mr. Bell. The Sunday-schools must be made more attractive; they must retain their hold on the youth.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>PRACTICAL SCREIdE.</head>
        <p>A PRACTICAL SCREIdE.</p>
        <p>The Leeds Free Church Council has under consideration an unmistakably practical scheme. It is proposed to establish a fund by which men and women belonging to the different denomi. tions who have fallen into poverty and distress through no fault of their own may be relieved. The scheme, if carried out, will show in • striking manner the solidarity and brotherhood of Free Church membership.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>"GETTING EP!"</head>
        <p>"GETTING EP!"</p>
        <p>"Getting up! "is the title of a bright, breezy article from the pen of the Rev. F. B. Meyer, 8.A., in the December Free Churchman. Mr. Meyer finds as much difficulty in getting up as most people these cold mornings, and says the task is easiest in St. Petersburg in winter—because the rooms are kept at an equable temperiture—lndia in the cool season and the Alps. He makes some telling remarks about the " getting up" in life as apart from the mere "getting on," so mut, emphasised nowadays.</p>
        <p>Pe.sonal. Mr J 11 Fisher (M A Cantab), solicitor, he joined Mr .Koln,rt Wham in De rtmashic. They will prac- tice et Golden-square,' Market-place, under the style of " Dobson and Fisher." Temporary Authority. At Dareshuiy Petty Sessions on Tuesday, Thomas Royle Westbrook was granted temporary authority, to sell at off beer-license premises recently in the possession of Sarala Hewitt.</p>
        <p>MI</p>
      </div>
