        <p>THE EXAMINER.</p>
        <p>NOVEMBER 28tb,1902.</p>
        <p>TEE manner of Mr. Mr. Chamberlain's CHA MBERLAIN'S setting Mission.</p>
        <p>- out for South Africa on Wednesday brings to mind the reports of OLIVER CROMWELL'S historic departure for Ireland. There were applauding crowds to bid "God speed" to the popular hero on each occasion, but the Colonial Secretary seems to have been far too happy to en- tertain such an idea as that which presented itself to the gloomier mind of Cromwell. "They would gather in equal numbers," he said, " to see me hanged." The applause of crowds is apt to be somewhat undiscerning, and the populace which makes clamour in the streets is much given to changing either the object of its notice or its point of view. Whatever Mr CHAMBERLAIN may suc- ceed in doing in South Africa, it is highly im probable that the man-in-the-street will be either so curious or so enthusiastic about him on his return as he undoubtedly is at the present time. These ovations and hurrahings are not to our liking, and we do not think that Mr CHAMBERLAIN himself has much relish for them. He had very serious work in hand. The situation in South Africa is by no means bright, and he will be confronted with problems that will try his mettle and put his statesmanship to a severe test. It is idle to pretend that the war has produced all 1 that was expected of it. No doubt expectation was too high. People forget that while the transi- tion from war to peace is sudden, the fabric of society and settled government cannot be up- reared in a day. But it has to be admitted that in South Africa the chaos is only being very slo Rly reduced to order, so slowly that our own kith and kin are restive and discontented. Although there is still a Press censorship in the Transvaal, and notwithstanding the fact that the newspapers out there are controlled by the capitalists, materials enabling us to piece together a fairly accurate I picture of a state of affairs which is anything but I agreeable or of promise for the future, continue to I reach us in private or in public letters. There I appeared in a London newspaper on Wednesday a communication from Johannesburg remarkable for its frankness. The development of the country and the exploitation of its mineral wealth is being seriously hampered, it appears, by British " red tape." Many of the persons most affected would be glad enough to revert to the conditions which prevailei before the recent conflict. " Bad as was the Boer rule as a whole," it is remarked, "it is universally agreed that its legislation in respect of mineral resources and mining was second to none." This is altogether a new story. "There is now stag- nation in enterprise all round," we are further assured, and the character of the official control which has been established is held to be mainly responsible for this absence of liveliness and pros- perity. There is distress on the Rand. Repatria- tion and land settlement have been clumsily directed at a large cost with poor results. Then although the Boer has gone under,the Englishman has not come to the top. It is the German who has emerged into sunshine and prosperity. The large financial houses of the Transvaal are novls' all under German control we are told. 'al the Chamber of Mines German!power is overwhelming.</p>
        <p>This is certainly a poor result of the expenditure of British blood and treasure in the acquisition of the Transvaal. And we are afraid that this is a result which can hardly be reversed by anything Mr CHAmsEnraric can do or advise. The indica- tions at the moment are that the COLONIAL SECRE- TARY has undertaken a task which is much too colossal for any, one man to carry through. He goes with an open mind as he says. We trust that he also goes with a predisposition in favour of the almost immediate establishment of respon- sible government in the newly-acquired territories • That seems to be the one remedy for such of the existing evils as statesmanship can cure. Should the present system be continued the seeds of new roubles will most certainly be sown through the creation of a new bureaucracy, which will be as little to the liking of any class of the inhabitants of the Transvaal as was the influx of Hollander officials in the later years of Mr KB.UGER'S Government. If Mr CHAMBERLAiN really sees for himself and not through the eyes of the young Oxford men who have collected round Lord MILER, his visit to South Africa may have the most important consequences; but even the COLONIAL SECRETARY cannot work miracles. How- ever, Mr CHAMBERLAIN has with him the best wishes of the whole of his countrymen, and the opportunity of crowning himself with more glori- ous laurels than ever made a circle round a con- queror's brow. We most heartily wish him God speed on his mission.</p>
        <p>THE character of the Edu- The Sequel of the • Education cation Bill and the means Bill. by which it is being forced through Parliament have, together with the clear disclosure of the narrow sectarian and selfish ends of the promoters, at length produced the inevit- able result of infusing new life into the disestablishment movement. The Liberation Society has issued a manifesto which sounds one of the old battle cries of liberty with a newly- revised vigour. For more than a quarter of a century past the Liberationist movement cannot be said to have possessed any great amount of life. We seemed to be much nearer to disestablishment in the 60's and '7o's of the last century than we were at the century's close. The lesson of the interval has been perhaps that the Church is never in real danger except when it offers provocation. It is not a church that is popular with the people, but the people can only be roused to interference with it by arrogant acts of its own. The Education Bill is provocation of the first order. As the Liberation Society manifesto points out, the policy that measure aims at is the strengthening and perpetuating of the English Church Establishes ent. It has been framed in accordance with the require- ments of the convocations of Canterbury and York. Bishops have been present at the House of Commons every day to keep the Government</p>
        <p>in mind of the fact that it must not swerve. But the mind of the English people is too tolerant to resent with any considerable force the . mere attempt of a Church to strengthen its posi- tion. That is the natural direction of the efforts of all churches. It must be something in the manner in which the thing is done that provokes popular resentment. What the people perceive in the Education Bill is a really gross attempt not only to confer a new endowment on the Church, but to so reorganise the larger part of the educa- tional machinery of the country that it will operate as a feeder to the Church Establishment* It is as Mr LOYD-GEORGE puts it, a device for "lamb stealing," and this is to be done with national money. The Church is managing to wriggle out of every present and prospective liability. It has even broken through the bargain that it should maintain what it will still continue to regard and to use as its own schools. By successive stages of meanness it has whilst the Bill has been in progress, contrived to procure such financial arrangements as will pro- vide a fund to do what even the original framers of the Bill expected the Church to do for itself. This is a thing that is not likely to be forgo tten. The burdens that are removed from the Church are laid upon the people, and there will be a con- tinual rankling of injury and wrong to keep alive the memory of the means by which the Establish- ment retained possession of its schools whilst compelling the publiC to provide the entire cost of education. "It is coming to be generally recognised," says the manifesto of the Libera- tion:Society, ',that it is the existence of a Church invested with political power, enjoying special privileges, and maintained by public endow- ments which is the main cause of our educational difficulties, and that only by striking 'at the root of the tree can we be rid of its baneful fruit." That is a conclusion that must by recent events have been very powerfully forced on the minds of the people. With the interests of the Church entrenched behind the House of Lords it may take a generation to undo the work that is now being done in Parliament at the bidding of the Church. But when the evil and menacing system that is being built up to-day is eventually destroy ed, as most certainly it will be, the Church Establish- ment will find itself in a much different position from that which it now occupies. It will not be arrogantly dictating terms and snatching paltry advantages. It will be a Church with its back to the wall, having made new enemies and provoked old ones by its relationship to the most short- sighted measure that an Euglish Ministry ever consented to adopt at the bidding of a section of its supporters.</p>
        <p>We. are glad that the London " Times " has p übhs.hed such an article as appeared last week charging the Corporation of Warrington with extravagance in its municipal undertakings, i because t affords an excellent opportunity of stating how the Corporation really does stand in regard to some of the enterprises which have been launched in recent years Anything more absurd, illogical and unfair than the atatements of the "Times" correspondent it would be difficult to find. A representative of the" Examiner "has spent some time in investi- gating fully the charges so recklessly made, with the result that facts have been unearthed which show the Corporation to be in an especially happy position so far as these particular undertakings are concerned. Our readers will, we are sure, read with interest and satisfaction the special article dealing with the subject in another column. They will there obtain information which has been eagerly awaited for some time past and for various reasons, and they will be able to see for themselves how far the Corporation has been justified.by actual results as well as necessities in incuriing the undoubtedly heavy expenditure of the past generation. Practically the only piece of fair criticism in the" Times " correspondent's articleis n reference to the notorious "palace of justice," and in regard to the cost and size of this building there is room for more than one opinion. Oae would imagine, from the tone of the critic's re- marks in general, that the Corporation had spent hundreds of thousands of pounds for the mere fun ofthe thing and not with the sole aim of benefit-</p>
        <p>ing both morally and materially the ratepayers of present and future generations. We are not I aware of the identity of this distant critic, but there is good reason for suspecting that he is one of the agents of those big American corn: bines which have cast a lustful eye on the English boroughs and would like to supply—. for a consideration—all that the Corporations themselves are unselfishly supplying in the way of gas, electricity, tramways and water. This kind of "municipal trading" puts them out of courtto a great extent and curtails the opportunities for doing business in a way which must be far from palatable. It is somewhat unfortunate for the credit of the " Times" that there is as much reason for questioning the bona-fides of the corres- pondent as there is for disbelieving his state- ments.</p>
        <p>, - . . .</p>
      </div>
