      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>EXAMINER FRIDA V NOVEMBER 28, 1902</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER FRIDA V NOVEMBER 28, 1902</p>
        <p>RUNCORN NOTES.</p>
        <p>LBY A CORUBSPONDENT]. The following special telegram appeared in a daily contemporary yesterday :—" The composition of the Cheshire team to meet Yorkshire at Brad- ford on Saturday is at present very doubtful. Nine men are chosen from Runcorn, and these have announced their intention not to play. The chief grievance seems to be the rate of pay, which is less than for a club journey into Yorkshire. Houghton is a certain absentee, as be has been suffering for some weeks." Needless to say the above par caused more than a little comment in f ootball circles in general and Runcorn in particular on its publication. As a matter of fact, however, the report is absolutely without foundation and the local powers that be, ' scoff at the idea of Runcorn players refusing to do service for their County. It is impossible to say how this rumour got circulated or what called it into existence but inquiries from local officials show it to be absolutely without foundation. At a com- mittee meeting held last night all the players selected, decided to turn out without any demur and the so-called "strike "is really no " strike " at all and never even had an existence save on paper. It is only fair in justice to the Club to give this denial the same publicity as the rumour.</p>
        <p>It is pleasing to know, with an amount of car tainty, that a new works is to be established in Runcorn. At present the exact particulars are not forthcoming, but in due time these will be made known. It is anticipated that the works will employ a considerable number of men and will be financed by some of the leading men in the town Anything that will tend to brisken up the long con. tinned depression will be welcomed, figuratively, with open arms.</p>
        <p>The School Boari have wisely determined to increase their buildings in order to cope with the continued pressure on their accommodation by scholars. Victoria-road schools are to have practi- cally two additional rooms and Granville-street school is to be made int, a higher standard school. The only pity is that extensions were not made before,so that the National school managers could not have had any reasonable grounds for extending their already too large premises.</p>
        <p>Rather a paltry case was the chief business at the Frodsham Petty Sessions yesterday. A domestic servant whose only offence, really, was the taking of two grapes was actually proceeded against by her mistress for theft. Her employer would have been better advised had she simply dismissed her instead of hauling her up before " beak and bench." It was obviously a case of house discip- line and was certainly no real case for a magistrate. According to the ethics of this Puritanically-minded employer a girl who takes two grapes is to have her character blasted for life and her infamy scattered to the far corners of the earth. From all such individuals " Good L n.d, deliver us ! " A severe reprimand would have met the necessities of the case arid the ignominy of the proceedings will attach no less to the prosecutor than t 3 the defendact.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>NORTHERN UNION COUNTY</head>
        <p>NORTHERN UNION COUNTY</p>
        <p>CHAMPIONSHIP.</p>
        <p>The Strike in the Cheshire Team. TO THE EDITOR OF THE LIVERPOOL ECHO,</p>
        <p>Sir,—ln your issue of the 20th inst., there appeared a paragraph saying that the nine Runcorn players, who have been chosen to represent Cheshire in the county match at Bradford, on Saturday nest, had " struck " end were not going to play. In all fairness o the Run- corn Club and the players concerned, I beg to state that there is not a word of truth in the report, and that the whole of the Runoorn players chosen will represent their county on Saturday neat.—Yours, J ELLISON, Soc. Runcorn F.C.</p>
        <p>Oa Wednesday night the members of the Runcorn team were entertained to a hot-pat supper at Sam Houghton's Hotel, and the attitude of the players Twith regard to the match with Yorkshire was discussed. Ultimately the players decided to take part in tee game for the honour of the county, and to save lacy unpleas;- aptness. Houghton. however, will be an absentee, and. as Warder, the reserve back,:will not be available, a full back will be wanted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>EARLESTOWN &amp; NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN &amp; NEWTON.</p>
        <p>P.S.A.</p>
        <p>_ The usual weekly meeting of the P.S.A. was held in the Co-operative Hall, Earle-street, on Sunday afternoon. Mr Hatton presided over a large at tendance. An excellent address was given by Ai Walker. Solos were rendered at intervals by Mis Hall. Mrs Christendon gave several interesting recitations. Miss Williamson accompanied at the piano.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>FREE DRINKS AT NEWTON.</head>
        <p>FREE DRINKS AT NEWTON.</p>
        <p>Mr T J Gillespie (chairman), siting. at Newton Petty Sessions on Thursday, remarked that it had been brought to his notice that at Christmas time there was a Practice of giving free drinks. He hoped that be done away with, or the magis- t4Los would require the police to take note of these houses that gave them, and at the next licensing sessions ju might have a very serious effect on the granting of the licences. He hoped that warning would do away with the horrible drinking that took place at Chrisimas time. DRUNKEN BULLIES AT HAYDOCK.</p>
        <p>William Twist, of Haydock, was charged at the Newton Patty Sessions on Thursday, with drunken- ness and with assaulting Constables Carroll and Thomas on the 15ch inst. The officers stated that they were called to defendant's house, and that he struck Carroll in the chest. He was fined 203 and costs for the assault and 53 and costs for the drunkenness. Mr Gillespie said that assaults on the police were getting exceedingly common, and the magistrates were determined to put a stop to them. The police duty was arduous enough with- out assaults by a lot of drunken bullies being added to it. 1.0,G.T. HOPE OF HARLESTOWN LODGE..</p>
        <p>The weekly session of the above was held on Tuesday, November 25th, in the Co-operative Hall. Bro T E Greening, CT, presided over a good attend- ance of members. The Lodge was favoured with the presence of a visitor. Bro Corporal Newberry, lately returned from South Africa, where be had been serving with his regiment. during the late war. He was able to speak of the value of the Order, and of being very kindly received by mem- bers in that country. Bro G Clarke and E Newman voiced the congratulations of the members on bis safe return. The programme arranged by Bro C Hatton, PC T, was then rendered. Songs by Bros W Rose, C Hatton ; recitations, Bro G Clarke, Sister H Naylor ; readings, Bro J Warburton.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>-THE WATER SUPPLY AT EAR,LEsTowN.</head>
        <p>-THE WATER SUPPLY AT EAR,LEsTowN.</p>
        <p>The supply of water to the Newton and Endes- town district this week reached an acute stage, owing to a serious breakdown at the Water Works. Had it not been for the courtesy of the London and North-Western Railway Company, the inhabitants would have been in sore straits, seeing that the Council have no reserve supply. As it was in some parts of the town the people had to beg their sup- phes from more fortunate residents, and even to carry it from the Railway station premises. It will be a source of relief to the residents when a con- stant supply of water is assured, but this happy condition seems to be as far off as it was three years age when the Council decided to have their own water supply. ST JOHN'S CHURCH COMPLETION FUND—CONCERT AT EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>A variety concert in connection with the St I John's Church Completion Fund was held in the Town Hall, Earlestown, on Thursday evening. Mr V C Moss opened with a pianoforte selection " Down south," Miss Olive Roberts followed with an excellent rendering of " When the heart is young," song as an encore " Killarney," Mr Jock Halben contributed a coster song" Its a marvel 'ow e does it, but 'e does," and Mr Bert Haber gave a coon song' Little Octorcon "in good style A 1.5 cital Lasca " was given With good taste. Mr. j Higham rendered " Neath the rolling tide " splendid style and Mr Pete Halben caused much amusement by a humorous song "A happy land A number of phonograph selections by Mr T Un worth were thoroughly enjoyed as were alsa " Nursery Rhymes "by the Ilalbe.n Trio After short interval the same artistes again appeared and gve a very good account of themselves.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>NOTES BY THE WAY AT EARLES rOWN.</head>
        <p>NOTES BY THE WAY AT EARLES rOWN.</p>
        <p>[BY A CORRESPONDENT.'</p>
        <p>Whatever this desolated township is coming to, goodness only knows. The Council during the past three years have been pretending to supply the dis• trict with water, and with the help of the London and North Western Railway Company they have to a certain extent succeeded. On ocJasion, however, tne people have had to supply themselves as best they could. This week, the situation has become chronic and had it not been for the kindness of the London and North-Western Railway Company, the district would have very soon become part and parcel of the great unwashed. Water has been as precious as gold, when you could get it, and if you want to bed thirsty, you would in all probability get up in the same state and continue so, unless you found someone better situated than yourself. And yet we are expected to pay our water rents without grumbling. Supposing damage is caused to baths and boilers through this mudd- ling process, who is to be responsible? The house- holder pays for water, and the Council should see that he gets it, otherwise take the consequences.</p>
        <p>To add insult to injury they have increased the price of gas. Ido not wish to call it names, but is it really gas? One day this week, a part of the district was in semi-darkness, and I suppose in case of accident through the want of light, the Council would say they were not in fault. That, however, is a matter of opinion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>Earlestown simply walked round Bacup on Saturday. The latter never had the ghost of a chance, and the result 8 goals to I fairly represents the play. Bartley, wno had been discarded, played centre forward, and he was constantly a thorn in the side of the Bacup goal keeper. A new player named Parker, late of Stockport, partnered Stanton and he proved an acquisition. That wing would have been decidedly more danger- ous had Stanton kept further out than he did. Many splendid openings were missed in consequ- ence of this, and he would be well advised if he kept nearer the line and leave his partner to look after the inside position. Limond who seemed to have recovered from his recent injury play6cl a formidable game and repeatedly checked danger. ous onslaughts by the Bacup forwards The victory gives Earlestown a much needed lift up the League table and now that they have commenced to climb,they stand a chance of getting a little nearer the top</p>
        <p>Newton were also at home on Saturday and were successful in administering a defeat to Witton Albion. The local lads seem to be going a lille better just now, but their great Ealing is inconsis- tency.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>NEGLECT OF -CHILDREN AT</head>
        <p>NEGLECT OF -CHILDREN AT</p>
        <p>-EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>Painfnl Ravelations.</p>
        <p>At Newton Petty Sessions, on Thursday, I:)efnre Messrs T J Gillespie (chairtnan),Calonel Wool and J Una worth, Mary Davies, of Athol-street, Earlestown, was charged with neglecting her throe childron• Defendant pleaded not guilty Mr E A White prosecuted on banalf of the National Society for the prevention of Crurlty to Children, and said that in June last the case came under the notice of Inspector Thompson, of Se• trelene, who visited the house of Davies, and found the children in an ill-clad, ditty, and verminous coalition. D - fendant and eight obiliren, together with a man with whom she was cohabiting, were living in a house in Haydock. They after wards removed to Earlestown, and Inspector Hackett visited them, and found the house there fin a wretchedly poor condition. There was one living room, with two hr iken tables and a box and a few cooking utensils. Upstairs there was only one flock mattress and an iron bedstead, with only one quilt to covrr it. The boy Joseph, aged twelve,was badly clothed, and in a ragged and filthy condition; William, aged eight, had no shoes or stockings, and was verminons ; and the child Peter, aged four, was also very verminous, and without shoes or stockings. Evidence was given by Inspector Hackett, Constable Mellor, Esrlestown,and Constable Dagger, Haydock. The Chairman described the case as ono of the worst eases that had come before the bench. Prisoner Would have to go to prison for two months' hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>RUNCORN SCHOOL BOARD.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN SCHOOL BOARD.</p>
        <p>ST JOHN'S CHURCH_ COMPLETION FUND.</p>
        <p>A social dance in aid of this fund was given by the young ladies' Bible class of St John's Church,in the Mission Ball, Legh-street, on Monday evening. There were about 40 couples present. Dancing was commenced to the strains of Mr Sudworth's band at &amp;o'clock and kept up till a late hour. Refresh- ments were provided at intervals.</p>
        <p>. _</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the above authority was hold on Tuesday evening. Present :—Messrs J Pritchard (in the chair), Canon Wood, F J Norman, E Imison, F Taylor, W Handley J Littler, with Messrs J W Light- burn (clerk), and A Lightburn (assistant clerk).</p>
        <p>- - PROPOSED INCREASED ACCOMMODATION AT BOARD SCHOOLS.</p>
        <p>a The School ManageinentiCommitteetreported that owing to alteration in the modeofipaying the granta,the salaries of Mr J F Moulton, head teacher at the Victoria-road Boys' School, and Miss Alice Kilburn were re-arranged. ' In consequence of the scholastic qualifications of the eertificeted assistant teachers net having been supplied as requested, the Committee were unable to consider the question of the revision of th dr scale of salaries. The salaries of assistant teachers in the evening school hav- ing been an recently determined the Committee could not reconsider the applicati in of Mr Parr at so early a date. A letter was read from the Rev J W Mold, of Frodsham, asking for an aopeintment for ilia daughter as a pupil teacher. The Clerk was instrueted to inform him that there was no vacancy at the present time but that his application should be registered. Aclounts amounting to £236 153 21 were examined and peed for payment. A special meeting of the committee was held in the Victoria-road school on the 20th inst, Mr Handley presiding. It was recommended that fair Wilding be instructed to prepare plans and specification for the erection of a classroom and stair case over tne Class-rooms at the end of the boys' department, also a class-room in the girls' yard where the covered play ground now stands, both at Victoria-road schools. Mr Handley, in proposing the confirmation of the minutes, said that the salaries proposed to be given to the teachers were not an increase in any sense. The basis on which they were paid had hitherto been by certain percentages, and the Committee thought it better to put the salaries on a definite basis which would aotually come to the amounts they had hitherto received without having to make any calculations. With respect to the assistant teachers, they had been asked to furnish their college qualifications, etc., as it was desir- able to recegnis e ability rAther than by the one block system scsale of salaries according to the position held in the sehool. There had been some mit- understanding about the matter, but their qualifi- cations would be considered at a future meeting. For some time they had been warned by the Inspectors and also by the Education Department that they had not sufficlent accommodation for the scholars wishful to attend or who were in attendance. This had become so acute that they had even been , threatened with stoppage of the education grant. 'Returns showed thet every aeriel in the t awn with the exception of the infants had more on the rolls than could he found accommodation for. The national boys had only room for one, and in the girls three. Trinity schools, one. The other schools were infant schools and their own Board Schools were very full. They had 444 certificated accommedation for the boys and 570 on the rolls, in the girls 380 and 477 on the rolls. They thus I had to provide other accommodation. At the Victoria- road there were 36 boys attending more than they had room for, and 34 in the girls. To meet the situation they proposed to raise the walls of a one story building which had been class rooms at the Victoria. road school. The roof would thus be utilised and they would hold the same number as below —namely 64. At the girls side was a covered play ground on the roof of which they proposed to build a recital which would seat 77 girls. At the Granville road they had certified eccommolarion for 285 but the pupil teachers centre had interfered with the numbers. The transference to another room of this centre would give them the full number of 285 so that some 25 could be transferred from the Victoria-road to Granville-street which could be made into a higher standard school. Mr Wilding had been instructed to prepare the plans which would be ready shertly. Mr F J Norman seconded and the rnieutes were ap- proved. This was all the business.</p>
        <p>Spiritualist Society. With the objct ot augmenting the building fund , a concert was held in the Spiritualist Hall, Bewsey- street on Saturday evening. The programme included songs by the Misses M. Bate and McCollom, Master R McCollom, Messrs B. Foster and Stout; a recitation by Miss Annie Furness, violin duets by Messrs L Edwards and Sydney; quartettes, "The Hardy Norsemen," and" Killarney," by Miss Annie Furness and Messrs W Massey, Pryce-Jones, and II Foster, a sketch entitled, " Henpecked," which created great amusement, in which the characters were taken by Misses Ada and Annie Furness and Nellie Carlisle, and Messrs Joseph and , Herbert Foster ; song, Soldiers in the Park," the solo being taken by Miss Annie Furness, and the chorus by the Lyceum Glee Party, who concluded the programme with a well executed glee " dood-night," The chair was occupied by Mr E Lewis and Mr Joseph Foster and Miss Ada Furness played the accompaniments B HST BOOTS FOR ALL.—UNDER TAR aLOOK.—Advt , UNDER THE CLOOW FOR QOOD DOOM</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>EARLESTOWN BAP FIST CHUEtCH. The anniversary services in connection with this church were preached on Sunday last. Toe Rev A H West, 8.A., of Sabden, conducted both morning and evening services. On Monday evening a public meet- ing was held in the chapel; The Rev S D Thomas pre- siding over a large attendance. Au excellent address on " The Cultivation of Spiritual life" was given by the Rev G R Green of Fabius Chapel, Liverpool. The Rev .1 L Matthews,of Latchford also spoke on " The relation of Christian Endeavour to the Church,''and the Rev A H West on " The obligation of Church membership." Special music was Pandered by the noir at both services. Collections were taken in aid of the choir funds.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>WYOLIPFE P.S. A. SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>WYOLIPFE P.S. A. SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>A. well-attended meeting in connection with this society was held on Sunday afternoon. Mr Smith, of St Helens, presided and spoke a few encouriging words on Brotherhood. Mr Blain, of Timper,ey, deiivered an address in which he re! erred to some of the social con- ditions of life and how they might be remedied. The President (Rev Jilin Yoage) led tee devotional part of the service. It was announced that two new members had jcined. An organ soh wiA onntribit.A oy Mr WEI Payton, Mus Bac. and Mr Do wnes,oue of the lion secretaries read the I Nioo.</p>
        <p>SUNDAY OBSERVANCE-9ERNION BY TRE REV .</p>
        <p>MORLEY STEVENSON.</p>
        <p>The Rev Morley Stevenson preached a sermon at St Paul's COUtOh on Sunday morning on "Sunday observ- ance." He said that durine the past 50 years great I change had come over the weenie of toe country in the matter of the observance of the Lore's Day. I eves in- structive to cempare the Courch census taken in Liver- ' pool 11 years ago with the one taken during the present year. E'even years ago out of every thousand people ire Liverpool on a particular Sunday 126 attended place of worship ; this year the number had fallen to 102, making allowances for the sick, infirm, and chil- dren ; yet in proportion to the incresse of the population there had been a decrease in attendance to Divine worship. After all deductions had been made the attendance was very small. There had been a great increase in devoting the day to amueerneat and pleasure, and that was partly owing to the greater facilities for travel. Tne preeceer peeeeeded to discuas the question as to how far the Seboatti day should be regarded as a day of abstineace from pleasure or as a day of some kind of recreation. He did not believe it was possible, nor was it desirable, they should lay down a hard and fast rule for everyene, as the circumstances of the people differed very much, and they must get for themselves a right .insight int, the principles whieh should guide them in the observance of the day ; and then they could apply these principles to their own particular cases. Some people seemed to think that the fourth Commandment was not binding upon us in these days, but it was quite as binding as the other Com- mandments. If one was not biading why Phould the others be? The preacher laid down three great points , for the congregation to consider, namely, the principle :of worship, the principle of rose, and the principle of service—worship a duty to G ad, rest a duty to self, and service a duty to their neigatimr. Tuley did not spend the Lord's Day as they ought to spend it if they neglected the worship of G ad, as that was the great pure° ie of the day. It was the ignorance of that principle which had eeten out the heart of attendance in God's house. It had been most conclusively proved that people who rested one day in seven did mere work in a certain number of years than people who worked seven days a week and did not rest. On the question of service, the rev. gentlemal said it was a bad thing far a men never to be able te think about his own circle or the thousand little kindnesses which went to mak, up the sunshine of a Caiatian home. If it svcr e not for Sun- day many people woald never b, able t came into centact with their own people, to Say nothing of other people, their neighbours. Sunday was a day for friendships and if they did things on a Sunday which robbed other people of their Sunday, where did their duty to their neighbours come in ? The rich people were the greatest sinners irt this respect, especially in the South and in London, by giving up the day Co amueement,by having parties and so on, chug making Suesay the hardest day of the week for their servants. Lag railway journeys on a Sunday in search of rest and recreation aerie c an. demned, and in conclusion the preacher said we were now at the parting of the wave. The old order was changing, and they did not quite see what the new was going to be. A great responsibility rested upon this generation to make a firm stand to keep the old Eaglie Sunday which the foreigner coveted an I envied us o CHOIR FESTIVAL AT BOLD STREET WESLEYI CHURCH.</p>
        <p>There were large congregations st Bold-street Wesleyan Church on Sunday, when the annual choir festival was held. As usual the musical portion of the services was a special feature, and tee members of the choir gay, evidence of the efficiency which they have attained under the direction oi' the able oreanist, Mr Frenk Hickman. The preacher at the morning and evening services was the Bay G H Csrasoa,who delivered appro- priat3 discourses. Tne morning service opened with the organ selections "Grand chorus in D " (Lemmees) " Andante cantabile" (Wider) and " Minuet " (Clau4- mann),which was rendered in a highly creditable manner by Mr Frank Hickman. The choir very sweetly sang Spine's! " Sanctus " and also readered the anthem " I waited for the Lord " (Ilendeiss ihn'e Hymn of Praise) with geed effect. Mr BEI Pearson contributed a violin solo " Serenata " (ilozkowski) with his customary taste and skill. In the afternoon a musical service was hell, the Rev G H Cameo's presiding in the absence of Mr John E Richar&amp;on.'An organ recital was a'aivcie /by Mr Thomas Lane, organist at Littlettorough Parish Church, with marked %batty. His selections were :—" Grand fantasia and Fuge in G minor" (Bach) ; foe ques- tion," and " Tee answer" (Wolstenholme), " Suuset melody " (Vicent), and " Grand chorus " (Hollins). Mass Lucy Davies gave feeling interpretations " Behold ! I stand at the door " (Jude) and " Light in Darkness" (Cowen). Mr Edward Pownall sang " Arm, arm ye brave" (Handel) with much spirit At the evening service the choir again rendered " Sanctus " end also the anthems " List ! the cherubic host " (G and " The day Thoegavest " (Woodward,. Mr Hick, man played as organ seleetiona " Andantino in D flit " (Damara) and " Fuge in C minor'! (Mendelssohn). The festival was brought to a close on Monday even- ing., when a very large audience assembled to hear the choir, which had been augmented for the occasion, render the weed cantata "The Holy City" (AR Gaul). An orchestra was in attendance, and rendered valuable service. Mr R A Naylor presided. The Chairman expressed the sincere gratification which it afforded him to take the chair at ene of their annual musical festivals. 113 had far a few years taken an intereet in the musical part of the services held Sun- day after Sunday in Bold-street chapel, owing to the recemmendation which he: gave thole organist when lie applied for the important position, which he thought they waul 1 all agree, he had eceepied most efficiently. Mr Frank Hickman was a rising musician, arid he (air :Naylor) noticed some years age the enthusiasm which Ihe displayed. Continuing Mr age, offered his sincere congratulations upon the gathering together of such MI I array of talent, and said they were much obliged the to ladies and gentlemen who had come for yard and I aFeociatedthecnselves in that eff art for the pleasure and benefit of the audience. In c Inclusion the speaker made I an allusion to the death of the Rev High Hughes, and and his remark will be Colloid in another column. Dr Gaul's beautiful work was rendered in a very 1 praiseworthy manner, under the conductorship of Mr W H Payton, Mlle. Bee. The choir and instrumentalists acquitted themselves admirably, and frequent epol t'l99 rewarded their efforts. The soloists were Mrs A Booth (soprano), Miss E Bawling, Blackpool, (contralto), Mr . Arthur Griffiths (tenor), and Mr vv H Wallington (bass) Mrs Booth tastefully sang " These are they which came," rine Miss Bawling was warmly anal tuded for a creditable rendition of " Eye bath not seen." Mr Griffiths' pure-toned voice was heard to advantage in My soul is athirst for GA," Mr Wallington effectively gang " A new heaven and a new earth," a pleasing feature being the rendering at intervals of the " Sanctus" by the choir. Mr Wallington else contri- bated the solo " I heard the voice of the harpers." The *Voices of Mrs Booth, Miss Taylor and Miss Bawling blended very sweetly in the trio "At eventide it shall be light. A meritorious interpretation of the quartette" List, th eherubic host," was given by Miss Bar'ow, Mrs Booth Miss Taylor and MiB3 Davies. All the choruses received capable treatment, tan concluding one " Great and marvellema are Thy Work; "--aeing especially effective. The members of the orchestra must be complimented upon the manner in which they discharged their duties ; the selections " Contemplation " and "Adoration" being rendered with taste and careful attention to expressinn. The instrumentalists were ;—lat violins, Mr Rll Pearson, , leader Mr E H Clare, Mr H Bennett ; 2nd Violins, N.Tr A Knowles, Mr W Brown, Mr T Wallington ; viola, Mr Johnson ; 'cello, Mr H Bond ; double bass, Mr R Whitfield ; harp, Mr T Ford ; flute, Mr Ray; oboe, Mr Davies ; horn, Mr R Lowe : betsoon,Mr W Thornton; clarionet, Mr Hipkiss ; cornet, Mr T Mannion ; trom- bone, Mr E Smith. Mr Frank Hickman presid-ul at the organ. Mr G Weaver nranosed a vote of thank; to the Chair- man and SSid Mr Naylor was always willing to render any setv lees that would be helpful to any denomination. He was the friend of all denominations. They very much appreciated what hie had said in reference to Mr Hickman. Mr Weaver also thanked all who had taken part in the cantata and said that there were r^preseata- tives of nearly every denoMnation. By comine, there they showed kindly spirit towards and interest to t tie work of that church.</p>
        <p>Mr Holland seconded. Mr N.ylor acknnarledird ttin c-nupliment and paid a tribute to ths sorvicee of the choir and ~rchestra.</p>
        <p>Th 9 proveling3 conehile3 with the singing of the Doxology.</p>
        <p>TABLE &amp; HANGING LAMPS.—J. S. GARLAND, 51, 53, &amp; 55 Bridge-street, IV arrington.—Advt.</p>
        <p>LONDON POISONING MYSTERY.</p>
