      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>TREFT OF GRAPES.</p>
        <p>At the Frodsham Petty Session, on Wednesday, before Mr J Murray (in the chair) and Mr J B Mortimer, Emma Davies, domestic, was charged with stealing a quantity of grapes, value 2s, the property of her mistress, Miss Uhristine Gardner, on the 6th November.—Mr Linaker prosecuted.— From the opening statement it appeared that from time to time grapes had been missed from the greenhouse, and so instructions had been given to the gardener to watch the place. On the date mentioned in the forenoon he saw the defendant take some grapes. The gardener wept and charged her with having stolen them and also with having stolen others previously. In reply defendant said she only took two and had never taken any beforeibut itnotherservant had. Mr Linker explained that the proceedings were instituted to clear the other girls character, and to prevent a recurrence of petty larceny.—Emma Robertson, cook, said that defendant had admitted to her tak- ing a few grapes.—Other evidence was given and the defendant was then charged with doing wilful damage to the greenhouse.—The evidence showed that on the day in question defendant had left her scissors and had broken a window in trying to recover them. She afterwards went to the gardener and told him she would break every window if he would not let her get them back.—{r Winker said he did not wish to press the case and the defendant wh:, sobbed bitterly during the case,was bound over under the First Offenders' Act. THE LATE Miss BARCLAY'.</p>
        <p>At the Runcorn Parish Church last Sunday morn- ing the Vicar alluded to the demise of Miss Barclay He said her father had been for nearly 40 years Vicar of the parish, and it was natural that they should take an interest in any of his family, and especially in the one who had just passed away. She was for many years superint,ndent of the girl's Sunday school, was intimately connected with many of the charities and when her father was taken with his illness she was his right hand, as was evinced by the fact that many of the records and minutes of the meetings were in her writing. She was emphatically a good daughter to him. For 16 years, since she left this place, she had taken a deep interest in its welfare, and each of their churches, St. Michael's, Weston, and their own Parish Church would bear, perhaps for centuries, the mark of her generosity. The splen- did reredos and altar rails in the Parish Church were her gift, out of regard for the work of the church and also for the memory of one who was so dear to her. Her death had appeared to them very sudden,but he was informed that for two years she had herself been aware that she was likely to be taken away suddenly, and her household accounts and also those of any work with which she was connected were in the most perfect order at her death. At the close of the service Mr F R Boraston played Chopin's Funeral March, and the hymns of the service were appropriate to the occasion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>TRIiF4T BY A SAILOR.</head>
        <p>TRIiF4T BY A SAILOR.</p>
        <p>At the Runcorn Police Court on Wednesday,before Mr F J Norman and E Imisou, a man named David Avail Jones was charged with having stolen £2 103 belonging to Robert Harris and £3 belonging to William Green from a schooner named "Mary Millington" on the 20th November,—Mr Green, of London, stated that he was the manager of the schooner, which was at the time of the robbery lying at Weston Point, but was now at Runcorn. The defendant was acting as cook. Witness was keeping £2 lOs for Harris, while £3 23 8d was his own money. He placed both sums of money in the pockets of his belt which was in his cabin. At about two o'clock on the day in question defendant asked permission to go ashore in order that he might post a letter. Witness let him go. Soon after- wards Harris asked him for some money in order to pay for his boots that had been mended. Witness went into the cabin to fetch the money but found that it had gone. The door was open but when witness left it, it was locked. The lock had been forced but the door was not damaged. All the gold had disappeared and there was only 2s 64 left. Harris went after the prisoner and gave informa- tion to the police.—Robert Harris, sailor on the schooner, corroborated. —Superintendent Ennion stated that as soon as he received information about the robbery he went to the railway station and searched the rooms there. The prisoner entered the station and went straight to the booking office. Witness was told that he was the suspected thief so he arrested him. Witness brought him to the Police Station and searched him. He had in his possession .E 3 193 33 in money, and Is worth of stamps. He was wearing the clothes produced, and had the ones he was now wearing in a bundle. He had spent .E 1 95 in buying the clothes. When .witness charged him he made no reply. Prisoner pleaded guilty.—Mr Norman said there were previous convictions against the prisoner. They would have to put a stop to his thieving, so he would be sent to prison for three months with hard labour,_</p>
        <p>DISHEARTENING' WINTlat PitosPECT AT EARLESTOWN. There is every prospect that there are bad times in store for many of the workmen and their families in Earlesto wn this winter at the Viaduct .works which isone of the principal works in the.dis- trict the men are not working full tima.insome of the departments, and for the past week or two many of the men have only been employed five days a week. This will make a difference in their earnings and Consequently the outlook is not very promising'.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>CRAWFORD'S</head>
        <p>CRAWFORD'S</p>
        <p>"LOTHIAN"</p>
        <p>SIORTBREAD.</p>
        <p>The Second Mrs. Ohapman. ---- Body Exhumed at Lymm,</p>
        <p>The exhumation of the remains of Mrs Bessie Chap- man, the second wife of Georga Chapm in, who stands charged with the murder of Maud Eliza Marih, took place on Saturday morning at Lymm Churchyard' A force of a dozen constables was posted at the entrance to the churchyard from an early hour but s) successful had the police been in their efforts to keep the exhumation seent that there was but a small gathering of people round the churchyard whea Ih3 solemn ceremony took place. The grave in which the secoad Mr 3 Chapman lay beneath her mother and father was surrounded by a screen of white sheets, and about five o'clock in the morning three stalwart gravediggers comnvoced the task of exhumation with the aid of lanteras. The morn- ing was bitterly cold, and the ground WAS froz 311 t) a depth of several inches. The work of bringing the three coffi as to the surface was accomplished at eignt o'clock, and the three coffins were ranged in a row by the side of the grave. At ten o'clock Dr Stevenson, expert to the Home Office, drove up to the graveyard in a carriage, accom- panied by two asiistancs. The trio immediately pro- ceeded to a temporary mortuary, the sex ton's hut at the far end of the cemetery, for the purpose of the post- mortem examination, and the coffia containing the remains of Mrs Bessie Chapman was reverently carried into this structure.</p>
        <p>At this time those present in the cemetery were Dr Stevenson, his two assistants, the Rev G B Thu,stoa, Rector of Lyme), D3teotive-laspector Godley, of the M (Southwark) division of police, who has charge of the investigation; Mr W Tule, an assistant undertaker to Mr A Smith, of Southwark Bridge-road (who had been ordered by the Home Office to be present in order to identity the plate ,upon the coffin), two brothers of Bessie Chapman, aril the husband uf Bessie Chapmsn's sister.</p>
        <p>Mr Tulle, at the request of the detective officer, scraped the dirt off the plate on the coffin, and displayed I the simple inscription recording the death of Bessie Chaplain in February 1901, and at once proceeded to unscrew the lid of the coffin. The body was found to be in a fair condition, and was perfectly recognisable, and the two brothers immediately identified it, in spite of the fact that the remains had been interred for a year and ten months. No time was lost in proceeding with the task of the post mortam, and under the personal direction and supervision of Dr Stevenson, the two assistants removed the chief organs of the bo iy,and care- fully placed them in squafe glass jars, which were duly sealed and labelled. 'lee various jars were placed into a large box, which was in its turn seale I, and removed under the ever-watchful eye of Dr Stevenson to his carriage. The famous medical expert and his two com— panions were driven off to Lymrn railway station, and after changing carriages at Harrington, the trio c veved the sealed box from Easton to Guy's Hospital. Whilst the post-mortem ex araination was proceeding the reinterment of Mr and Mrs Taylor took place, the Rector reading the service. The examination concluded, the remains of Bessie Chapman were replace i in the coffin, and reinterred, th I Rector again officiating. Tne three gravediggers then filled in the grave, and tate stone was carefully replaced.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>Inquest on Maud Marsh. Result of Post-mortem Doctors Swear to Arsenical Poisoning.</head>
        <p>The Inquest on Maud Marsh. Result of Post-mortem Doctors Swear to Arsenical Poisoning.</p>
        <p>Dr Waldo on Tuesday sat for the fifth time at South- wark in connection with his inquiry let) the mystery surrounding the death of Maud Eliz ,bath Marsh, bar- maid of the" Crown " public-house. ..George Chapman, landlord of the " Crown," who is alleged to have caused her death, was agai a present. Louisa Beatrice Cole said she went as a general servant to the " Crown" about four months ago. One Satur- day night, absut three weeks before the death of Miss Mash, witaess bought a rabbit, and the followin; morning she cooked it and served it upstairs. Chapman, Mi3EI Marsh and witness partook of it. This was at first supposed to bay. caused Miss Marsh's illness, but wit- ness said that neither Miss Marsh nor Chapman 00M- plained of the rabbit, and witness suffered no ill eff She alone prepared all the meals. Chapman used to prepare Miss Marsh's medicine in the kiccnen. The '.l,roner : When he cemi into the kitchen where did you go? Witness : I went down into the bar. Flow long would you be in the b r ?—Five minutes to half an hour.</p>
        <p>Have you ever seen in the kitchen a vessel contain- ing white liquid ?—Yes, I saw Mr Chapman with two glasses, one containing a white stuff like milk and one was empty. Wnat did you see him do ?—He mixed it fr ma one glass into the other. Who took the glasses away ?-1 suppose the governor, Mr James Henry Tar4e:t, surgeon of Guy's Eio-:pital, , who had Miss Marsh under his care for thr-:e weeks at ' the hospital, said she became a pst'ent 0.1 the 26th of July, and left on the 20:h August. Miss Marsh told him that she had been married ten months, and said she had been seized with violent pains in the abdomen thne weeks previously. The patient was occasionally sick, but it was never an important symptom. Opiates had to be administered to rene,ve the pain.and her temp, ra- ture during the first fortnight was 102 and 103. Fr nu 10th August Miss Marsh gradually got. b Tee Coroner : Did you form any opinion as to the nature of the illness ?</p>
        <p>. Witness : I thought at first she hid peritonitiq. What conclusion did you arrive at later ?—Seeing there was no ciuse for the peritonitis, we thought it might possibly be a case of consumption. Did you ever suspect an irritant poison P—Not in the slightest. Dr Jamei M irrie Stoker, the dottor who attended Miss Marsh at the "Crown," said Chapman called on nina on OA ober 10,and told him Mrs Chapman(Misi Marsh) was suffering from diarrhea i and vomiting. After visiting Miss Marsh several drive Dr Stoker suggested that she had better go into hospital, but she began to cry-, gni said she did not want to go into the hoipital again. Ile attended deceased till her death, of which he waq told by Chapman. ELI did not know she was dead till he got to the "Crown." The Coroner : Was her death unexpected P—lt was. I did not expect it.</p>
        <p>U e nld Chapinta he shorOd like a post-mortem examination, and he sidln did not see the use, addiag that she died nf .xtutpitiou CALISPI by diarrtice I and vomitiog. Eveatuslly CbipmAn cmsented to a vast- mor tem.</p>
        <p>Witness said he remov/d the stomach and Dor of other organs, and Ook nem to the enemies' Research Association, in Southwark-streat, and asked them to test for irritant poisoning. Toe Coroner :Dd yen susgest any particular poison?</p>
        <p>, Witness : I suggested arsenic. When did you first sumect your patient had takim poison P—l cannot: say I had any reason to suspect until I had made a post-mortem. Why did you make a post-mortem P—Becadioe I could not find out the cause of death. I had a saspicion tuat thcre was something before I stared the post-mortem, b-cause Dr Grapple, of Croydon, had wired to me " Look out for arsenic at the P.M. t.p-night." I The following day he received the result of the I Chemical Research Association's analysis, which stated that the atointei and contents contained in appreciable amount of arsenic.</p>
        <p>Chapman told him he was not married to deceased, but that she was living with him as his wife. Witness gave deceased no arsenic in her medicine. As long as deceased took no food she did not voini,, tuc when she took food the attacks came on again. He did not call Chapman's attention t the similarity of the symptoms in the case of his second wife and Miss Marsh.</p>
        <p>Witness list became auriainted with Chapman con- sequent upon his attendance on his wife, who died on the 13`.,h February, 1901, at the " Monument " pnblic- house. Her symptoms were constipation, abdominal pains, dierrhoe 1, and uncontrollable vomiting. He had three different consultations over her. Be gave the death certificate in the case, and described the cause of death as internal obstruction, vomiting, and exhaustion. The symptoms in each case were so, much alike as to I excite his suspicion. Dr F U Grappel, of West Cr.)ydon, said the father of deceased asked him to see lois daughter on October 21 last. Witness described Ddiag Marsh's condition, and said his opinion was that deceased was suffering from poisoning, and he first thought from ptomaine, through eating the rabbit. Afterwards, when thinking over the matter, he became convinced twit the case was one of arsenics' poisoning. The inquest was adjourned to December 13.</p>
        <p>Warrington Field Club. " The dispersal of seeds" was the subject of an interesting paper read at the fourth meeting of the current session of this club by Mr A Deane,assistant curator, at the Museum. Starting with the thesis that the object of every adult organism is reproduc- tion, he proceeded to describe the various kinds ot devices by which in different groups of plants this object and the preservation of the species is secured. Flung with explosive violence from drying pods, floated in runnel or river, wafted by breezes or ocean currents, clinging to the muddy feet or hooked into the feathers, hair or skin of birds and 1 mammals, in these and other ways seeds pass from the parent plants to the soil in which they are to germinate. Mr Deane illustrated his subject by diagrams and with a profusion of beautifully pre- served and prepared specimens In the discussfon which followed,though his preliminary assumption was challenged, and one or two details were adversely criticised, the lecturer was warmly and deservedly congratulated on the skill and clearnesswith which he had arranged and delivered his address. IH. Al. ASHTON'S LIFT TONIu is TER KINO. OF IONICS.-63, SANSICEY-BTREST, Warrington.—(Advt).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>GILCHRIST LECTURES AT EARLESTOWN.</head>
        <p>GILCHRIST LECTURES AT EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>In consequence of the Gilchrist Trustees having offered another course of lectures to the Newton-in-Makerfield , district for the coming season, a public meeting was held lathe Town Hall, Esrlestow,, on Wednesday even- ing, for the purpose of electing a representativeoCom- mi‘tee in accordance with the conditions of the Trust. Mr C B F Borron presided but there was not a large attendance. The Chairman said he had been asked by the committee which was elected two years ago, to , occupy the chair that evening. Toe object of the meet- ing was to elect a committee because the Gilchrist Trustees bad honoured the district with a course of lectures to be held there this year. One of the con- ditions of that offer is, that a public meeting be held so that a representative committee should be appointed. The Gilchrist Trustees granted them some six years ago a course of lectures free to the district,vp Weft were really preliminary lectures. Since then several courses of lectures bad been held, and they had been succ2ssfully carried on, and had met with the approbation of the district. The st:cond course which the Trustees granted to the district, the Committee had to pay the sum of EP. Now they were so satisfactorily placed and had grown so formidable that they asked them now ,to pay 30 guineas. _</p>
        <p>The meeting then proceeded with the election of a Committee. The Right Hoo Lord Newton was appointed president ; Colonel R Pilkington, M P, vice-president ; Mr C B F Sorron, chairman of committee ; Mr R Stone, J P, vice-chairman ; Dr Hacdyside, chairman ; bon sec, Mr T Ormond. In the election of committee, Mr Gilmour said it was essential they should have a good working co.ninA;ee. They did not want drones such as were OD the I,st committee, who liked to see their names in print, but never did any work. Dr Latham supported what Mr Gilmour arid. They had mernbars on the committees who neither attended the meeting nor bought tickets for the lectures. It was a shame and be suggested that they should not be in— cluded on the committee. . _</p>
        <p>Mr C W Emmett proposed that the committee b 3 limited to 40 members, that tbe Viaduct. Works, McCor- quodale and Co, and Vulcan Foundry have each 3 representatives, the other works to have 2 each. This was catried. _ _</p>
        <p>The following were elected Messrs P Aspinsll, W Parks and W Speakman, as representing McCorquadale and Co's works' T Cane jan, and T Elaselden (Sankey 'Sugar Co), Dr Forrester and G Goff (Co-op.rative Society), T Bolton, W Clare and J Gooding (Viaduct Works), C H Bodmer, D Barry and W Braminer(Vulcan Foundry Co); J Sin and .1 Shepherd (Sloe and Co), P Parr and W Wilkinson (rand T Vicars), J Brodie and R Barton (Gill.spie and Co), J Rylance jun (Borron and Co), J 11 Profit and T Molyneux (Y M C A), Councillors Emmett, Collingwood, Latham,Thompsoo and Simpson, Messrs J. Allan, W Davies, H B Schofield, G Bunting and C W Emmett (Viaduct Inititnte Committee), Drs Patter- son, W Watkios and Valentine, Messrs T Parr, C Cole, Sandiford, T E Clough, E Goalie, A Clarke, G Burgess, A Brown, R Chandler, J Singleton, G Dring, and the Clergy of the district. Messrs P Pa er and W H Parks were appointed auditors. A vote of thanks to the Chairman, terminated the proceedings.</p>
        <p>The followin,7 are the lecturers and the subject 3 fur the course :—January 19 h, Rev W fi D danger, L L D, D C L, " Ants : a Stady of Sociolo zy and Politics amongst Insec:s." February 2ad Dr Andrew F RS S, " Blue and Muscle." February 16:6, Hugh Robert Mill, DSc (Edin) LID " Weathrr and Weather Forecasting." March 2ad,Ales Hill,M A,M D, " The Brain as the apnaratus of Mind." Pdarch 16 Lb, Charles Waldstein Litt D, Pu D it 3, "Labour and Art in English Life ; ill astrated by Greek Art'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>EMMANUEL CHURCH, WARRINGTON. -- Annual Sale of Work.</head>
        <p>EMMANUEL CHURCH, WARRINGTON. -- Annual Sale of Work.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday and Thursday and again yester- day, the annual sale of work in aid of the general expenses of the Emmanuel Cnurch (Reformed Episcopal), Bold-street, WAS held in the schoolroom over Arpley Station. A number of useful and ornamental articles were offered for sale. There were six stalls in all and they were in charge of the following : CONGREGATIONAL STALL :—lits N3rman and friends. . _</p>
        <p>CHRISTIAN ENDEAVOUR AND CHILDREN'S STALL: —Miss Booth, Mr W Atherton and friends. FLOWER AND FRUIT STALL :—Mrs Smithson and friends. SUNDAY SCHOOL STALL : —Mrs A Gandy and friends. MEN'S STALL:—Mr W Dicken4, Young Men's Bible Class and friends.</p>
        <p>REFRESHMIINT STALL :—Miss Lees, Mrs El I.ondon and Miss Mason, TEA RO3ll : —Ars H Gully, Mrs Allen, Mrs Minards, Mrs Hallwood and friends. .</p>
        <p>In a circular appealing for financial assistance, the Vicar (Rev A D Gee) and the secretaries (Mr A Landon and Mr W H Dickens; state that "the need of a movement like that of the Reformed Episcopal Church is greater now than ever it was. roe rapid growth of the ritualistic and sacerdotal movement gives much cause f3r alarm to all who take their stand by the sound Protestant principles of the Reformers. Our Church is a refuge for those who are driven from the Established Church by the growth of ritualism and sacerd)talisno, and who love the old-fashioned C.-lurch liturgy in its simplicity and purity. We do not make the protest in any spirit of bitterness, yet wejeel that if the work accomplished by the Reformers, at so great a cost, is not to be made void, then a strong stand must be taken by all lovers of a purely Evangelical faith. But not only does our Church exist to make a protest. We desire intercommunion with all who love our Lord and Saviout Jesus Christ in sincerity and in truth ; and above all, we desire to bring the unconverted into a saving knowledge of Christ's ' Gospel. A work like this cannot be carried on without financial aid. Our Church is supported by the voluntary contributions of the Congregation, which is not a wesltay one,and therefore an annual sale of work is a necessity. Money is needed for the maintenance of our Church services, Sunday schools, and other agencies at work." The opening ceremony was performed by Mr J. Darlington, of Liverpool, on Wednesday afternoon. The Rev A G D Gee presided. There was an encouraging attendance ; amongst those present being the .Revs John Yonge, J S Hughes, J F Matthews and J L Prayer was offered by the R 3,7 John Yonge and the Rev A G I) Gee briefly introduced Mr Darling- ton, who expressed his pleasure at being present and in assisting the work. Mr Atherton read a list of donations amounting to £4 19/. The subscribers included the Rev Venahles Green, a miAsionary of the Reformed Episcopal Church in India. The opening ceremony concluded with a vote of thanks to Mr Darlington, which was moved by Mr Atherton and seconded by Mr Dickens.</p>
