      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES</head>
        <p>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES</p>
        <p>IMPRISONMENT FOR CRUELTY TO A HORSE AT TNELWALL William Welland. of Iforkshire-st,liet, Salford. was coarged at the Altaicliam Pollee Court on Saturday, with cruelly ill-treating a horse at The!wall on October 26th.—Vergeaot sharp stated than on the day in question he met prisoner coming from the direction of Warrington with A horse. The animal was extremely lame in all its limbs. It was only fit for " knacker "purposes. Prisoner said he was oa the way to Swinton, in order that he might put the horse out to rex* at a farm. ii• wanted to "try to get him up and get a little out of him" Sharp ordared the animal to be stalled at a neighbouring farm, and It had remained there since Inspector Lucas, of the 0 .1.. who prosecuted. also spoke te the lame cendition in which the animal had been.— Prisoner said the animal fell lame ON the road. He had bought the horse for £3 les, and one did not give that price for an animal only St for the " knacker's ' yard.—The Chairman said the Benoit considered the prisoner had been guilty of gross eruelty. He was accustomed to h,rses, and he must have been aware of the condition that the animal woe In. He would be econnsitted for one meath's hard labor.</p>
        <p>IMPUDENT ROBBERY /N I WARRINGTON LODGING ROUSE— STRUGGLE IN A BEDROOM At the Warrington Pollee Court on Tuesday before Alder- man Sutton (presiding) and other magistrates Edward Sauce, Ship Yard, was charged with stealing a sliver watch and guard and £1 7s 2d in sash, the property of John Cleary between 11 o'elook on Sunday night at 7.30 on Monday morn- ing —Cho Chief Constable said the prosecutor, who was a farm labourer, went to lodge at Caveny's lodging house In Ship Yard. He retired to rest at 11 p.m. on Sunday night and °mauled a room along with several other men whom he did not knew. Before going te bed he bung his vest which contained the watch and guard at the fo,t of the bed, and placed a bag containing £1 3s 2d under his pillow. When he had been in bed a short time he saw the prisoner get out of another bed, walk te the foot of his (proseeutor's) bed, and , take the watch and guard frem the vest. Prosecutor said " Pat that watch down. It is not your's," Prisoner said, in a threatening way " I shall do nothing of the kind." Clary said he should get up and Inform tlao pollee, and prisoner immediately became violent and rushing at ths prisoner said " You shan't leave this room," Prosec itor held his peace until morning when he informed the police. It appeared that daring the struggle between prosecutor and the prisoner. Sauce bed abstraeted the bag containing the cash from under prosecutor's pillow. Superintendent Venaghan and Betectlve Barlow saw the prisoner on Cropper's Brow on Monday and the superintendent told him what they " wanted " him for. ere replied that he had no watch, but the Superintendent told him that it had been seen in his possession a short time ago and he would have to go to the Police Station. Prisoner then said a an gave him the watch. On the way te the Police Station be pro- duced the watch and guard from his pooket. and said a women gave them to him but he would not knew her again if he saw her—Prisoner told the magistrates that when he was under the influence of drink on Monday morning a woman ran up to him and gave him the watch and guard to pawn—This was prisoner's 39th appearance and be was eenteneed to three months lin orisonmer.it with hard labour,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>MINOR OASES.</head>
        <p>MINOR OASES.</p>
        <p>Ararrington Borough Court.</p>
        <p>DIM/PAY—Before Mr F Monks (ix the chair), Councillor Smethurst, and Messrs T Birchen, 3 Wright and Hasty</p>
        <p>Thornton</p>
        <p>PREYiNG oN RETURNED SoLDIERC—Pater Scully. a wall- known character. residiny In Plumpton-street. and Michael Griffin. a labourer, ef Turner-street, were suiamened far committing a breach o. the peace by fighting In the Market Place OR November 15th.—P.C. Hayes said that prisoners were fighting In the centre of a large mow&amp; Griffin said Scully was the aggrestor.—The Chief Constable said Griffin recently returned from the front and Scully was one of those men who preyed on remised soldiers.—Scally was bound over to keep the peace for six months, and ordered to pay the costs, and the charge against Griffin was withdrawn. BETREME PROVOrATIoN.—AIfred Riley, ironworker, 5, Gough's-yard, and Arthur Themes**. 17, Cyril-street, were summoned for committing a brraela ef the peace by fighting In Winwick-straet. November 17th. P Newev proved the case.—Thompson stated that Riley same up to him and asked him if be could worry a rat. He then pushed a dead rat into witness's mouth, and ma resenting this conduct, he wiped the rat across his face. Witness again remonstrated with him and he pulled his hat and coat off. Witness then struok him.—B•th defendants were bound over to keep the peace, but Thompson had Cot te psy any costs. No LlGRT.—George Thomas, 61, Wellington street. was fined 5s for not having a lighted lamp attached to s lurry of which he was In charge on November 12th at 825 p rat.</p>
        <p>TUEEIDAT.—BeIore Aldermen Sutton (In the 'hair) and</p>
        <p>Hutchinson, Dr Fox and Mr M Y61.10g. THEFT OP A SPADE.—Themas Price. Longtord-street. was charged with stealing a spade value 2s 61, the property of the Corporation, fa June last —The Chief Constable said prisoner worked for the Corporation until June as a labourer. He had a spade given to him to do his work with, and should have returned it on leaving the service of the Corporation. Instead of doing so, be returned a spade which he hal Improperly become possessed of, and kept the other spade. In consequence of information he received, P.C. Lewis visi- ted the prisoner's house on Monday and asked the man if ke had a spade belonging to the Corporation In his possession. Prisoner replied that he hal not but subsequently admitted -that he had, and the spade was found in the cellar.—&amp; due of 10s was imposed.</p>
        <p>" ROMETI3ING FOR NOTNING."--Dr. Tibblee' Vi.. Cocoa, as a concentrated form of nourishment acd vitality, is invaluable ; nay, more than this, for all who wish to face the strife and battle of life with greater endurance and more sustained exertion, it is absolutely indispensable. Dr. Tibbles' Vi-Cocoa can be obtained !rem all Grncers and Stereo, or from 60, Bashill Row, Llcdmn, E.C. Dainty sample free. A postcard will d )</p>
        <p>Ex-Sergeant Leonard was formally dieobarged frog, the Army on Wednesday afternoon on the grouhd of but it is undergo d that this being medically mils', will not affeet the Committee's exertions to obtain his reinstatement and en inquiry into his case.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>NON-TRADERS' VICTORY. Congratulatory Meeting at Parr Hall. AN INQUIRY DEMANDED.</head>
        <p>THE NON-TRADERS' VICTORY. Congratulatory Meeting at Parr Hall. AN INQUIRY DEMANDED.</p>
        <p>Interesting Speeches.</p>
        <p>Parr Hall oral again crowded on Tuesday evening on the occasion ef a meeting held under the auspices at the Municipal Non-Trading Cmamittee, to celebrate the victory of November lit. The proceedings were presided over by Mr I A Naylor, and •rnongst those on the plat- form were a—Mies Naylor, Councillors James Dodds, John Petnberton, and J Upsen,Rev W Harvey (Widnes), Messrs S Norbury Williame,A Hulmo, John Lord,Charles Longshaw,S Kenyon, Horrocks, 8 Gmgh, J Holden. Shakeshaft, and ethers. THE CHAIRMAN CONDEMNS MUNICIPAL TRADING.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN, who had a cordial reception, said they were met together to celebrate a great, signal, and magnificent victory at the poll in Warrington oa Novem- ber Ist. According to his way of thinking he belieted that was a victory in the cause of righteousness They had stormed and tnken the citadel, and they had shaken the town from the centre to the circumference, and whet they hid done on November let would be repeated. (Apolauee.) They were only just beginning to fight and getting th.mielves in working order. (Laughter.) They were met together that sight to give a magnificent we'c 'me to the champions of the cause. (Ap- plause.) Someone told him a little while ago that if he (the sp ater) retired from supporting the mevement it would at ones lutterly collapse. A mere mistaken notion was never entertained by e mistaken comrausPy. They had long been aware of the fact that there had been something radically wrung and very rotten about the way the if sirs of the town had been manipulated. (Haar, hear.) When they got a few more men to represent then in their local Parliament of the type of Mr Dodde—(applause)—a gentleman who understoed fiasco, and Mr Pemberton —(applanse)—ts practical man who understood what a thiag ought to cost, they would have no more experi- ments made for the advantage of a private individual at the public cost. (Hear, hear, and applause.) Let them bear the fast in mind that the story had been cir- culated throughout the whole of Lancashire that certain things had been carried on in that wonderful building known as Longford Depot that would net stand the light of investigation. (Applause.) There were more things they would require to look into. Hs noticed that the London " Times ' had turned its attention to the state of things in Warrington. Some of their rather enterprising etitics had satisfied themselves that everything was square and satisfactory. There was one point, however, upon which everybody seemed to be agreed, and that was that they hod got heavily in debt—to the extent of one million pounde sterling. If it had not been for the manliness that the ratepayers bad displayed, and the public, cpirit that they had sown the one million would anon have been travelling up at a rapid rate to two. (Applause). He contended on principle that it was wrong for the Corporation to build a shop and make the peopie in the same line of business help to find the money. With regard to the question of municipal trading, which was producing suek a eommotion throughout the country—he mreldt the Corporation taking up trading—it did not matter whether it was buying a gas works or a water works, or anything else. When they were accused of spending a million pounds, one of the Warrington papers said : I " We have had to pay through the nose for the water works and gas works." If the Hl9O who Sanctionqd the expenditure had been finding the money out of their own pockets. he asked them would they have paid the exorbitant price they did ? (Cries of " No, no !") It was said the works purehased saved something which went to the aid of the rates. What did hat matter when they charged so much for the gas ? Whereas gas in Widnes cost is 51 a thousand feet. in Warrington it fleet 2s 71, (A Veiee : 35 1d). He understood it was 3s if they did not pay in time to get the dismount. (Laughter) Would any reasonable man with his head screwed on right, and in his proper senses, believe that the town was doing them a favour by charging 2s 7d, when, if they lived in Widnes, they could get gee for Is 51 ? When they Came to 101 k at the question of the Warrington Corporation trading with themselves, the most ebeolute folly they had been guilty of,was alhwiog their pockets to be picked in such an abominable fashion. (Hear, hear.) SPEECII BY COUNCILLOR DODDS. HIS COMMITTEE EXPERIENCES. AN INQUIRY DIIMANDED.</p>
        <p>Ceuneillor DODDS was received with the greatest es- thuslasre, the audienne cheering and singing, " For he's good fellow " HO said : I thank you fur the arless eoP tits reception you have just accorded nae. I feel it ropey' me for the little I have done in this con- troversy and agitation. I have ts mewl the fallowing resolntion :—" That this meeting of ratepayirs and inhabitants of Warrington rejoices in the success of the non-trading cause at the recent municipal elections,and hereby Pledgee itself to continue the struggle for muni- cipal purity. It calls upen the Town Council to institute a full ani an exhaustive inquiry into the trading of members of the Cluing with the Corporvion, whether direct or indirect, by contract or by s ih-cmtract, as individuals or as partners in firms, or as shareholders in limited companies, during the six veers endiog the 31st lldareh lost. It also calls upon the Town Council to frame and adopt such resolutions or rules as shall render it impossible in future for "my member of the Council to make or receive any profit or gain in respect of his sot- vices rendered or of goods supplied to the Corporation." (Applaum..) That resolution is the logical and necessary corollary of the Signal viotery which you, tho people of Warrington pained on the first of this month ; a victory which not only pulverized our oppoteents, but</p>
        <p>ASTONISHED ml MUNICIPAL WORLD. It astounded me. I was net so sanV.itine as were most of the members of the Non-trading Committee. It was owing to my advice that we made no real tight in the Bewsey or the St Austin's Wards, aad that the Howley Ward was not contegtsd. I stood out against the opposi- lion to Councillers Evans, Tinnion, and Bennett. I was modest. I tberight we might carry ens or two seats,and in regard to S Austin's Wird, relying on the personal assuranee of Caunoillor Minion I antieipated an over- whelming defeat. How far wrosig I was the results proved, and there in not the slightest doubt whatever that had we contested every Ward and divided cur working forces we should have carried, and carried easily, the whole of the nine seats. (Clvers.) It is, however. useless t• cry ever spilt milk, indeed we have every reason to be proud of the substantial success we achieved. And to the electors of the Town tiall Ward, the Fairfield Ward, the o:ford Ward, and last but not least the Latchford Ware, we are deeply grateful for the truly magnificent triumphs we gained. Persoaally, I shall never fetgot the splendid services of tile band of volunteers (not sae of whom I personally asked) who suatainei self-denial, onergy.•nd eathu•iatim carried t• So remarkable a result the flag of municipal purity in the Town Rail Ward. Chewed officially by the Con- servative party, with a retired otaelidate who had been bine years in the Connell, and with the openly sa- p d hontility of many of the lasadq of tie Liberal party, we nevertheleas routed an %lithely alliance. It was iatieed</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>SOLDIER'S BILTTLE,</head>
        <p>A SOLDIER'S BILTTLE,</p>
        <p>and the cream of the rank and file of- both the Coneervae tiye and the Liberal parties, rIIPC and women of char- acter and conscience, determined te be ne loager dumb driven pArty cattle, te no longer meekly sustain an im- morel and discredited party compact, became united as one man, and swept the fiEll in a way which will fee ever endear the Town Hall Ward to my memer7, and lalong with our other solendtd triumphs, render the municipal elections of 1902 ter ever memorable in the annals of Warrineton. (Cheers). It is barely thirteen menthe singe we commenced our work, yet in that time we have killed the party compact ; we lave get rid of the imbecile arrangement as to aldermanic office, and we have shown at the pee that we have behind us all 1 that ic best, all that is unselfish and patriotic) in the t 'seri, and that these constitute the great majority of the electors of Warrington. We have demoralized the immoral traders. As a result of our efforts several of them have sold their shares in at least one company whieh, in relation to the extent of its operations traded heavily with the Corporation, and we have seen one conspicuoce trader retire from the Coun- cil. But there is a great deal yet to be done. At the initiation of cur work I told you that it might take two three or more years to eradicate the evil. You meet rerasmter that the non-traders in the Council only num- ber four out of 37. What are they anion se many ? True, we have some good men and trua in the Council,</p>
        <p>men like Councillor Wilkinson, and several others who we believe will work with us, bus we need to keep our armour bright and t) be ready, and to be prepared, to contest, and to carry, if possible, every seat which may become vacant, from now up to and including the Ist November next year. I assure you it is no bed of roses open which you have placed m 7 colleagues and myself who have the honour of reprevnting you in the Council Chamber. The whole atmoaphere of the Town Hall is</p>
        <p>SATURLTED WITH PREJUDICE AND 48/811PATRY in regard to the cause we are proud to represent. Let rae be clear ; Ido not conap'ain of this, I do not say it is intentional ; but there it is all the same; not by any means upon the part of all the members or offinials, hut by covert insinua'tone and misrepresenta- tions it shows itself from time to time in committee. For instande, Councillor Bennett at one committee meet- moved that I should be put upon a certain sub-com- mittse, because, as be alleged, I had had • good deal to say eutaide upon the department governed by the sub- committee referred to. I had no difficulty in proving I had never even mentioned the department (the Library) in question, and in forcing a prompt apology. And 89 again with regard to a misrepresentation of my views by the same councillor it a meeting of the General Purposes Committee. No doubt we shall get over these little troubles, but I am afraid from the very short ex- perience I have already had in committee, that there are bound to arise sooner or later, and I fear seoner rather than later, fandameetal difference. between my- self and the committees of which I am a member Already I have had to make strong and uncompromis- leg objentien to</p>
        <p>A COURSE FOLLOWED BY THE FINANCE COMMITTEE in regard to the scaounte 'if pertain committeee. On the 10th iestant,l was preient at the meeting of the Finance Committee, and, althoueh I felt it my duty, as a new member, to remain quiet, I was astounded to notice that many of the accounts which came before us had no mark upon them ti show that they had been examined and passed by the committees specially appointed to see that such accounts had been properly authorised, that ties goods had been properly supplied, and at the con- tract rates. Neither was there any reference to minutes confirming coatracta -, in fact, the aceonnee I have re• ferred to were passed by the Finance Committee and the orders on the bank for the honouring of the Borough Treasurer's cheques in payment of the aceounts ware then acid there signed by the required three members of the Finance Committee, although the accounts I refer to had actually not boon passed (in fact had not even been geese) by the committees specially selected for their experience and epeeist capabilities to con- trol, examine, pass, submit and certify the payments to the Finanee Committee as in order. These accounts, as I have said, ;were passed by the Finanse Committee for payment on Monday. the 10th beet., and at the same time, on the same day, the order to the bank was signed by the three members of the Council, one of whom WAS the Mayor. Now come with me to the meeting of the Museum Osenneittee en Friday, the 14;11 inst. On that day, an item whieh,as I have al- ready told yea, had been passed for payment by the Fi- nance Cernmittee,and far which a payment order on the beakers had four days beenbeferesigned,nenee before the Museum Committee for examination and certification, which examination and certifieation the Finance Com- mittee ought to have insisted upon before even ener- taining the question of allowing the payment of the amount. I ask yhu, did you aver hear or such a putting of the cart before the horse ? Is there a tradesman, or a raerchantor a manufacturer in Warrington who treull sign an order on his bankr to honour the cheque of his cashier or clerk several days before the account for which the payment was propoled had been examined and eertified by the managers or foremen epecially set apart to buy and contract on his behalf ? (Cheers ) I venture to say that there is no such man in Warrington or elsewhere. And yet thisis what is going on in connection with your Town Council. Remember that the members of the Finance Committee are responeible if any improper payment is male. The payments may turn cut all right; the Beroueit Treasurer would, no doubt, withhold payments until the reepon- 'able committees certify their correctness. That is net the point at all. The Finanne Committee has no right to authorize 3 single payment whioit has net been first certified as in order by the committee epecifioally elected to so examine and certify. I yesterday called the atten- tion of the Finanee Committee to the unsatisfactory state of things I have felt it my duty be report to you at this length, and you will be glad tee leern that after a good deal of opposition a motion which I ultimately proposed was unanimously carried, to the greet that henceforth no order on the bankers of the Corporation for the payment a any account shall be signed by any member of the Council until such account has been examined and paused by the committee specially res- ponsible for the exponditere represented. (Cheers). In finance you oannot be too particular, however reli- able your officers. It is due to them that you should make it easy to de rieht, and difiletilt to do wrong ; and the members of the Council and of committees should net throw upen the permanent effieieli the work they thereeelyes were elect.d to d• and ought to do. I need not, I submit, take up your time in recommending the reeelotion I have already read in your hearing. You all thoroughly undorstand the grounds upon which we have time after time, called for an</p>
        <p>INQUIRY INTO THE rumour, AND ILLEIGAL TRADING of the past oix years (we sly six because we wish to include the five years originally asked for), sod judging fronta disoussion at the General Purposes Committee a week age the Coeinoil itself professes to desire the introduction of rules for the stop- page of all future impropristv in this respect- I was asked yeeterday at that meeting If I wanted to refi.ot upon the honesty of the Boreueh Treasurer. That question was put to ine by the Town Clerk. I said "No sir. I would trust the Hireugla Treasurer with every penny I have in the world, but publics moneys I am not allowed te se trust him with." (Applause). Referring to the published letter of an anonymous critics Mr Dodds sail :—He had, of course, t 3 tone exception to nay posi- tion as an officer of a limited company, and asked what difference there was between the position of a secretary and the position at a director. Well, in 99 cases out of 100, there is an important difference. In praotically all trading limited companies a direetor has under the re- gulations to be qualified by holding a certain prlportion of shares, whereas a secretary is aet required to he a shareholder. lam net a shareholder in any company doing husiness with the Warrington Corporation. Any rensuce-ation I receive does not depend 1.1.•4 the buil nolo done by any company trading with the Warrington Corporation, and that being so, I am absolutely and entirely, morally and legally qualified to be a member of the Warrington Town Council. (Applause). The whole fasts were before the Town Hall Ward electors on theist of November, and they voted with these before them. If, however, there should turn out at any time to be</p>
        <p>THE t+IILDOW OF A DOUBT IN BEBABD TO MY 01:71L1FIC&amp;TION</p>
        <p>I shall net hesitate t-17,-r-e-s-ign-r-4 ;int. The true duty of the Christian man is not to forsake municipal life be- cause it is corrupt or because it is impure, but to carry into municipal nativity the law and the spirit of the Master, and to resolve to do Isis part to enure for his foll•w-towcsmen all the blessings which a municipality jostly, win ly, Carefully and efficiently governed can secure for them. (Applauee). It is in that spirit that I have entered upon the service of the town, and I hope you may never have Gauss to regrel; the trust you have reposed in me and in my colleagues. In conclusion let me rally you as by a trumpet Call to stand by and Sustain your cause. It is, as I have before said, the issue of common honesty. Your representa- tives are but a remnant as yet. They will de what they can, but you must send us reinforcements as occa- sions offer. We must not abate any effort ; we must not even rest on our ears ; we must fight and win every vacancy that may sonar between now and next NIVOI2I- - first, and on that day we must contest and win every one of the nine seats. If you stand firm and work as heretofore, and with the same unfaltering will, Ide not doubt that a year hence we shall have the majority we need, and then when we are in a position to act effectively you rnay rely upon our surely doing so. Will you stand by and sustain your own cause ? That is the question. I leave it with you. (Cheers). MR. HULME AND THE LIBRARY.</p>
        <p>Mr ALFRED in seconding the resolution, said it was new understood from one end of Warrington to the other, and also in the innernaest recesses of the Town Ball, that Dodds, Pembertors, Gough and Co., was limited, but were practically as unlimited company of Warrington ratepayers. (Applause.) Eh, bPlieoed that the people of Warrington, and the inhabitants of many other towns, were now going to take an active and a permanent interest in the administratien of their losal affairs. They had Councillor Dodds on the Finitoce Committee, and he was pleased to say that he had &amp;heady made an impression there. With all respect to the Mayor of Warrington, who had the reputation of</p>
        <p>being a financier of the first water, it was not goad finance to pay the bills on the Monday and check the aecaunts on the following Friday. (Laughter and applause.) He (the speaker) did not do things in that manlier in his own house,and he did not think any mem- ber of the Corporation did so either. (Hear, hear.) Referring to the library, Mr Halms asked the audience if they were satisfied with it as it existed that day ? (Cries of " No. no.") Teat was the answer he had had given to him by every person he had asked the question of. He oontended that the peeplo ought to tell the Librarian what they thought. He (the speaker) had a oonversation with the Librariaa the other day, and he understood Mr Madeley was under the impression that they had a fairly up-to-dato library, but he (Mr Hahne) could not dad anyone else who thought the same. He questioned the Librarian as to the inadequate supply of modern ficticia, and he was sold that they " had whole rowe of excellent books on the shelves written 20 or 30 veers ago, and that the people ought to reed them." Ha (Mr Huhn.) suggested a bigger proportion of modern books, and reply was "The Committee buy as many books as they can, but they are short of money." The Library Committee had got some very good books they had purchased cheap from a subscription library and asked the public to put up with those. But it was net good enough. The people of Warrington paid the piper, and they wanted something better. (Applause). They did not want to rival Smith Kensington 'in a museum lout they would like a proper supply of modern literature. Ho (Mr Hulnas) ' went for some information. He wanted to compare Warringtoa Library with other towns. The Librarian earns out and said they did not publish any report. No ' (Mr Hillevi) thought th et wat very trauma% as other , libraries published reports. Ile believed the fact that so much was dons in the dark that the publio eould not , at to know who was responsible for their inefficiency. Fortunately the bad old days were going, and they had a representative on the Muleurn Committee who would llok after their interests. (Applause.) He suggested Ithat they commission Mr Dodds to sit on ths committee very severely. (Hear, hear and laughter.) COUNGILLOR PEMBER FON'S EXPERIENCE -3.</p>
        <p>Mr PEMBERTON, who heel a hearty reception, said he must take that opportunity of again thanking the 674 elooters who returned him on the let of November, and all the workers and friends who so kinlly assisted him, and last, but not least, the Good Templam (Applause.) He fully grasped the important position he had been placed in, and he wonld not forget that he was but one of the humble instruments by whistle the tewn was try- ing to break down jobbery, corruption mil extrava- gance. and he would do ell in tois power is bring about that desired result without fear of any person, parties, er cliques. (Applause.) If be sir any Councillor did not do his duty, his advice Was, do not vote for him again. (Hear, hear.) There might be a few persons ire that ball who bed read in a certain paper that he had a mintraot with a public body of which he was a meinher. Thet was a deliberate false- hood. (Cheers.) It WAS also false to say he bed stood against a Conservative candidate for the District Como- • for the election was not foueht on party lines at all. It was airs stated that he contested the election of Mr Parr as county councillor, but hew could he do that when tiers was no election? He was aloes asked to resign his neat on the Tramway Committee because the Council had soheduled a very small strip of land in frent of ehreo shops be owned in Stockton Heath. He had net, up to that day, received any notice to treat ; and he under- stood the Oweeratien might not want the land so- did ' he wish to sell it, He would not do so if he could help It. If the read was to be made wider the addition ought to come off the other eide,but if he was cempelllel to sell he sheuld claim to go to arbitration,and the public would then see that he got no favour from the Corporation. Who would suspost the Town Council of in- tending to do him a favour? (Loud laughter.) He had owned the property six years and they could trust him not to allow anything to happen that would reflect on hisiself,upon the geetlernen he was associated' with or upon his supporters. (epplause). Mr Pemba'- , ten proceeded to refer to the attituide of the " Ohserver' I ten years ago towarde Corporation contracts and con- trasted it with that paper's attituto at the present time. He could not for the life of him see how it mill support this kind of trading now. Reference was made to trading which had been done by the Warrington Slate Company, Mr Alderman Sefton aud Mr Alderman Franeenab with the Cerpor ttion If these men did these I things in Manchester they weall have to resign' but in Warrington the members of the Town Council did not seem to think anything of it because in face of the ver- dict of the people at the polls they had voted trading Aldermen and Conacillore to the position of Chairman of various committees as if they bad always done every- thing square and above board. He did not find Town Cocoon affairs any better that he expected. He had not been on long, it was true, but they must remember that he had given more time to Town Ceunefl businees these last two Years than many ea-called representatives who had eat there for years. (Applattee.) He hal exemined every printed minute (other minutes were refeeed to bina) and every order for payment for the last three years, so be was better posted up than many old mem- bers. Things were weree than he expected, booeuae he foued arivate committees that he did not knew abut, The General Purposes Committee, he foaled, elected three lute or private einnmittees. Oats of these was the Property Purchasing Committee, so they 94W when the Town Council passed the minntes of the General Pur- posse Committee it passed also, without the public pre- sent knowing, the minutes of these other three sub-com- mittees. The trot committee he eat upon was the Water Committee. As soon as the business opened an Aldermen complained most strongly that things were done. by the Chairmen and Deputy Chairman and effilials,twithout the knowledge of the whole of the com- mittee. There Was a hot ten minutes—(laughter)—but the Alderman proved his case to his (Mr Pemberton's 'mind. Os another committee he found that if a mac wanted to go into anything tint would take time, there was a pulling out of watches and oemplaints of waste of time. (Let:elder.) At the meeting of the General Purposes Committee, there wag a seat vacant on the Property Purchasing Sub-Committee. He was proposed and seconded, but the Mayor pat his foot en it at ones, saying he was not at old 'lough member to ne put on, (Laughter.) Anyone would have thought that his busi- ness experience in these particular matters would have been of good service to this committee. (Hear, bear.) He always thought that the Mayer was neutral in these matters, unless he had to give a (meting vote. (Hear, hear.) There was a very interesting disouseiett with regard te the sale of the Green Dragon in Bridge-etreet. He asked for particulars of sale and for any agreements, but, of course, was promptly ruled out of order. Then a councillor moved : That it boa etanding order that no official of the Corporation attend before tie magistrates, without the consent of the Council, to give evidence in support of a licence being granted to any person." (Applause). Some of the ceunoillers left the meeting, and ethers wasted to take a vote. Three Councillors left their seats and stood beside the door, and someone called "No quorum." (Laughter.) The three Councillors at the door walked out, and the Mayor then said there WAS no quorum. (Laughter.) Who did they think the Councillors were who walked oat? Why, two publicans and Councillor William Roberts. (Laughter.) Since that meeting he had in- quired from several old members for particulars of the terms the Corporation were making with the owners of the Green Dragon, but he had been unable to get to know. Teis was a wrong state of things altogether ; tilers was too mush done he seeret,and the public should net allow it. (Applauee.) At the Tramways Com- mittee's last meeting tenders were opened for the supply of cable, and were banded over to an official to make a schedule. Ho wanted the committee to go into the matter than, because, keowieg that tenders were to be dealt with,he had come thirty Miles purposely to attend the meeting. He was met with the excuse that it would take too long. (Laughter.) He pressed, and some said they had not the time. He replied, "Then you should not hav• coins on the Council. (Cheers.) He wanted to take the amounts of each tender, but was refused. ; (Cries of " Shaine.") He proposed without being able to carry his point; the chairman, deputy-claserman,and the official could be trusted; he could not. 'He said then, and he said it now,it was a very loose way indeed of doing business, which, as they coald all see, left open a very wide door indeed, What was the use of securely fastening the tenders and having thorn opened in committee if they were not to be allowed to know their contents? (Applause..) They were, after ins protest, passed round, but be was set allowed to copy and figures. (Laughter.) He must refer to the letter written to the press by Mr Henry Roberts. Ha could hardly find reasoseeb:e words to express his contempt for trying to put the cause of his resigna- tion that they had slandered his innocent wife and 'Ail- dren. No one en that platform had ever said a word of disrespect about thorn; in fast he had always under- etood that Mrs Robects was all that was good mad ladylike, but that was just like men when they got in a</p>
        <p>igial corner tl try and shield themselves behind some. one else, but it w %CI playine, it v3r7 low dswn t) bring in ladies and children._ _(diriplause.).._</p>
        <p>COUNCILLOR UPSOiki'S SPEECH.</p>
        <p>QUESTIONS AFFECLING THE WORKING CLASSE.4,</p>
        <p>Councillor Lipson said he was very glad to take part in that meeting. He knew that he isad not appeared upon that platform before. bed that had not been because he had not sympathised with the object of the movement. It had been a very great pleasure to him to sit in the audience and to listen to the speeches which had been made in supper.; of the movement which had been so triumphant at the recent eleation. He received a very considerable amend of support in his election from that eeseeeetieu and he was very grateful for chat asaietance. He believed they were that night celebrating the dawn- ing of a bright°, an.-1 hatter day in Warringtoo. (Applause). The most encouraging thine to him about the whole election was not the actual results achieved, although they were very gratifying in thernselvns, but the fact that the men and women of Warrineton, were taking such a keen interest in she government of this borough ; and the fact that the appeal which had been made to the men and women of this borough in the sense of moral resetitude had eluied them to brush on one aide, as a s'r mg man would brush on one side a spider's cobweb, all those party ties and obligation. which bad seemed to be so irresistible, arid which had seemed to bind down the people of this borough in (sad- iron bends. He was delighted ti find that the Mel and women of Warringt in had the courage to break those bonds and to say that so far as they were I concerned they would maintain the right and oendecan Ito wrong. It had been said that when the people of any city or borough took a great interest in the work of the Council, that Crooned thereby became progressive. He believed that was essentially true. Thera were, it seemed to him, two ways of progressing. Progress might be shown by the expenditure of huge surds of money in the building of palaces of juetice—laughter) —and the promoting and carrying out of extensive, expensive, unnerseastry waterworks, but he was present that night because he advocated another kind of pro- gress. and that progress was the promotion of the material welfare of the people of this beroueb. (Applause). Mr Hulme had referred to the Library quereen. He (the speaker) wead refer to the housing question. (Appleuse). There were hundrAds of housee in this borough te-lar which were back to back, and with- out offaeding delicate ears, he might say that decency and sobriety were inepoesihle. He knew a ease only a shed time ago where a child died, and three days after- wards a echoed attendance officer visited the house- The mother and father and children were sitting round the Wale having dinneromi from almost any position round Vast table, they could put their hands on the three der corpse. Such a thing was disgraceful ie any state of sorsiety. (Hear, hear.) With regard to the library COSS.. time, we had a library bet where was it ? Among the people who had libraries and reading rooms of their own. (Appian's.) was not vs advocate of the redaoti on of a rate in iteelf but he was an advocate of getting value for the money they spent. applause ) They might have an open-air bath, branch reedingreems, and better houses for tho working Cia9fiee. There were many ways by which the expenditure of one tithe of the amount of money they had lavished in past years on great under- tairings,rnight have dens incalculable goad to the people of this borough. Most of them knew be was a school- masterond he had worked at Town's End for seventeen years. His heart had bled many times when be had aeon the terrible suffering that little children hid to endure owing to their social surroundings. They might ask, what had that to do with the question before them to-night ? It had a great des! to do with it. He con- sidered that be his poeitign as a member of the Town Connoil,ho was a person planed in anthority,whese duty it was to remedy, as far as he could, the ills of which -e was cognisant. (Applause ) It was pointed out that men and women in what were called the slums spent their time in public houses. Ho did not blame them if the Town Council did not provide any other place for them, Where were our open spaces ? Where were our means of reeroation ? A municipality ought to provide for the recreation of all its people, and he had gone into the OS mnoil not to fight for the reduction of their rates, but to do what he could to improve the material condi- tions of the poorest people in the borough. (Loud applause.) They might call him a socialist, they might osil him a fanatic, but he asked them to hold up his handl( and the hands of those gentlemen who were pre- sent that night, in carrying out the work which they evidently approved of. (Loud applause.) _</p>
