      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>EXAMINER FRIDAY NOVEMBER 28 1902</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER FRIDAY NOVEMBER 28 1902</p>
        <p>MR.-NORBURY‘ WILLTS AND THE VICTORY. • A. THOROUGH WAKENING UP.</p>
        <p>In asking edr Norbury Williams to address the meet- ing the CHAIRMLN said he wanted them to give the best welcome of the evening to the man who deserved it— the man who had to bear the brunt of the abuse, who bad been stigmetieed as the imported orator, and who bad done valiantly for Manobester, Blackpool and Wom.rinnten. Mr Nonarray WILLIAMS. who was greeted with loud and prolonged oheerinc, said that was their tenth meet- ing. He did net know whether they had grasped the meaning of ten Conservative meetings of that size in this town. He considered them to be municipally abso- lutely a record and a credit to Warrington or to any other town where such a thing happened. It had been such an awakening as he had never heard of in any single hiccough ie this kingdom. Bleakpeel was not far behind Warrington in the awakening, hat he held Warrington to be chief in this matter. The number and euthusiasen of those meetings, and the continuous atteetion paid by the hareems to those who had come to address them had been absolutely unicine in the history of muniei- panties. Fe told them a few meetings ago that a great authority in this county had told him that Warringtoa was the sleepiest old teown in Lancashire. Well it had been • thorough wakening up. (Laughter). Warring- ton !lead rubbed its eyes and wakened up, lead he con- sidered that instead of sneaking of it as the sleepiest old town in this county, it was one of the meet wide- awake, and it deserved well of the county. (Applause). Mr. WILLIAMS went on to say that he had occasion in 1 the course of the meetings to refer by names, he sup- ' °seed, to at least a dozen gentlemen connected with the Town Council. He had always referred to them by names, and nobody had had the slightest doubt is to what he had to say about them. He had tried to be straight and above board and keenest, and he had never referred to them exeipt in publics sense, os- cause be did not bear any sell, Aosity to eny man or woman in Warrington. (Applause.) Some. 000 r crea- ture, hie had not the smillest idea as to who L might be, had sent to one of those twelve gentlemen an anony- mous eornausuieation of an insulting and ainovine character. It bore, ho understood, all the likelihood of healer; Come from him (Mr Willianav). It wee an insult- iny deouneent, he • understood, and even worse than in- sulting, but what enaoyel him was that their shoeld be found in Wm/el/Dote* a man who would stoop so low aid be en contemptible a se ward as to attach his (Mr Williams's) name to a document he had chosen to 'end anonyneously7 to one of these twelve members of the Connoil. He (Mr Williams) knew nothing of the eerernunimetion. He had had nothing but kindly feeling towards tie gentleman who received it and to whom he had never spoken. He hoped that gentleman would believe that when he (Mr Williams) was speaking in public on a public matter, he always tried fire+, to give the man whom he was speak- ing about dietinotly to uederetand who he (Mr William) was, secondly that he was referring to him in a public sense, and thirdly that ho had an absolute and peeve; right as a citizen of this land to refer to the public acts performed by a public man. (Applause.) Continuing Mr Williams said they had fiaished the first volume at tt Warrington municipal reform." (Applause), He had an impression from what he had beard that some of their good friends on the Council thought the move- ment would splutter out like a squib in the rain, that it would die oat and be no mere. They wore never more mistaken in their lives. Home of them (the non- traders) load felt the pulse of Warrington. They found that the men and women of Warrington were anaesable to reason, that they loved right, and if wrong and right were placed before them, it seemed to him that they would choose the right. (Applause.) He predicted that the fight, however many seetiene it might consist of, would not cease until all evils and eorruptioes of whi,h !they had hitherto spoken were abs*lntoly eliminated and put an end to. He abould like their friends la the Ceenell to fairly underetand that they most not be dwelling in a fool's paradise. They (the nen-treders) were going to fight to the finish. All the Aldermen and Coancillers would not be able to with- stand the volume of feeling which there wag in Warring- ton in fevour of the right and against the wrong. No asked at the last meeting in that hall—perhaps in rather a dramatio way—" Who is t. boss Warrington ? " Deep had given the answer now. It was the mere (Deletion " Shall a man and his following govern the town er shall the burgesses govern it thenteelyee,</p>
        <p>The burgesses had replied in a sense that nobody (maid - mistake and that a great many people outside Warring- ton had hoard of with rejloing, that they would no tenger be toe bond-slaves of any man or of any Bastion of men. He had never heard with greater pleasure of the result of any elections than of the result of the Warrington elections. Someone—(laughter)—sent him a long telegram giving the details with regard to everylward, so that before he went to bed that Saturday night, he know exactly that the enemy was as dead as a flake. (Laughter and Applause.) There wore several obarecterisies of the contests which made • deep mark upen his mind and aa impression on his memory whiote would never be removed. The Town Hail Ward exhibited an extra- ordinary coaditiou of affairs. It was practioelly at the last moment Viet Mr Dodds was prevailed upon to come forward. fie was not anxious to coma forward. he was not pressing to be adopted as a candidate. He was not knocking at the Town Hall front door and saying " I beg you to let me in." Instead of that, be (Mr Williams) was going to say they load actually to pull him and make him be the candidate. They saw the reply Warringtou gave in refer- ence to him. Referring to Latchford Ward Mr Williams said tie hid Peen fighting for thirty-four years in muni- cipal and parliamentary contests and he hal all sorts of ocembinations to deal with, and even sometimes to take part in, but he had never heard of anything like the Lalchferil Mad combination. He had heard sometimes of Liberals and Conservatives joining, but he had never heard in the same place of brewers and temperance pompie joiniag. There never wee a combioation, he believed, outside New York or Chicago—applause)— which could compare with that entry and lamentable cambination. John Pemberton ayes ouly a simple fellow —(laughter)—but all his desires were right—(applause) sad all his aims were for what wan right and clean aud straight,and that was why he was returned. (Appleuse.) rho parties who entered into that immoral conapaot and the people who led tnernsalves into that entry combina- tion, had—he was going to say Ls* ever—discredited in that district the name of Liberal or Cenieryetive. As for the temperance people, those who joined with the brewery poople—he was ashamed of them. Them were only a few of them, but it was • scandal that there iheuld be time. It only sheaved the condition things had got to in Warrington. He wanted them always to remember that this oondition of things had arisen from the forgetfulness and lethargy of the people. It was better late than never and it was well to wake up before they were dead. (Laughter.) Mr Williams also referral to the victories in Orford and Fairfield Wards, Continuing he said that his conclusion in reference to the whole matter was that the people of this town were now posi- tively tumbling over eaohlother to go to their (the non. traders') side. (Applause.) They wanted every man and woman in Warrington to be en their side. He was sorry for some of the leaders of the political parties. He should be ashamed to have been the leader of either of those parties—there was not a pia to chose between them. It was mainly the leaders they were grumbling about, bec suss they had led their followers into a quandary. He was sorry for the Liberal party and the Tory party in Warrington. He did not know what was going to happen at the general eleotioo. (A voice : Well, you moo' put a non-trader up). He would tell them what he thought. If they polled to-morrow and Pierpoint, Crosfield and Dodds were the candidates, Dodds would poll twice as many as the other tw ) put together. (Applause.) That was leis impression of how things stood to-day and it all arose from the political parties allowing themselves to be dragged into these illicit combinations, He had the question on his notes " Who is trading now ? " and he had the answer " Nobody." Ho had been making serious inquiries in Werringten for the lest twenty-four boors and he could not hear of anybody who was trading exeept one, and that was kept is secret and private that he oould not prove it. He thought they might practi- cally say that to-day trading had been put an end to. (Applause.) The four non-trading counoillors would very shortly be re-inforeed, and they wanted to have such a body of men in the Council as would alter the whole complexion of public life in Warrington. They swept the board at the last electiona, mud they intended to do so again. Referring to the bye-election which would probably be necessitated by the election of an alderman, Mr Williams said that already four gentle- men who sympathized with the movement had expressed their willingness to contogt the seat. He asked his hearers to watch the ()legion, as he had heard something which tended to make him believe that there was just a chance that some bribery might go on. At a recent meeting he asked whether Alderman Robert Heaton had ever traded with the Council. Alderman Heaton had never answered that question, but 69 (Mr Williams) was going to put the qatestine repeatedly until they got an answer. If Alderman Heaton had, as he (Mr Williams) believed, traded with the Coancil, he ought to be called to book and to retire. It was intolerable that a man who had done so should remain to the end of his days •oting away their money. (Apt:Madge.) Alderman Heaton had been voting away their money all these years. Had he been making profit at any time eet of the Corporation ? IF he had he (Mr Williams) called upon him to retire—(Loud applause)—ancs be hoped the men of Warrington would insist that this man if he lead traded, should retire from the Council. Re dd not know how strong they were in Warringtou, but he knew that if this had happened in Manchester— if the nhadow of it had happened that it would never be allowed for a single hour. Alderman Heaton must answer that question or they must see that by some means or other the plane was rendered to hot for him, sad his should leave the Council. (Applause.) Cloun- °Moe William Roberts said that other members of the Council who had been guilty of shady things had been purposely withheld from the agitation. Ha asked Coun- cillor Roberts to name anybody that they had not named If Coeneillor Roberts thought they were parposely leaving ant anybody in order to shield laim,he washnis- takeu.:lf he knew these things were wrong it was his duty to report them. (Applause.) Alluding to the demand .for an inquiry the speaker said he expected neembere of the Council would be falling over each other to vote for one, although they had opposed one before. They expected they would go in almost unanimously for the six years' inquiry. Whether they did or not there must be a lie years' inquiry by some impartial outage.. They also wanted a reso- lution adopted by the Council which weald say "We shall never trade again wreogly." They believed they would get the resolution in a few months. Referring to tendere, Mr Williams said it was infamous that a man like Mr Pemberton shottld be told that he must not take • single note. The speaker also produced • form which had to be signed by members of the Manchester Council before accounts cenld be paid. What Mr Decide had told thorn of,io regard to the Finance Committee was absolutely preposterous. Mr Williams read the poll list of councillors whose term of office expires next November :--rown Hall Ward, Councillor Smethurst ; Whitememe. Councillor Barton ; Bewsey, Councillor nierpaiac ; Orford, Councillor Peacock ; St Johns', Couleillor F R Roberts ; Fairfield, Councillor Hulse ; Rowley, Councillor Join Etrava ; St Austin's, Councillor W Roberts ; Latelaferd, Councillor • Sampson. He had hen looking over the atteadaneee for the pas` year of Councillor F R Roberts and he found that the total number of times he could have been present at committee meetings was 21, whereas his total attendant:fee were only eleven. Again speakine of tenders, Mr Williams said he had heard of sales where tenders came in and the price of one firm was mere than 25 per ceet higher than another. The one which was 25 per cent higher obtaised the order, and the stuff was said to be absolutely equal in both caw. Mr Williams continuing, said they wanted a complete record of bow members voted, and from access to all the minutes. They also wanted a new elective auditor. (Applause.) Alluding to Councillor Penthertmes position in reeard to the Tramways Committee he said Councillor Pambertea did not want to sell his land. If the Corporation took it from him they would have to pess the proper price. That was not trading with the Corporation. There was nothing corrupt in it. It was something the very opposite. (Applause.) Mr Williams asked his hearers to "keep meths steam" and impressed upon them that they ware the custoliaas of the honour of Warrineten. Ninety-nine public men out of a hundred seemed ti if they could not be depended upon. They seemed squeezeble, changeable, anti turnable ; and it ought not to be so. Ono man wise sailed straight and honest to the goal was worth a hundred of those men who were wandering about. (Applause). Mr Norbury Williams and the Chairman having been thanked, the meeting coneluded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>P 4'20 3, , tk'</head>
        <p>P 4'20 3, , tk'</p>
        <p>"THE FOOD THAT IS ALL FOOD."</p>
        <p>"FORCE," eaten with a little milk, forms a meal—breakfast, lunch, or supper—" fit for a king." It component parts are whole- iheat and barley-malt. Consequently, it is a combined tonic, nourisher, stimulator, and healer. Where there is no vigour, "FORCE" ins it and where there is vigour, " FORCE " increases it. 7%c1. a large package. Ask the grocer.</p>
        <p>" WHAT A BRI3AKFAST FOOD SHOULD Blit." " 'PORCH' exactly takes my fancy. I think it just what a breakfast food should be. My wife likes it, too. I don't think she would ever be in favour of our going back to oats—and cooking." (IVame furnished on application.)</p>
