      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <p>THE LATE REV. HUGH PRICE</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>HUGHES.</head>
        <p>HUGHES.</p>
        <p>Public References.</p>
        <p>Primitive Methodist Chapel, Greenway-raad, Runcorn,</p>
        <p>Oe Sunday morning, the Rev WA Eyre made sympa thetia reference to the life and work of tho late Rev Hugh Price Hughes,s man of intellectuallability,spiritua fervour, strong convictions, dauntless courage and untiring energy. He packed into his 54 years, said the preacher, more service than most men could put into a hundred years. The hymn " 0 God our help" was sung and the anthem " The ranaomed of tha Lord " was 'rendered by the choir.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>SONS OF TIMPERANCE AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>SONS OF TIMPERANCE AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>At the no oeting of this association held in the Green- way-road School on Saturday, Mr Edward Key, jun., presidinz, Mr Sisbreavo made a few observations on the death of the Rev Hugh Price Hughes. He said the churches and the temperanco cause had sustained a •zreat less.—" A prince and a leader had fallen in Israel." He had conspicuous abilities and had left his mark on the history of the temperance movement. He was a born leader and it was an inspiration to sit under him • It was sad to think that be had been cut down in the meridian of life. But " Gail buries his workmen, but he carries on the work." It Was well to remember that God never made a mistake, that nothing could happen in our lives without his knowledge and permission. At the funeral every respect was paid to his memory. The Church was represented. A hundred wreaths were sent by loving friends. The one sent by his wife had on it these wards : I have fought a good fight I have finished my course I have kept the faith." Tao Chair- man (Mr Key) made a few similar remarks, and the meeting closed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>MIL B. A. NAYLOR.</head>
        <p>MIL B. A. NAYLOR.</p>
        <p>At Bold-street Wesleyan choir fcatival on Monday even- ing Mr R A Naylor (chairman) alluded to the late Rev Hugh Price Hughes. He said one of the greatest and best men in connection with the Wesleyan denomination had suddenly been called away from this world to hl s home. Last night week be (vir N4rlor) was determined to go and hear the Rev Hugh Price Hughes in St Jamee'e Hal!, London. When he got to the door, he was told that the hall had beam full for half an hour with 4,000 people. Little did he think, when he name away, that the next day hut one, the man who had such power as to sway a multitude like that for OA and for good would be called to his rest. Ho was proud to know that members of the Established Jhurch, as well as Noncon- formists, on Sunday paid high tributes to the memory of that great and hood minister of God. He noticed that on Sanday afternoon in St Paula' Cathedral the Ven Archdeacon Sinclair paid a magnificent tribute to the memory of the departed, whom he was pleased en acknowledge as a friend and a man who tried to do his duty. (Applause).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>FR EATMENT OF DEAFNESS.</head>
        <p>FR EATMENT OF DEAFNESS.</p>
        <p>A Speciality.</p>
        <p>The establishment of a estsoial Institute in Loudon for the treatment of defective hearing and diseases of the ears, nose, and throat, marked the beginning of a Derr era for the deaf, for in tits hands of its staff ef medical men, who have made deafness a life study, the nese Dreuet method of treating ear ailments by local applies- tiers, is now curing an average of 35 cases every month. A. full aoeount of this Institute and of the new treatment is contained is the last illltle of the " Journal for the Deaf." This Journal. together with a detailed account of cured eases (whose 11121103 and addresses are given), can be obtained free from the Seerstary, Drouet Insti- tute, 72, Regeat's Perk-road, London, NW. With the " Journal "tiers is also enclosed a Report Form, 11l means of which any sufferer can obtain advice free by eserrespondenee and instructions for borne treatment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>Runcorn Petty Sessions.</head>
        <p>Runcorn Petty Sessions.</p>
        <p>MONDAY—Before Messrs F. I Norman (in the chair) and ;</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>Pritchard.</head>
        <p>Pritchard.</p>
        <p>THEFT FROM A LonnprOlio-u-s7..—James Lynch, a travel ling printer was charged on remand with having stolen a vest valued at 4s and a knife valued at 3d from a lodging house in Runcorn on November 15th.—The evidence showed that the prisoner went to Mrs Woodfine's lodging house in Friar street and was pot to sleep in the same room with a mall named Peter Knowles whose suit hang behind the door. Next morning, after the prisoner had left the house, the vest was missed and information given to the police. The prisoner was traced to two pawnbrokeen shops,where he offered t° pledge the vest. At both places the vest was refused and it' had not been recovered. The knife was found in hie posses- sion and evidence was given to prove that it belonged to Mrs Woodfine.—Priseaer denied having offered the vest at the pawnshops and contended he had not taken it. The knife te,' put in his pocket not thinking of stealing It but out er absence of mind.—Prisoner was committed for one day's Imprisonment. TRANSFER or LIOENCES.—The following licences were transferred :—London and North-Western Railway Motel from Rmily Harrison to James Robinson Hall ; prosped lan, Weston, from James Robinson Halt to George Broos Jones ; Derby Arms, from Elizabeth Crosby to William 3- Crosby ; Ship Inn trons John Leyland to Charles Leyland an the Nelson Inn from George B Jones to Thomas Ernest Gillgrass. _ _</p>
        <p>INTERESTING LICENSING ASE.—Mr Burton applied 00 behalf of Messrs Peter Walker and Sons. Warrington, for the name of the licensee of the Wheat Sheaf in the records of the apart to be altered from Mary Taylor to Mary Bees. He sat 'a that Mrs Taylor was a widow and had recently been married. —Superintendent Bunion said the licence should be in the husband's name. That was the usual thing. The polled' would have no objection.—Mr Jones. Messrs Walker's agent. said Id rs Rees was the tenant, and all the furniture belondo4 to her. It was desirable that she sh•uld retain the licence 05 account of herself and her children. The licence originalif belonged to her first husband, and was transferred to her el, his death, This applicition was made so that the records 0' the Court should be kept in proper order. Mrs Taylor we° still the licensee. She had simply ohanged her name.—Thl Magistrates' Clerk (Mr Johnson) There is another oceoPle,! now.—Mr Jones said that no objection was ever made Liverpool and Birkenhead to these applications. The woMell, was still the tenant of the noise. If Coo application were new granted it would place Mrs lees and her family in e° awkward posltion.—The Chairman said she should hal° thought .of these thing. before she was married. The eele, was a peculiar one, and it would perhaps be best to leave " over to be dealt with at the annual sessions.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>IN A YORKSHIRE DALE.</head>
        <p>IN A YORKSHIRE DALE.</p>
        <p>Shakespeare has told us that disCretion is tho better park of valour,and those who havo had most exporience of life are least disposed to dispute that assertion. Toe knowing ones among us seek to altogether avoid danger rather than meet it and wrestle with it. Tnough pre aortae of mind is a goad thing when tire house is on fire absence of body is better. The valiant fight, the wise abstain from quarrelling. -</p>
        <p>The object of sanitati; is to suppress, 0: limit the spread of infectious diseases, and generally to promoSe health. Na one with the least claim to common seas° will lightly expose himself to infection, however confy dent in his power to resist it. Ent unfortunately thero are diseases which we cannot always refuse to meet, b: we ever so wary. For instance, indigestion will ne: unfrequently attack the individual who lives 0" most abstemious and carefully regulated life' The occupation and surroondings of Mrs Matadi' Agar, of Thornton Dale, ?Mitering, Yorkihir6l wife of an agriculturist, have always been such 0 shored ensure immunity from indigestion, so far as Of; thing can ensure it. Yet what do We find ? Mrs AVIA writes on 2nd May. 1902 : " As one who has derive" very great benefit from Mother Seigel's Curative Syro!: 1 think my experience cannot fail to be of service those who are now in the unhappy condition fror which that medicine bao so providentially rescued had always an excellent appetite, and was a stroaP and healthy woman, until I bed coagestioa of the lune, which so shattered rny constitution that for yeoll afterwards I Was more or less of an invalid. I w,es always languid and tired, and my weakness nro_ me despendent and irritable. Whatever I ate lay IRO ; heavy stone on my chest. After a time my stoulac't beesana so deranged that it rejected even the lightest °, food. Chronic constipation, toe, was responsilN: for occasional violent sink headaches, accompanied /21 dizziness and sickness. My nights were partially sleeP; less and never properly restful. Though I had ell/ kindest of doctors he was powerless to benefit me, ano found tie relief until I resorted to Mother Seigel's Cure; tive Syrup. One bottle produced a slight change for 05 better ; I could eat a little food and retain it. Tbel was the beginning of my recovery, which was slow D,e, sure. I took several bottles, how many I fail to reracP'' ber, and in the end my perseverance was rewarded °.lo perfect cure. For humanity's sake, my case cannot too widely known.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>SON'S CONDUCT.</head>
        <p>A SON'S CONDUCT.</p>
        <p>'Judge Bowen Rowlands' Strong Comments. His Honour Judge Bowen Rowlaede, 8.C., had occa- sion to make some strong comments in an action which came before him at the Warrington County Court on Thursdaylastiweek. Elizeboth Hathbone,siwidow,reeiding at 78 Victoria-terrace, Stockton Heatbosued her son William Wright, Whitefield-road, Stockton Heath, for the return of a large number of articles of furniture and Wearing apparel, or their value (£l3), and £2 damage for their detention by the defendant. Mr Brown 111PPeared for the pleintiff and the datandant was repro seated by Mr Willett. At the outset Mr Browne stated that all .efferts to bring about an amicable settlement of the case had been ef no avail. Defendant was employed by Messrs Pletcher, Russell, and Company. Plaintiff formally lived at Pendleton, and when her husband died she con- tinued to line there for some time, working as a tailoress. In August 1901 she was approached by the defendant's wile and asked if she would to and live with her and the defendant at Warrington and bring her furniture with her. Mr. Wright also told her that he need not work as defend- ant could easily afford to keep her, and if she made any oney by her trade she could have it for pocket money. Defendant, was in receipt of £3 a week. Plaintiff came to live with the defendant, awl from time to time she earned money by making shirts for Messrs Ceates and Company. She stayed with defendant about twelve stonths and then left him. Defendant refused to return the articles of furniture and wearing apparel which were the subject of the claim. Another effort was made to settle the action, but defendant chose to fight the case. Plaintiff gave evidence bearing out this statement,and Stated that defendant's n ife received every penny that She (plaintiff) earned by making shirts. Defendant's version was that plaintiff gave his wife tile furniture in return for her services in nursing her during five weeks' illness. His Honour: I think she got well paid. Ton ought to be pretty rich by now. Witness, continuing,said plaintiff eameite live at War- regton because she was ill and not able to look after sereelf. About six menthe age she had some dispute With him and left. She took Rome furniture away but Witness told her to bring it back and she did so. His Honour : You ought to be ashamed of yourself. How you can stand in the witness-box and tell this Story without being thoroughly ashamed of yourself I ,don't know. I thank goodness there are very few men like you in the country. This story you have told taalees my blood run cold. I did not believe there were such people in the world. , In answer to his Honour, defendant said he did not Snow anything about the wearing apparel. Ellen Wright, defendant's wife, said she nursed the Plaintiff five weeks, Plaintiff was very poorly and said she mull not keep the house en any longer, and asked if arrangements could be made for her to live with wit- teas and her husband at Warrington. Witness said she would see her husband about the Matter. She did so and defendant consented to Plaintiff coming to live with them. Witness went back to Pendleton and told the defendant what her husband had said and asked her what she was going to do about" the things." Plaintiff said she would give Witness what there was in the house, but witsees told her that she could not take them all. Plaintiff then told her to take what she wanted and she (plaintiff) would sell the remainder to pay for the carriage of the Others. With regard to the clothing she gave witness same and plaintiff badithe ismainder. Alice Swift and William Henry Taylor gave corrobora tive evidence. His Honour gave judgment for the plaintiff for the return of the goods or tite payment of £l5 with costs. „Ile said defendant's story was not only incredible but Is was unnatural in toe highest degree. He refrained from giving further expression to his opinion. Happily Such things as this occurred but seldom, and he did not know there was any language he could have used to express the view he took ef the defendant's conduct. His Honour complimented Mr Willett upon his Conduct ti such a difficult case.</p>
        <p>(TRADE MARK.)</p>
        <p>It does the raising quickly and evenly makes baking pleasant, easy work, and gives lighter, sweeter bread.</p>
        <p>Ask your Grocer for a 7d. Packet and try it at once.</p>
        <p>)</p>
