      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>Zettent to the Eltttor.</head>
        <p>Zettent to the Eltttor.</p>
        <p>Another Big OfPfcial for Warrington.</p>
        <p>Sir, —Another highly paid Corporation office is to be created.—A director of education for the new Technical school at a commencing salary of £3OO a year (this means £.500 by and bye), a graduate preferred. Surely thy, the ratepayers are burdened enough without being called upon to support another highly paid official. Besides auch a pest is quite unnecessary for a town of Warrington's population. There are teachers for each department at the Technical Schopl, I suppose, and some of them well paid; there is besides a secretary to organil.3. What need then of a Univenity M.A., to supervise the kind of teaching required for fitting a youth for the Warrington tr bdes. I suppose when the Education Bill passes arv.totr " araccor will be appointed for tte elementary schools. If this new director is aprointed frr the Technical Schools,will the present Secretary still be retained ? And if not, will this " many-sided man" still c intinue to receive the total Ware of £250, name, e)ale, and gas, in his eapacity as Secretary, Librarian, Curator, ,k 3. Sze.* As the Yankee says" I want to know." Perhaps our newly elected Csuncillors will make it their business to find our. November 26th 1902.</p>
        <p>PRO BONO PUBLICO.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>PRESENTATION TO MR. JOHN WHITE, J P. Speech by Lord Derby.</head>
        <p>PRESENTATION TO MR. JOHN WHITE, J P. Speech by Lord Derby.</p>
        <p>At the annual meeting of the Royal Lancashire I Agricultural Society held at the Town Hall, Preston, on Monday afternoon, Mr John White, J P, of Warrington, chairman of the Council, was presented with an hono- rary vice-president life-membership of the society, in -recognition of his long and valuable services. In making the presentation, Lord Derby, who presid- ed, said it was hardle necessary for him to expatiate fully upon Mr White's merits, and he would not like to say in his presence all the members of the society felt for him for the services he had rendered to the society which rightly entitled him to the presentation. He had to ask Mr White to accept the honour which the society hoped it conferred upon him by his appointment as hors life vice-president. The address (which was enclosed in a handseme silver casket) read :—" At a special Council meeting of the Royal Lincashire Aericultural Society, held at Preston on November 10th, 1802, it was moved by Phillip Park, Esq., J.P., seconded by; Chas. E. Linaker, F.S.L, and unanimously resolved, that Jehn White, Esq., J.P., chairman of the Council, be elected an honorary life vice-president in recognition of the long an I valuable services he has rendered to the society." Continuing, his lordship said he must ask Mr White to be good enough to receive, on behalf of the society, and as a small earnest of their feelings towards him, that which recorded in form his appointment of the life membership to be enclosed in a silver casket. They trusted he would receive the gifts as an evidence of the best wishes cf the members, with the assurance that he would always be looked upon as one who had done excellent service to the society, and who bad eadeared himself on all occasions to those with whom be had been brought into contact. kApplause.) MR, WHITE CONTRASTS WARRINGTON SHOW WITH TER</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>PRESTON 911)W.</head>
        <p>PRESTON 911)W.</p>
        <p>In reply, Mr White said he was thankful to his lord- ship for the very kind remarks he had made concern- ing him. They might think that in some measure ha was entitled to the honour on account of the great success the society had achieved during the past year but although he had had the honour of being chairman of the Council be could claim for himself no share of having brought about that success any more than any other member of the Council. Still he was thankful that such a success had been achieved during the year, end Ise hoped it might not be the last which the society might gain during the remainder of his lifetime. (Applause). He was not going to r .pay their kindness by an autobiographical sketch, but he might, perhaps, be permitted to say that his connection with the society extends more or less over 50 years. His father was officially connected with the Liverpool Society before it joined the Manchester Society. He was the first secre- tary of ths Manchester and Liverpool Society, and he remained secretsry for seven years. He was on the Council until 1869, sines which time he (Mr White) had had the honour of being a member of the Conacil of that society, and of the Amalgamated Society. Perhaps it might be interesting to give them some figures. He attended the first show in 1250,and he hed in his posses- sion the reports of the society since 1849, and on lock- ing at the reo.-rts for the year the show was held in Warrington he found the following particulate : At Preston for the four days the subscriptions ameunted to £1,500; at Warrington they came to £530 ; which was considered a very coosiderab:e sum in those days. At Preston the entrance fee came to £2,003, at Warrifte- ton to £B. (Leughter.) Gate money : Preston £5,212, Watrington £223,aa d in the report the seciety congratu- late themselves upon the increased amount of gate money. (Lamehter.) The local contributions at Preston realised £1,984, and at Warrington £2O. (Renewed laughter.) The Society in prizes this year £3,118, and at Warrington the sum was £315 ' • the showyard fittings, &amp;c., at Preston cost £3,124, and at Warrington the printing at Preston £402, and at Warrington 134. (Laughter and applause.) He mentioned these figures to show what immense strides had been made, and he did not think they need despair that they should not have something approaching the same seceess in the years to come. Re hoped to see the society teach a I errater pinnacle of success than it bad done during the year. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>South Lancashire Tramways. The Haydock District Council, at its monthly , meeting on Thursday afternoon, presided over by ' Dr A Dowling, had before them several questions concerning the e,outh Lancashire Tramways. The clerk reported to the committee that a representa- tive of Messrs Krause, contractors for the construc- tion of the tramways, had had an interview with him, and intimated that they (the contractors) had received instruction to proceed with the road-widen- ing immediately, and he desired to know whether the Council would undertake the work. After consideration, the Council decided that they could not Fee their way to do the work. The laying of the line from the St Helens tram terminus at hay- dock to the junction with the Wigan tramway at Hindley is almost finished, but until the widenings at Haydock and Ashton-in-Makerfield are completed , the trolley wire posts cannot all be erected. Great Sankey School Board. This Board met on Tuesday atternoon,Mr Wright presiding There were also present Nir 0 Thomp- son, Rev J R Jones, Mr W D Jeans, jun., (clerk),and the headmaster (Mr 0 Clarke). The Clerk stated that the Treasurer's account boek showed a credit balance of £349 13s 10d. Tne headmaster's report was as follows :--Week ending, November let: On books, 303 ; present, 273 ; average, 246; fees, £1 Os 81. Week ending November Bth : On books 298; present, 271 ; average, 2484: fees, 19s 44 Week-ending November 15th On bloks, 296 present, 272; average, 245.2 fees, £1 Os Bd. Week- ending November, 22nd : On books, 292 ; present, 273; average, 248.1 ' • fees 19s 34. Average for the month, 2469; total fees; £3 19. s 10d. It was decided to issue a precept on the rate for £l2O for the half- year. The tender of Mr Harry Fairclough for the rebuilding of the wall in front of the school at £l6 was accepted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>ST- HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST- HELENS.</p>
        <p>STRANGE FATALITY TO A ST HELENS BABY. The circumstances of the death of a child are reported from St Helen,. Frederick Slavin, aged 17 months, the son of John Slavin, chemical labourer,24, Vernar.-street, was ill, and on Wednesday Mrs Slavin went out for medicine, leaving the baby in a cradle elevated on two chairs so as to be cut of the way of draughts. Another Child, aged four years, was left in the house. Mrs Slavin was absent only ten minutes,but when she returned she found the cradle had rolled or been pulled over,the baby being dead on the Boor. a</p>
        <p>The Brussels correspondent of the " Daily Telegraph says that according to a tell gram received from Batavia by the " Algerneau Bandeishiad " a great robbery was committed on Saturday night in the bank of Java by two former clerks of the bank. They gc t int.) the buildings durisg the night and forced. a safe.from which they took the sum of one million fi,,rins in twtes. Caught In the act by three clerks they fired atthetn. One was killed and the others injured,but the thieves were unable to escape with their booty. They were arrested by bank porbirs and recognised as former clerks, who had been dismissed on account of their dishonesty.</p>
      </div>
