      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>RELIGIOUS LIBERTY AND EQUALITY. Lecture by the Rev. John Yonge.</head>
        <p>RELIGIOUS LIBERTY AND EQUALITY. Lecture by the Rev. John Yonge.</p>
        <p>The Education Question.</p>
        <p>" Religinus Lihavty and Equality, Past, Present, and Future," was the subjezt of a lecture, illustrated by lantern views, given before the members and friends of the Wycliffe Young Pe3p!e's Svciety on Monday evening by the Rev John Yoage. The proceedings were presided over by Mr C Eccles, and the duties ot ianterniat were satisfactorily discharged by Mr E Wallington. , In the course of his lecture, the Rev Join.; Yolsaz said religious liberty was the birthright of every citizen of the British Empire. So far as they who lived In towns or cities or even in pupulous rural districts were con- cerned they possessed and enjoyed this freelom. It was, however, not so certain that they who lived in villages—where squires and parsons were virtually the local rulers—had as complete a religious liberty as had the dwellers in towns. Taey might have it in theory, and if they chose to submit to social obloquy, and perhaps even to persecution and depriva- tion, they might also have it in actual feat, But there were numerous country parishes where the little trades- man could only be a Dissenter at a business loss, and , where the agricultural labourer stood to lose even borne. and livelihood, unless be showed himself an obsequious C ' hurchman obedient and bumble to his "pastors and masters." In these unquestionable fact, of village life to-day, they found a strong reason for advancing abso- lute religious liberty fcr all classes of the people ; and also for taking particular care that no new legislation whatever should make this religious function more diffi- cult and mere limited than it was already. Proceeding to refer to.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>_RELIGIOUS FAZIILLITY.</head>
        <p>_RELIGIOUS FAZIILLITY.</p>
        <p>the Lecturer said that phrase —meant that every religion and every form and creed of religion stood equal before the la w, that no church, no creed, no sect poss sseed any distinction, privilege, or power which was not also possessed equely by every other. Well they had not got that yet. So long as the sovereign of the Realm was compelled to be a member cf the Anglican Episcopal Church, so long as he could only hal ecclesiastically crowned by the clergy of that church, and so long as Nocconformist ministers could only be present at his Corosation by special permission (and in very back seats), so long as Rectors could urge that the Mayors and Corporations wire in duty bound to pay 'their effi. cial visits exclusively to thd Parish Church; se long as Anglican bishops remained as peers of Parliament ; so long,in fact, as the Anglican Episcopal Church remained established by law as " the church " in this realm. enjoying exclusive prerogatives and exclusive rights to natioaal buildings and national moneys,so iong,religious equality was a thing unattained and unenjoyed. It had been the Nonconformist contention for centuries that the church of Jesus Christ had no need of political supple and that he bartered her freedom when she accepted it. Jesus Christ's kingdom was " not of this world," and hal ne need of either this world's soldiers to fight for it. or this world's civil servants to make its laws or collect its dues. Recent'and present events were once more bringing this question within the range of practical consideration and it would not be surprising if the early part of this present century should witness a new endeavour of Nonconformity to promote dis- , establishment and its victory made certain by an up- rising within the establishment itself to buret its fetters and gain its freedom. The education controversy would, in his belief hasten that most desirable event. The lecturer preceded to deal with the past history of the Christian Church in England as casting light upon the religious liberty that they enjoyed to-day. The sub- ject was illustrated by a number of lantern views. , He showed how the Church of England came to be an Act of Parliament church, not governed from Rome or Canterbury, but from Westminster. It had no power, ' be said, to legislate for itselt whatever. The real rulers of the Church of England were the members of the House of CaMOVII9. Now that</p>
        <p>TUE EDUCATION QUESTION was Sc, supreme in the mind of the nation it should not be forgotten what had been the attitude of the Church of Ele,land to national education. If the statements of clergy and members of that Church were to b 3 accepted as. accurate, they would have to believe that the nation was unspeakably, and indeed even exclusively, indebted to that Church for the provision of education throughout the past. They were continually being told that the Church of England had done everything for national education and that Noacon- formiste had done next to nothing. And in proof they were pointed to the national schools which in all the parishes of the kingdom bad existed under the shadow of the Church. Well, no doubt every episcopal parish or nearly so, had had for long years its parish sohoel. But what were the historical facts ? History was stubborn, and it was impartial, end it testified what it knew. It did not offer rumour, or fancy, or part:zen desire, as evidence upon any matter. History told them that in 1807 the House ef Comrnens passed a Bill for the estab- lishment of a national system of education, but the bishops induced the House of Lords to reject it. The Bishops represented their church, and their Church rejected national education. With regard to the Nonconformist side of the question, as far back as 1796, a young Quaker, Joseph Lancaster, opened a school in his father's house in Southwark, for the edu- cation of the children of the poor. In two yeir3 his school grew to a thousand scholars. He introduced the monitor system of teaching. His great success soon attracted notice, and it became quite fashionable to go and see his schools. King George HI. openly encouraged them, and encouraged also their unsectarian principles. In 1808 Lancaster saw the foundation ef " the goyal Lancastrian Institution for promoting che education of the poor," a title afterwards altered to "The British and Foreign School Society," which had bsen and still was ander the patronage of the Sovereign. Schools of this unsectarian character were soon established in all the principal towns of England, known as " British schwls." The first serious attempt to deal with National Education came from the Nonconformists. Let them lay hold of that, and not forget it, and when anyone said to them " Where would national education have been but for the Church of England?" they must reply "It would have been nowhere ; for the Church of England never started, and always opposed, until 18081 when a Quaker, and therefore a Nonconformist, estab- lished a system which was repidly spreading all over the land " It was the success of the Nonconformist scheme which compelled the Church of England to move at all. Then with the old cry " The Church in danger !" and denouncing Lancaster's as a " wild, absurd, nu- Christian scheme," the Church of England started its " National Seciety for the Education of the children of the poor according t 3 the principles and doctrines of the Church of England." Proceeding, the Lecturer contended that the Church of England had always thrown hindrance in the way of national educe- Geo. For neatly 20 years after Queen Victoria's accession, Nonconformists were shut out of the national universities of Oxford and Cambridge and mostly ales from the great endowed schools. The idea was that this exclusion would tend to suppress and abelish Noncon- formity. It was hoped that the sons of wealthy Dissent- ers would be driven from *racial and educational reseone, into the Established Church. After referring to the early days of</p>
        <p>BARD SCHOOLS</p>
        <p>the Lecturer said the latest figur-es showed that the number of children attending school was 4,7,923. Of th,a-, the largest number were in the 'bard Schools, 2,137,805. The second place was taken by the Church of England voluntary schools, 1,895,559. The Roman Catholics came third with 249,293. The 't British " voluntary schools came fourth with 229.916, and the Wesleyan voluntary school last with 125,355 In towns the Board school education was of the beet and highest kind, but in village. and rural districts this was not always the case and from various causes. One was that the tendency of rural and village life was to study economy at the cost of efficiency. Another was that the clergy and suppor- ters of the Establishment were able in those districts to exert greater inflame than was possible to them in towns and cities. A good deal of eontroversy bad been excited of late reeneetrag the eomparative educational merits if Board sch Nels and voluntary school.. As a matter of fact there was no real basis for any centention on the point, because the Government Inspectors had abundantly testified to the far superior efficacy of the Board schools. But they ought to be superior. They had these advantages because the School Beard rate enabled them, whereas the bulk ef the voluntary school.; were (-Ally poorly endowed with either premises, appara- tus or staff. The Education Bill proporeito do away with these superior organizations.and to give increased power to three witioh had been adjudged inferior. As Noncon- formists they objected to the effeet this Bill would have in globss larger opportunity for the importing of dog- matic Anglican teaching in public schools et the cost of the ratepayers to multitudes of whom that teaching was as offensive as the teaching of Rome itself. The Non- conformist cootentien was that</p>
        <p>DOGMATIC SECTARIAN TEACHING shoull not be possible in rate-tiled schools; that the children of Nonconformists should not be compelled to listen to it ; and further that denorninstional or even religious belief should not be a test for the teaching pro- frs=ion, whether in regard to Training Colleges sup- I Ported by the State or rats-aided elementary schools. In conclusion, the Lecturer gave statistics showing that the number of communicant's connected with the Free Churches exceeded those of the Church of England. It might be notfidently said that the Free Churches were nearer the original model, that their message was more true to the original gospel and that their whole history and character were mqre identified with religious free- dom:and equality than in the church established by lave. The services of the Lecturer and lanternist were acknowledged and the meeting terminated.</p>
        <p>COMMBRCIAL FAILIIREL-4COOrdin7 to " KAmp'S Mer- eantiln Gazette." the number of New Bankruptcy Fr - ened ngs is England and Wales gazatted during the week ending 11-vernber 22nd, was 81. The, number in the eorreapondinff week of last year was 107. showina a decrease of 28, being a net decrease in 1902, to date, f 29.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>EXAMINER FRIDAY E NOVEMBER 28, 1902 NOTES ON UAMES AND 1" MUNICIPAL TRAMH." ATHLETICS, Warrington's Extravagance.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER FRIDAY E NOVEMBER 28, 1902 NOTES ON UAMES AND 1" MUNICIPAL TRAMH." ATHLETICS, Warrington's Extravagance.</p>
        <p>The correspondent's figures with reference to the rates and indebtedness of the Borough are also in- accurate. He speaks of the rate as being less than is in the .£ in 1884. It is true that in that year the rate was 4s 10d but the correspondent altogether overlooks the fact that the redaction was due to the poor rate being Is 63 in the .£ as against 2s 101 In the previous year and 3s at the present time. As la matter of fact the rates have not increased in the ratio which many would have us believe. For the first twenty years after the incorporation of the ' Borough the averare rates were 5i 10d in the .E. 'For the next ten years the average wa, 51 8/ 1. in the .£ and in the next decade the average was 5s 9d in the Z. The average for the past eis years works out at 64 21 in the .£ The Corporation must not be held entirely responsible for this in- creased rate. The poor rate has increased of recent years and over this experditure the Cor- poration have: no control. The increase in the @minty calls for the maintenance of lunatics andithe large expenditure on better accommodation for the sick poor at the Woskhouse have made ,the call of the Guardians of the poor much head&amp; than it used to be. And now let us turn to the indebtedness of the Borough. The statement that the town owes just over £1,000,000 is practically correet.bat the writer altogether overlooks the fact that the property, essets and outlay of the Corporation are valued at £1,350,000. Year by year the Corporation are re- paying loans which have been incurred on various undertakings Most of the loans which have been taken up in recent years have been on remunerative undertakings alai altnough the liabilities have in- creased there is a corresponding amount to add to assets. For instance since the issue et the last aggregate balance sheet of the Corporation, the Cor- poratioulhave borrowed £14,000 for waterworks purposes, .£23,000 for tramways and .£10,009 for the electricity undertaking but all these are renumera- tive concerns and to speak of the money borrowed In establishing or extending these undertakings as debt is absurd. A business concern always regards it as capital, and instead of repaying the money within a period of years as the Corporation is bound to do out of the profits, the private cbncern disburses the profits in dividendli and allows the capital to remain owing. The Corporation have had bitter experience of the unvviedom of allowing private companies to undertake work which pro- perly belongs to the Corporation. They were made to pay through the nose for two terrible legacies from private companies in the gas and water under- takings. If these works had been undertaken by the Corporation from the ,outset the ratepayers would have been saved scores of thousands of pounds. Profiting by the experience of the past, the Corporation hate shown foresight and enterprise in inaugurating the electric light and tramway undertakings, and the present generation will hand down to posterity a legacy which instead of being a burrlen will be a source of profit. In closing this article, it may be advisable to refer to some criticisms of Corporation gas undertakings which. have appeared in the " Liverpool Courier." The writer shows by an array of figures that the charges made tor gas by Corporations exceed the charges of private companies. The writer, however, entirely overlooks the fact that whilst Corporations are gradually reducing their indebtedness by the repayment of loans, private companies are doing nothing of the kind. The Warrington Corporation gas undertak- ing may be taken as a case in poiet. Since the undertaking was a,guired, the Corporation have paid off something like ..£55,000 of the capital, and in c-iurse of time, the indebtedness will be extin- guished. What private company has done that? Then again the Corporation gas undertaking has con- tributed .£11,658 19541 in direct relief ot the rates, and furthermore the price of lighting the streets has been reduced by £lOOO a year, • and a reserve fund of .£lO,OOO has been accumulated. Nor is this all. When the Corporation , acquired the under- taking, the price of gas was 3/4 61 net, to-day it is 2s 7cl net to all consumerd in the Borough. Despite many difficulties, the Corporation have at least ustifial their managem int "ii the cos wide rtaking</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>Rugby Footba:l. [By "Ovalist"]</head>
        <p>Rugby Footba:l. [By "Ovalist"]</p>
        <p>The club'which was next to the bottom of the Northern League succeeded in defeating Warrington OR Saturday, a result in contradistinction to the failure of Halifax, the leaders, to get the better of the "primrose and blues." As it is some Weeks since Warrington met Halifax, this would seem to point to the fact that Warrington are deteriorating. This, I believe, is the case. The team just now is showing nothing like the dash and confidence of the earlier matches. As I pre- dicted some .time ago,the forwards have tired,the strain of keeping up an unequal struggle week after week having proved too much for them.. There are some who say that they could play better and it may be that they could, but we have expected too much from so light a pack, unrelieved as it is by any capable reserves The weakness in the front division is the sole cause of the team's recent failures. Neither half-backs nor three- quarters can be expected to shine on the attack when they are for the most part busily employed in keeping their opponents out. I shall have to talk about for- wards ad nauseant until a few either drop from the skies I or are secured by the enterprise of the Committee.</p>
        <p>On Saturaay,l suppose,the Rovers' forwards literally walked through the Warrington men at times and the marvel is not that Warrington lost, but that the score against them was not doubled. Most of my readers will recollect that friendly visit of the Rovers to Wilderspool a few years ago when they were expected to prove easy victims and instead,ran up a score of about 21 points with an ease which made Warrington look ridiculous. lam told that they played in much the same style on Saturday only Warrington defended better. There was no beating them, however, chances of scoring for Warrington being like the proverbial angels' visits—few and far between. This was un- doubtedly the best game the Rovers have played this season and Warrington are to be sympathised with, in meeting them when on the top of their form. They were good both fore and aft, but more energy and avoirdupois in the Warrington pack would have given the chroniclers a different tale to telL</p>
        <p>Both Warrington's visits to Hull have thus ended disastrously this season, and each of the third port teams has run up sight points against us but as we got 5 points against the premier club, the Rovers have come best out of the deal. They certainly beat War- rington fairly and squarely, whereas Hull were lucky to scrape home in the last five minutes.</p>
        <p>Most of the Warrington men shone on the defence last Saturday, Knight playing finely after a somewhat uncertain start. Brooks, the new centre, did by no means badly, his principal weakness being in the taking and giving of passes. In fielding, kicking, and tackling he held his own. Association with better corn- pan y may bring him out. This is just what several of the juniors in the "A "team require. At present they are handicapped by want of opportunity,for the " A" team was never so short of skilful and experienced players.</p>
        <p>There were some very curious results last Saturday. The leaders came a severe "cropper " at Weaste where Salfard rubbed in the first defeat by 12 points to nothing. Strong, determined forward play was the secret of the Reds' success. With the heaviest and cleverest pack in the League, however, they ought to make an impression now and then. Last season's champions also sustained a shock, in the shape of another pointless draw, their brother Rangers of Brig- house sharing the points. The Broughton men failed to win here last season, but on Saturday it was entirely the fault of the wing men who failed to take the ball when openings had been made These slips will happen sometimes, but two pointless draws in succession will not be palatable for the Broughtonians.</p>
        <p>Runcorn's resort to Birkenhead for health, pleasure and points has not panned out as expected so far. Hull paid a visit to the new nest of the Linnets and ruth- lessly despoiled it, there being joy in the East Riding and lamentation in Cheshire. Runcorn seem to have fallen on evil times aid I am inclined to think that the secret is once more to be found in the pack, where, like Warrington, Runcorn are much lighter and weaker than of yore Widnes did very well to give the Swin- ton lion such a fright in his own den. Only one point separated the'teams at the close and a bit of luck for the Chemicals would have turned the scale. There was a great levelling-up--or down—on Saturday and it is no worse for the game.</p>
        <p>The League Table up-toolate is as follows : _ For. _ Apt.</p>
        <p>P. W. L. D. G. T. P.rts Halifax l2 10 1 1 13 17 77 8 5 31 21 Broughton R 12w1aton Leigh l2 7 3 2 17 15 79 713 53 16 [Salford Itenoorn Oldham Bra iford ll 6 3 2 14 17 79 /0 11 53 14 Warrington l2 5 4 3 911 61 11 11 63 13 Hull Wigan Batley St Helms linddersleist ...... 11 3 7 1 5 7 31 11 11 55 7 Huaslet Hall IC Rovers l2 3. 9 0 410 38 17 21 97 6 Brighouse Rgs Widnes</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>• Warrington P.S.A. Society. Mr. Taker presided at the meeting of this society in the Co-operative Hall, Sankey-street, on Sunday. Mr Ford gave two clarionet solos, for which he was loudly applauded. Mr B Farrar gave an ieteresting address on "The work of our hands." 11113 DOOTOS BREMY.—A pair of ALF TAYLOR'S - . . . .</p>
        <p>Wonderful Boots.—Ailvt. Historic Society of Lancashire and Cheshire. At the eighth ordinary meeting of this society Mr J Paul Rylands oreqided over a good attend- ance. Mr Thomas May passed round for inspection several objects of Celtic work. Tao) paper of the evening, on " Extracts Locally InterestinT from the Patent and Pipe Rolls from 1272 to 1345," by Mr T H Lumhy, B.A. was then read, commencing with an account of Edward I. and the wars during this unsettled period, with the names of many Lanca- shire and Cheshire men who took a pr )minent part in the events of the time. Mr W E Gregson pro- posed, and Mr .1 G Prentice seconded, a hearty vote of thanks to Mr Lumby for his paper.</p>
        <p>KREP YOUR FRU WARM.—Wesr ALI, TAYLOR'S Good Boots.-45, Horsemarket-st., Warrington.—Adyt•</p>
        <p>Ihe Green Dragon Licence. On Monday, an application will again be made to the magistrates for the removal of the licence of the Green Dragon Ina, Bridge-street, to a re4idence known as " Brooklands " in Lovely-lane,which it is proposed to refit as an hotel. A meeting of property owners and residents in Lovely-lane district was held in the schoolroom, Thewlis-street, on nuts- da3 evening, to consider what action should be taken with regard to the application. Mr Haley was voted to the chair. A deputation attended from the Temperance Federation and informed the meeting that the Federation was prepared to give financial and moral support to the pr.lperty owners and residents in opposing the application. After some discussion it was decided t 3 offer the strongest opposition possible to the application and a com- mittee was appointed to take what action was deemed necessary.</p>
        <p>LADIES BEST GLACE BOOTS AWD SHOES. UNDER THE CLOCK, BRIDGE-STREST.—Advt. ASK for CUNNINGHAII'S HIGH-CLASS MINER WATERS.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Warrington to Northwich Light Railway. At the meeting of the North wich Urban Council on Tuesday evening, the clerk read a letter from , the Light Railway Commissioners forwarding a copy of their order approving the Warrington to Northwich light railway schema, and intimating that they had sent it on to the Board of Trade for confirmation. They had fully considered the sug- gestiens of the various councils, but in regard to certain amendments emanating from the North- wich Council the cornmi•sioners had not found themselves able to depart from the policy and the precedents of previous orders as many of the pro- posals involved. They, however, thought that the order safeguarded the interests of the Council. The Clerk said that this statement was a matter of opinion, and if the srder was approved as it stood there were several particulars in which Northwich would suffer, and which rendered of no effect agree- ments which were made between them and the promoters of the scheme. For instance, all pro- vision for the Council's expenses had been struck out ; there was no provision as to compulsory sale of the railway to the Council; a contribution which was promised towards the Navigation-road improve- ment was omitted; it was not made clear that the Council would not be responsible for any damage to the railway or the promoters' property by reason of the sinking of roadway., dec. A special com- mittee was instructed to confer with the Clerk aqd lay a protest before the Beard of Trade.</p>
        <p>DUES CURBlll•—Oxien Pile Cure. Removes like magic I all forms of bleeding. blind and !telling piles. Safe. speedy and guaranteed. Of all Chemists, or (rem TIM GIANT Om Co., 27, Shoe-lane, London, ICC. (1),</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>Statement in "The Times."</head>
        <p>The Statement in "The Times."</p>
        <p>[SPECIAL.]</p>
        <p>A correspondent writing to the great London daily, "The Times," calls attention under the head- ing of" Municipal Socialism "to what he describes as the " extravagance" of the Warrington Corpora— tion. It is not our intention here to discuis the motive of "The Times" in belittling what it is pleased to call municipal socialism, but we give the statement as it appeared in "The Times" accom- panied by a few facts which throw an altogether different light on the expenditure of the Corpora- tion than that intended by the writer of ttle article. The statement in " The Times "reads as follows "&amp; Correspondent writes s—During the last few years the Corporation of Warrington have been extending their possessions at an alarming rate. Already the possessors of waterworks, gasworks, sanitary works) at which they carry on the trade of patent manure manufacturers), and other similar public establishments, they have launched out suddenly and have within the last floe or six years pot down an elaborate electric light plant, and instituted a system of electric tramways to all the outskirts of the town at a cost of over £100,030. It is estimated that the work of re laying and levelling the streets that the latter work has interfered with will cost the town £15,000. An order has been obtained granting power to construct two light railways outside the borough. The town has erected a new police station at a cost of 133,000, which has accommodation for a town ten times its sit and is variously known as the • Palace of Jastioe ' and the ' Palace of Beauty.' Only last week the Bari of Derby opened a Municipal Technical School which has cost to date £20,003, and is expected to cost much more under a completed scheme. It is contemplate Ito plant secondary schools at different parts of the town. The waterworks are being extended at a cost of several hundreds of thousands of , pounds. . . The rates now stand at 6s 6d in the pound, against' less than to in 1884, when the rateable value was nearly £lOO,OOO lees. The town owes just over £l,OOO 030, and Its popu- lation is only just above 60,000. • . The worst feature of this extravagance is that a great portion of the material needed for the several works that have been carried out at so groat a speed has been supplied by members of limited liability companies, having directors or large shareholders on the Council. or by contractors who have obtained their mateelal from those companies. . . The Council, in spite of publics opinion and the more influential section of the Press, have maintained their policy, and have been assisted to do so by a political compact under which each side undertook not to contest a seat held by the - other side, All attempts to get an inquiry into the matter have been voted down, and the outcome of continued agitation was the formation of a Non-Menlcipal Trading Association, whieh felt itself strong enough to challenge the political COM. pact of the two parties this November. Three of the retiring cJuncillors were allowed a walk-over, but in the other six wards the Non--Tcaders forced a contest, the 'trading ' question being the only question at issue. Seven Liberals and Conservatives (two of the former and Ave of the latter) were opposed by seven Non-Traders (three Liberals and four Conservatives). In only one Ward dia a Liberal and a Conservative, as such, get nominated, ana here a Non-Trader also intervened. . . When the result of the polls Was declared, it was found that the Non-Traders had made a clean sweep in four out of the six Wards."</p>
        <p>To deal with the statements respecting the elec- tricity and tramways departments first. The writer remarks that the Corporation have launched out " suddenly" into these undertakings. To any- one acquainted with the real facts the exact oppo- site is the case. It took years of prodding and steady agitation before the Corporation mild be induced to embark upon an electric light under- taking. The Corporation owned the Gas Works, ' and the opinion prevailed that the introduction of electric light would seriously affect the income of the gas works. Tbe Corporation were, however, at length induced to introduce electric lighting, ' and the short experience already obtained has dissipated any fears respecting the gas works. Despite the competition of the elec- tic light, the consumption of gas has steadily increased, and the receipts are larger than they have ever been. With respect to the introduction of tramways, Warrington had long been a byeword for its lack of enterprise in provid- ing means of communication. The Corporation did not meet the want and it Witri only when they were threatened with the introduction of an elec- tric tramway system by a limited liability company that it was decided by the Corporation to institute a tramway service of their own. So much for the " suddenness "of these public services in Warring- ton. Now for the financial position. The wrlter of the article speaks of an " elaborate" electric light plant,evidently with the intention of convey- ing the impression that Warrington has made the same mistake as Salford and other places in pro vie- ing plant in excess of what was required Nothing of the kind 'has occurred. The demaud for electric light _and power in Warrington has exceeded the anticipations of the committee and additions have had to be made to the plant since it was put down. The progress of the concern has been uninterrupted right from the commencement, and during the past half year the undertaking has made a profit which will not only meet the repay- ment of the loan but will also show a balance which can be devoted to the relief of the rates. The Cor- poration have been granted 25 years in which to repay the loan on the undertaking and at the end of that 'prim the ratepayers will be in possession of a profitable unde-takiug unencumbered with debt.</p>
        <p>The statement respecting tramways is even wider of the mark than that concerning electric light. The writer speaks of the institution of a " system of electric tramways to all the outskirts of the town at a cost of over £lOO,O 10." It is hardly necessary to inform our readers that of the five tramway routes which have been constructed not one goes beyond the Borough boundary. The cost instead of being £lOO,OOO has been .£68,000 What is still more important to the ratepayers is the fact that the working of the system for the half year ending September last shows a clear profit of £461 after providing for working expanses, interest, sinking fund and all otter est-..bli,hment expenses. With Bach a satisfactory mink from municipal enterprise, the equanimity of the ratepayers is not likely to be disturbed by the round figures of "The Times '' correspondent. For the correspondent's remarks with reference to the new constabulary buildings, there is perhaps 80010 justice. The new buildings are certainly very much farm than the old police station,which was too small, but it must not be forgotten that the Corporation were compelled by the Home Office to provide accommodation at the new premises for a number of resident officers and constables. This largely accounts for the siza of the new buildings. Of the Corporation's expenditure on technical education, little need be said in justification. It is refreshing to find the Corporation 80 alive to the importance of this work that it is prepared to keep abreast with the leading municipalities of Lanca- shire in providing accommodation for students, The Corporation is to be congratulated rather than condemned for its action in this matter. The statement of the writer that "it is contemplated to plant secondary schools at different parts of the town" is utter nonsense and has no foundation in fact.</p>
        <p>Aeother mis-statement is that concerning the watei works. "The waterworks" says the corres- pondent, "are being extended at a cost of several hundreds of thousands of pounds." By the War- rington Corporation Act of 1899, the Corporation was empowered to spend a sum of £147,000 on extensions of the waterworks. These extensions included the construction of a new reservoir at Winwick, new machinery at Winwick for pumping the water to a new reservoir which has been con- structed at Hill Warren, the sinking of new shafts as Delph-lane and pumps, and new storage tanks and filter beds at Appleton. These extensions were undertaken on the advice of such reliable experts as Mr Mansergh, Ir Hawsley and Mr Hill. The experience during the past summer has more than justified the necessity of these extensions, and proved beyond doubt that they were not undertaken a single day too soon If the Corporation bad not proceeded with the extensions immediately at ter obtaining the sanc- tion of Parliament, there would have been a water famine • within the last• six months. The cost has been well within the esti- mates, and there is every reason to believe that the whole of the extensions will be completed as less cost than the estimated sum of £147,000, which in the the eyes of "The Times " correspondent appears to equal "several hundreds of thousands of pounds." Since the water undertaking was pur- chased by the Corporation, the ratepayers have not been called upon to contribute a penny towards the cost. On the contrary, the undertaking has con- tributed two sums of £5OO towards the relief of the rates. The contribution to the rates would have been much larger were it not for the fact that the proEts, after meeting the repayment of loans, have been mortgaged for the purpose of raisingnioney for the extensions. The profits last year, for instance, were something like £l7,ooo,but this sum has been absorbed by the extensions at Winwick and Appleton</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>Daresbury Petty Sessions.</head>
        <p>Daresbury Petty Sessions.</p>
        <p>FRlDLY—Before Mr W Long (In the chair.), Colonel Ridgway</p>
        <p>Mr if Niel&amp; Mr J M kidelaton and Mr. itogar Parr</p>
        <p>TRESPAMING FS SEARCH OF GAME. —lsaac -introbus, Wm Antrobus, and Matthew Statham, of Lower Walton, were charged with trespassing on land belonging to Sir Gilbert Greenall, at Moore. on November 19th, in pursuit of gsrne. They pleaded guilty.—Mr Draper. solicitor, of War- rington. stated that they received Int °smatters that a number of men employed on the railway embankment at Moore had made it their custom in the dinner hoar to go Into the wood adjoining in pursuit of game. On November 19th instruc- tions were given to the keeper, John Hrlam, to go into the wood and see whet was being done. He concealed himself and between twelve and one o'cloek he saw tha defendants I going through the wood. They were about ten yards apart, three of these had their hands full of stones,lind the other had a catapult. They kicked at all the covers they came to. When Statham saw the keeper he gave an alarm and Wm Antrobus, who had the catapult. ran away. The other stayed, and the keeper got their names and addresses, He knew the man who ran away. There was plenty of game In that wood, and it wes preserved by Sir Gilbert Greene!' for shooting purposes. Bach Idefendant was fined Es 6d and sosts.—William Massey and John Lightfoot, of Bastford- road, Walton Inferior, pleaded guilty to a similar offence. rhev els° admitted the charge of killing and taking game P.C. Ellis deposed to meeting the defendants in Chester- road, Walton. at 2.30 on Sunday morning, the 26th ult. Another man ran away, but witness caught the defendant,. The bludgeons produced were in their possession, as was the net, and witness found a rabbit under Massey's w sistcoet. They said they used the sticks for walking with.—Mas say said he was out of work, and he went out with the obj set of catching a rabbit.--This was defendants' first appearance. and they were fined Ds and costs each. Ths net was forfeited.</p>
        <p>MINOR Caeße.—A Warrington carter, named William Collier. was ordered to Day the costs for not carrying a tail light on his vehicle.— Mr Draper, who appeared for defend- ant, said Collier was ignorant of the law.—For k wing been drunk and disorderly Frederick Smith, of Lat3hford With- out, was fined Sc. —Joseph Webb labourer. of Warrington, and William Bellfield, boatman, of ftuncarn, were each fined in the colts (3s 6(1) for having dogs at large —Patrick Murphy, of Appleton. had to pay 2s 64 for driving without reins, and for neglecting to send his son Herbert regularly to school a man named James Smith was mulcted itia penalty of 53.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL</p>
        <p>WINWICK ALTHONS V ORFORD-LANE.</p>
        <p>Placed on the Recreation Ground,Orford-lane. The Alblone kicked off againsc a strong breeze, had all the play throughout the game and won ewily by 4 goals to 1. The scorers were J Spnacer t F Wright,l Holcroft d Houghton for the Albions and T Hudson for Orford-lane.</p>
        <p>NEWTON-LE-WILLOWS V W/TroN ALEION. At Newton. Newton started before a fair gate. The visitors at once took the ball to the Newton god, but could make no Ilea iway. About fifteen minutes after the start. Ellis, from a corner oper.ed the sooting for Newton. Shortly after the restart gills had another try for a goal jnit heading over the crossbar. From a corner by Vs./ovine Broster scored a second goal. The visitors w re in,ssing when the interval arrived. Scare at half-time—Newton 2, Witten nil. Final, Newton 3, Witton 3. WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT LEAGUE. 2ND Divie/oN. WARRINGTON CENTRAL v WARRINGTON ALEIGNS.</p>
        <p>This match was played at Longford last Saturday and after a fast and exciting' game the Albion came out on tap winning by 2 goaIS to 1 goal.</p>
        <p>EIRLESITIWN V BACUP. (LANcisar HE LEAGUE).</p>
        <p>Played at ilarlestown on Saturday afternoon, in miserable weather. lineup won the toss, and Bartley started before a mere handful of spectators. Earlestown played Parker, late of Stockport County. The bait was immediately taken int, the visitors' half. and in the first infante Bartley easily de- feated tlitchell from short range. This livened the visitors up, and a centre from Brieriey was cleared by Robin, Pounder placing the return high' over the bar. Offside against Hartley spoiled a Move on behalf of the homesters. A perfect centre from Leach again pot the visitors on the defensive, and three corners in cloi-tr soce.asion fall to the hornestere, from the last of which Bartley sawed 'heir second goal. Immediately afterwards Bartley was Injured, and had to leave the fisld. Parke,. scored the third goal twenty-five minwes after the staet. Bartley having in the meantime taken his place on the field. Faulty kicking on the cart of Leigh let the visitors In, but Gungq spoiled a splendid opportunity by shooting yards too high Lowe scored for Basun. Interval :—Sarlestown 4, Basun 1. The second half of the game was contested principally In the Thou° quarters, who owing to an injury to one of their players had to play with 10 men. The final result stood liarlestOwn 8 goals, Bacup 1.</p>
        <p>Rechabites and the Green Dragon Licence.</p>
        <p>The monthly meettng of Chardy tent No 11 branch of the Independent Order of Rechabitias was held in Friars Green ,choolroorn on Tuesday. After the minutes of the previous meeting had beets confirmed and the district representative repor adopted the question of the transfer of the licence of the Green Oregon Came up f3r the consideration of the meeting. The following rc•ro'ution was sal,- Initted and carried unanimously, "That this meeting of No II branch of the Independent Order of Recha- bites consisting of 160 members believing as they do that the drink traffic is a fruitfal slurce of crime wretchedness and paverty in our midst, strongly and emphatically protest against the proposed transfer of the Green Dragon licence in Bridge- street to the Brooklands in Lovely-lane, that such a policy would be detrimental to thelbest interests of the town. Also they condemn the attitude of the two paid officials of the town appearing in court to support the application in the interest of a private brewing concern, without the sanction of the Town Council."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>CAPTAIN CROSFIELD'S RETURN FROM SOUI H AFRICA. Welcome at Bank Quay Schoolroom.</head>
        <p>CAPTAIN CROSFIELD'S RETURN FROM SOUI H AFRICA. Welcome at Bank Quay Schoolroom.</p>
        <p>Mr. Henry Roberts, J.P. on Municipal matters.</p>
        <p>There was an interesting gathering in The wlis-street schoolroom on .Saturday evening when a hearty " welcome home " was extended to Captain George Croatiaid by the members of the Bank Quay Mutual Im- provement Society, of which gaiety Captain Crosliold is president. About one hundred Persons were (exeunt and they partook of tea. Subsequently a meeting was held at which Mr Elwell xresided. He was accompani.d on the platform by Captain Crosfield,ftir Henry Roberts, J.P., and Mr Nathan Hough. The CHAIRMAN,• in the course of a few introductory remarks, said the memberi of the Mutual Improvement Society were very much disappointed when they found that their president had volunteered for active service in South Affirm and was about to leave them. Although disappointed they deaired that Captain Crosfield should serve his country with honour and be spared to come back again. To.day that desire was accomplished. They could not fail to recognise the great servioes which the Volunteer force had rendered during the recent war. Prior to the South African war tho Volunteer force was not looked upon as an effective military organisation aud it was sometimes stated that Volunteers only played as soldiers,but he was glad to say that during the recent war the Volunteer force came out second to none. (Hear, hear.) He thought great credit was due to the men who gave up the pleasures and comforts of home life to risk their lives in the service of the 4 country abroad. (Heir, hear). It was the duty of the Government to encourage the Volunteer force and do all in their power to make it effielent, whilst on the other hand it was the duty of the employer of labour to give the fa:Male necessary in order that the volunteer should be properly trained. (Applause). _ _ .</p>
        <p>Mr NATHAN FIOUGH said they were all delighted to see IMr George Crosfield amongst them once more. (Hear, hear). Personally he (Mr Haugh) was not a man woo I advocated war. He never forgot the bitter experiences of our soldiers in the Crimean war. There were many who like Mr Crosfield left their homes for South Africa and who had been buried there. Mr Crosfield bad a noble heart and belonged to a noble family. (Applause). He deserved the heartiest welcome theyeoula give MM. (Hear, hear). Mr HENRY ROBERTS. J.P., who, on rising, was received with continued applause, said when a man got a wel- come like that it put heart into him. (Applause.) There were a good many things which were not quite as pleas- ant as tuat. (Laughter.) As they knew, he had been going through the mill a good deal lttely,and he thought ha had taken his gruelling like a man ought to take it. (Hear, hear.) He hoped that some day, perhaps sooner than many imagined, the present condition of things might alter, and what they all wished to see, whichever side of the controversy they rook, was that one thing— and one thing only—the truth should come out on top. (Applause.) Perhaps they would forgive him if he said is passiog that he had not been driven out of public life. (Hear, • hear.) He hoped still perhaps in other channels to serve his town as faithfully and as loyally, and he hoped as satisfactorily as he had tried to do in the years past. (Applause.) He was not going to say that he had shut himself out altogether of municipal life. (Hear, hear.) He was going to wait in quietness and in coefideace for a short time to see if those,who on the wave of what he b-lieved to be tren- I Went popularity bad succeeded in finding a way into the ITown Counsel, a position which upon their own in- dividual merite they would never have found, would ' vindicate,or would try to vindicate the statements which they had made in public and which yet remained to be proved—(ll Voice : " They oannon—lf they did not, they could not plead that they had not had every opportunity to do so. (Hear, hear.) They were in the citadel itself and had access to all the books and papers in the possession of the Corporation. Therefore they had no excuse for withholding from the people who sent them to the !Council, the truth about the statements which lied been made.(Elear, hear.) If they with, ld that proof then he should wait to see if there was still left in the minds of the con- scientious people of Warrington a determination that they shall be proved. (Applause.) Perhaps as time went on he would have opportunities of saying more. They were not there to discuss municipal matters at all. That was a Meeting he understood for the purpose of offerine the president of the Mutual Improvement Society in connection with that school, a hearty welcome home. (Hear, hear.) That was a ques- , tioo upon which there woiald be no disagree- ment. He knew the Bank Quay People so well that he had no hesitation in saying this, that if there was one feeling stronger than another in the hearts of most of them it was a feeling of regard and affection and respeot for everybody who bore the name ot Cros- field. (Hear, hear.) Referring to the recent war, Mr Roberts said whatever their opinions were as to the cause of it, there was no difference CT opinion as to the manly patriotism which prompted their Volunteers— men upon whom there was no legal call what- ever—to volunteer for active service in what they believed to be the defence of the empire, and of such was their friend the President. (Applause), Many men weat out and came back again, havicg done-thee duty well. There were not many however, who went back a sec and time as Captain Crosfield did. (Applause) Now he had ceme back and he (Mr Reberts) hoped Captain Crosfield had come to stay—(hear, hear)—and he believed he had. (Applame.) Re was sure of this that as this big industry at the Bank Quay works grew—and he did not think they had any idea of what its growth would be in the next ten years —Captain Crosfield would form friendships and associa- tions amongst the workingmen of that place and outside it which would be to him, as they had been to him (adr Roberts), the pleasantest reminiscences of his lifetime. (Hear, hear.) With regard to the society under whose auspices they were met,Mr Roberts said he bad been con- nected with mutual improvement so:lianas more or less all his life. The last he was connected with had been described as a mutual admiration society—laughter)— but he was no longer a member of it. (Laughter.) His earliest recollections were in connection with a mutual improvemeht satiety connected with Wycliffe Sunday Solsool. When he 'first joined he would not be more than 14 or 15 years of age. He was always a talker— (laughter)—and he was afraid that reputation had stuck to him. (Laughter.) the members of the society were always encouraged by the minister, .the Rev G Heaney, to practise public speaking. P •rhaps it might be a fault of this age that in acme respects there was , too much speakine. There was not, however, too much good epeakieig—(hear, hear)—and not too much speaking on good subjuts either. He was certain that a well-cooducted mutual improvement society was bound to have a permanent influence for good upon any young fellow who joined it and made a proper use of .t. Ho knew of men in Warrington who would never be holding the positions they did to-day if it had not been for the training they got in public speaking at mutual improvement eocietiee. When he saw young fellows preaching the gospel of politics and temperance that their fathers preached before them it warmed his blood and made it course a little quicker He would advise the members ef that society not to be Afraid of their enthustaam,and let them not be depressed if they made a failure or two. If they did not make a failure they would not make • success. If they were in earnest is what they were doing they would read up the subject and furnish their minds with opinion. In conclusion, Mr Roberts thanked the audience for the heartiness w-th which they had received him, and sail he joined with them in extending a welcome home to Captain Crosfieid, whom he hoped might become more and more intimately apsociated with the good work (harried on at Bank Quay, which was started many years ago under his father's kind auspices and destined, he believed, Co achieve even greater suc- 05S1 in the Mere. (Applause.) Captain GEORGB CEIO9FIELD. Who as rifling to reply, was greeted with the singing øf " For he's a jolly good fellow," said it was difficult fer him on that occasion to express his feelings for the welcome they had given him, which was wholly undeserved on his part. Us appreciated their kindness in inviting him tiro and the far too eulogistic things which had been said about him. His brother Arthur was very merry he could not be present. He hai been suddenly called clown to London and was therefore unable to be with them. Perhaps some of those present had been wondering what he (Captain Crosfield) had been doing during his absence in South Africa. They were deubtless sick of the war, but probably et-hey would be interested to hear of one iceident that happened to him out tramp. He was going to tell them how he did not catch De Wet. (Laughter.) His company had been teekkine in the middle of Orange River Colony and bad travelled about 20 miles one day. They were all rather tired and were settliag down for the night when an officer came to the General in charge of the column' with the news that De Wet was going to camp at a drift on the river Ititaaoster that night about 30 miles from where tlae column was situ- ated and the officer asked the general to give him all the mounted men he had under his command. His (Captain Crosfield's) cempaay—the 77th Imperial Yeomanry— was the otly mounted company in the column and they immediately made preparations for a stirt. It was ten or eleven o'clock before all the arrangements were com- plete and they had to cover the distance in about four hours. They took with them a porn-porn, which they eloubtlsse knew was a qisiok-firing gun, firing a one posed shill. They ha i a most intore.tiag trek right across the veldt guided by a most capable Kafir who displayed a wonderful knowlndenc of the country. It was very dark and they went at a ',tee of about six miles an hour. They trotted for ten minutes, then dismounted and walked fee five minutes and then got on horseback again and so on until they came in eight of the Rhenoster river. Stouts were sent out but came back with the information that the place was empty and that De Wet had left the af ternotm previous. (Laughter. As they had covered fifty miles and their horses were too tired to travel further they could</p>
        <p>not proceed after the enemy. They had some compensation for their dies appointment in finding an excellent farm where they were treated with the greatest hospitality by the wife of a prominent Boer. He bad several Incidents of a like nature during his first period of service in South Africa, but on his Remind visit the war was praetically ever when he got there. Be had a great deal of leisure time and he took the opportunity of read- ing John Stuart "Political Economy," He did not mind making the admission that the book made a great impression on him and changed the view with which he looked at a great many things. He would like te give a lecture on that book tu the members of the Mutual improvement Society and be would offer prizes for the best essays ea the lecture. It dealt with, the production and distribution of wealth and as man spent meet of his time ia producing wealth and in distribution, it became in that way the study of man. It opened a vast field for inquiry and discussion and he would put the subject before the members , on any date they named. (Hear, Mae.) He was pleased to see Mr Roberts present and be was glad they had welcomed him in the way they had. Mr Roberts referred to his (Captain Creifieles) return to Warringtoo. Now that he bad got back it was his desire to settle down and live in Warrington and to take his fair share in the public work of the town. (Hear, hear, and Applause). He would endeavour to help onthe society which he tlioneht was a thoroughly useful and good society. Referring to Mr Roberts's rafereace to Bieck Quart Works, Captain Crostield said 'se knew it was the desire of ttie direc,:ors to do as well as they possibly could by the men they employed. (Hear, hear). Be did not wish anyone to think that the directors were actuated by any other motive than that of the welfare of them whom they employed. (Applause). During the evening an excellent musical pro- gramme was 'submitted. A quartette party of the Apollo Male Voice Prize Choir, consisting of Messrs Woodcock, Critchley, Cook and George, was deservedly encored for a successful interpretation of "On the banks of Allan Water." Al a later stage of the pro- ceedings the same quartette party sang " The long day closes." Mr T Bough WAR the aocompanist. Songs were also sung by Mr Critchley, Mr George, Mr Hassell and Mr Broadhurst, and they received hearty recap - tions. Light refreshments were handed round duria g the evening and an enjoyable evening's entertainment concluded with an acknowledgment of the services of all who had taken part in the proceedings on the proposi- tion of Mr F WILLIAMS, seconded by Mr N Com and supported by Mr T Dam</p>
        <p>The New Tramway Routes. Saturday witnessed the completion of the Cor- poration tramway service within the borough, the new routes to the Cemetery and Longford Bridge being inspected on behalf of the Board by Lieut. Colonel Von Donop. The inspection commenced at 10.20 when two cars left the shed in Mersey-street for Longford Bridge. Amongst those who accom- panied the Inspector were :—Major Cardew, Mr Scotter (representing the Warrington and North- wich Light Railway Committee), Aldermen Monks and Heaton, Councillors Smethurst, John Evans, Baird,Bennett,Rogers,Pierpoint,Ellison,and Duncan the Town Clerk(lr J Lyon Whictle),awl Mr Grime- dale (electrical engineer and tramways manager. The inspection of both routes passed off without a hitch. The Inspector suggested the rounding-off of the corner of the footpath near the vaults at the end of Scotland-road, in order to allow more apace between the rails and the footpath. This alteration was subsequently carried out. At 12.40 the Man- chester-road service was open to the public and during the day the cars were well patronized.</p>
